{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 79 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "c9c7f528585ab735ae168c36863b0e2b07edf8d54f9c36c8a82796928969dc80" :edb/db-before-t 78 :edb/db-after-t 79 :edb/tx-data [[13194139533391 50 #inst "2026-10-10T07:44:01.470Z" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186054654 1038 #edb/ref 17592186237781 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186054676 1038 #edb/ref 17592186237987 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186054709 1038 #edb/ref 17592186237790 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186054823 1038 #edb/ref 17592186237961 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186059633 1071 "Express $\\sin\\left(x + \\dfrac{\\pi}{6} \\right)$ in terms of $\\sin x$ and $\\cos x$." 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186059633 1071 "Express $\\sin\\left(x + \\dfrac{\\pi}{6} \\right)$ in terms of $\\sin x$ and $\\cos x.$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186059633 1074 "Recall the sum formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\sin(u+v) = \\sin{u}\\cos{v} + \\cos{u}\\sin{v}\n$$\n\nApplying the sum formula for sine with $u = x$ and\n\n$$\nv = \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin (x + \\frac{π}{6}) &= \\sin x\\cos (\\frac{π}{6}) + \\cos x\\sin (\\frac{π}{6}) \\\\\n&= \\sin x \\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) + \\cos x \\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\sin x}{2} + \\frac{\\cos x}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\sin x + \\cos x}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186059633 1074 "Recall the sum formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\sin(u+v) = \\sin{u}\\cos{v} + \\cos{u}\\sin{v}\n$$\n\nApplying the sum formula for sine with $u = x$ and $v = \\dfrac{\\pi}{6},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}+\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186059634 1071 "Express $\\cos(x - 60^\\circ)$ in terms of $\\sin x$ and $\\cos x$." 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186059634 1071 "Express $\\cos(x - 60^\\circ)$ in terms of $\\sin x$ and $\\cos x.$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186059634 1074 "First, we recall the difference formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\cos(u - v) = \\cos{u}\\cos{v} + \\sin{u}\\sin{v}\n$$\n\nApplying the difference formula for cosine with $u = x$ and\n\n$$\nv = 60^\\circ\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\cos (x - 60^{∘}) &= \\cos x\\cos 60^{∘} + \\sin x\\sin 60^{∘} \\\\\n&= \\cos x \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} + \\sin x \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\cos x}{2} + \\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\sin x}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\cos x + \\sqrt{3}\\sin x}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186059634 1074 "First, we recall the difference formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\cos(u - v) = \\cos{u}\\cos{v} + \\sin{u}\\sin{v}\n$$\n\nApplying the difference formula for cosine with $u = x$ and $v = 60^\\circ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-{60}^{∘}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡{60}^{∘}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡{60}^{∘} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186059682 1038 #edb/ref 17592186237979 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186059731 1038 #edb/ref 17592186237815 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186059736 1038 #edb/ref 17592186237832 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186059737 1038 #edb/ref 17592186237833 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186059743 1038 #edb/ref 17592186238022 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186059743 1038 #edb/ref 17592186238023 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186059743 1038 #edb/ref 17592186238024 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186059743 1038 #edb/ref 17592186238027 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186059743 1038 #edb/ref 17592186238028 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186059743 1038 #edb/ref 17592186238030 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186059752 1038 #edb/ref 17592186237936 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186059763 1038 #edb/ref 17592186237782 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186059777 1038 #edb/ref 17592186237903 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186059781 1038 #edb/ref 17592186237994 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186059869 1038 #edb/ref 17592186237880 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186059962 1068 #edb/ref 17592186056101 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186059962 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237659 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186059962 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186059962 1071 "Consider the augmented matrix $M$, given by\n$M = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 9 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 2 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$.\nWhat matrix results from carrying out the row operation $R_{2}:=R_{2} + (-1) \\cdot R_{3}$ on $M$?" 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186059962 1071 "Consider the augmented matrix $M,$ given by\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 9 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 2 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat matrix results from carrying out the row operation ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1)\\cdot {R}_{3}$ on $M?$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186059962 1074 "The operation ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1)\\cdot {R}_{3}$ means that we need to add $-1$ times the third row to the second row.\n\nApplying this row operation, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 9 & 1 \\\\ 5 & -1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 2 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1)\\cdot {R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 9 & 1 \\\\ 5+(-1)(5) & -1+(-1)(2) & 2+(-1)(-1) & 1+(-1)(1) \\\\ 5 & 2 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 9 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 2 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186059968 1068 #edb/ref 17592186056137 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186059968 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237474 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186059968 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186059968 1071 "Consider the matrix $A$, given by\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 8 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 4 & -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$.\nWhat matrix results from carrying out the row operation $R_{1}↔R_{2}$ on $A$?" 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186059968 1071 "Consider the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 8 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 4 & -1 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat matrix results from carrying out the row operation ${R}_{1}↔{R}_{2}$ on $A?$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186059968 1074 "The operation ${R}_{1}↔{R}_{2}$ means that we need to swap the first and second rows.\n\nApplying this row operation, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 8 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 4 & -1 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1}↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 8 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186059970 1068 #edb/ref 17592186056149 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186059970 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237541 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186059970 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186059970 1071 "Find $f^{′}(x)$ for $f(x) = x^{3} + \\frac{1}{x^{2}} - x$." 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186059970 1071 "Find ${f}^{′}(x)$ for  $f(x)={x}^{3}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}-x.$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186059970 1074 "The function $f(x)$ is the sum of three power functions. So, we apply the sum rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}-x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{-2})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x) \\\\  & =3{x}^{(3-1)}+(-2){x}^{(-2-1)}-1 \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}-2{x}^{-3}-1 \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186064168 1038 #edb/ref 17592186238011 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186064168 1038 #edb/ref 17592186238012 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186064168 1038 #edb/ref 17592186238014 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186064265 1038 #edb/ref 17592186237882 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186064268 1038 #edb/ref 17592186237949 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186064269 1038 #edb/ref 17592186237977 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186064269 1038 #edb/ref 17592186237978 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186064279 1038 #edb/ref 17592186238009 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186064279 1038 #edb/ref 17592186238010 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186064325 1038 #edb/ref 17592186238054 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186064325 1038 #edb/ref 17592186238055 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186064326 1038 #edb/ref 17592186238056 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186064326 1038 #edb/ref 17592186238057 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186064327 1038 #edb/ref 17592186238052 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186064327 1038 #edb/ref 17592186238053 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186064349 1038 #edb/ref 17592186237881 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186064388 1038 #edb/ref 17592186238059 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186068678 1074 "First, we recall the following formula:\n\n$$\nx^2 + {\\color{red}{2}}{\\color{blue}{a}}x + a^2 = (x + {\\color{blue}{a}})^2\n$$\n\nNotice that we have a perfect square in the parentheses that we can factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} + 14x + 49 &=  \\\\\nx^{2} + 14x + 7^{2} &=  \\\\\nx^{2} + 2 \\cdot 7x + 7^{2} &=  \\\\\n&= (x + 7)^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x^{2} + 14x + 49) - 8 &=  \\\\\n&= (x + 7)^{2} - 8 \\mid\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186068678 1074 "First, we recall the following formula:\n\n$$\nx^2 + {\\color{red}{2}}{\\color{blue}{a}}x + a^2 = (x + {\\color{blue}{a}})^2\n$$\n\nNotice that we have a perfect square in the parentheses that we can factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+14x+49 & = \\\\ {x}^{2}+14x+{7}^{2} & = \\\\ {x}^{2}+2\\cdot 7x+{7}^{2} & = \\\\ (x+7{)}^{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}^{2}+14x+49)-8 & = \\\\ (x+7{)}^{2}-8 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186068679 1071 "Given the graph below, solve the inequality $x^2- 3x-10 >0$.\n\n![](images/bb/bb4b28c1fbc4e166f22acf62ae4ab11cdab0ee07c2c4af080db0bf7e290744aa.png)" 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186068679 1071 "Given the graph below, solve the inequality $x^2- 3x-10 >0.$    \n\n![](images/bb/bb4b28c1fbc4e166f22acf62ae4ab11cdab0ee07c2c4af080db0bf7e290744aa.png)" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186068679 1074 "Notice that our inequality\n\n$$\nx^2 -3x -10> 0\n$$\n\nis a \"*greater than*\" inequality. Therefore, we need to find the values of $x$ for which the parabola\n\n$$\ny =x^2 -3x -10\n$$\n\nis *above* the $x$-axis. We mark these values of $x$ in blue in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/ca/ca22e171b36e21802769e3612a163d29b01209af1ef75887a65ab6013bb1401a.png)\n\nSo, the solution to the inequality is $x < -2$ or $x > 5$." 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186068679 1074 "Notice that our inequality\n\n$$\nx^2 -3x -10> 0,\n$$\n\nis a \"*greater than*\" inequality. Therefore, we need to find the values of $x$ for which the parabola  \n\n$$\ny =x^2 -3x -10\n$$\n\nis *above* the $x$-axis. We mark these values of $x$ in blue in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/ca/ca22e171b36e21802769e3612a163d29b01209af1ef75887a65ab6013bb1401a.png)\n\nSo, the solution to the inequality is $x < -2$ or $x >   5.$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186068680 1071 "Given the graph below, solve the inequality $-x^2+2x+3 \\leq 0$.\n\n![](images/93/93fa79fb8411a6bdf16b3eb9c1aab489377a9b0d039b0be4b884f8cdc47bf683.png)" 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186068680 1071 "Given the graph below, solve the inequality $-x^2+2x+3 \\leq 0.$    \n\n![](images/93/93fa79fb8411a6bdf16b3eb9c1aab489377a9b0d039b0be4b884f8cdc47bf683.png)" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186068680 1074 "Notice that our inequality\n\n$$\n-x^2+2x+3 \\leq 0\n$$\n\nis a \"*less than or equal to*\" inequality. Therefore, we need to find the values of $x$ for which the parabola\n\n$$\ny =-x^2+2x+3\n$$\n\nis *below* the $x$-axis or *touches* this axis. We mark these values of $x$ in blue in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/6b/6b11e0ab1c3cd92003d3b5330b4eab64e192efbe1953cfbc560b717ccc0e3c57.png)\n\nSo, the solution to the inequality is\n\n$$\nx \\leq -1\n$$\n\nor\n\n$$\nx \\geq 3\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186068680 1074 "Notice that our inequality\n\n$$\n-x^2+2x+3 \\leq 0,\n$$\n\nis a \"*less than or equal to*\" inequality. Therefore, we need to find the values of $x$ for which the parabola \n\n$$\ny =-x^2+2x+3\n$$\n\nis *below* the $x$-axis or *touches* this axis. We mark these values of $x$ in blue in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/6b/6b11e0ab1c3cd92003d3b5330b4eab64e192efbe1953cfbc560b717ccc0e3c57.png)\n\nSo, the solution to the inequality is $x \\leq -1$ or $x \\geq 3.$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186068681 1071 "Given the graph below, solve the inequality $-(x+2)(x-1) <  0.$      \n\n![](images/c7/c764652