nNotice that we have a perfect square in the parentheses that we can factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-12x+36 & = \\\\ {x}^{2}-12x+{6}^{2} & = \\\\ {x}^{2}-2\\cdot 6x+{6}^{2} & = \\\\ (x-6{)}^{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}^{2}-12x+36)-5 & = \\\\ (x-6{)}^{2}-5 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186068701 1038 #edb/ref 17592186237877 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186068921 1038 #edb/ref 17592186237804 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186068921 1038 #edb/ref 17592186237805 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186068924 1038 #edb/ref 17592186237796 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186069010 1068 #edb/ref 17592186065463 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186069010 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237747 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186069010 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186069010 1071 "Calculate the integral $\\displaystyle \\int\\sec (7x)\\tan (7x)dx$." 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186069010 1071 "Calculate the integral $\\int \\operatorname{sec}⁡(7x)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(7x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186069010 1074 "If we let $u=7x,$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=7\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{7}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\operatorname{sec}⁡(7x)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(7x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\operatorname{sec}⁡u\\operatorname{tan}⁡u\\cdot \\frac{1}{7}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}\\int \\operatorname{sec}⁡u\\operatorname{tan}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}\\operatorname{sec}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(7x)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186078397 1038 #edb/ref 17592186238076 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186078397 1038 #edb/ref 17592186238077 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186078397 1038 #edb/ref 17592186238078 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186078563 1038 #edb/ref 17592186237821 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186083144 1071 "Consider the function $f(x) = \\sqrt 3\\sin{2x} - \\cos{2x}$. Find critical points of $f(x)$ for $x \\in \\left(-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)$." 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186083144 1071 "Consider the function $f(x) = \\sqrt 3\\sin{2x} - \\cos{2x}.$ Find critical points of $f(x)$ for $x \\in \\left(-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right).$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186083144 1074 "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either $f'(c) = 0$ or $f'(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(x) &= \\frac{d}{dx}(\\sqrt{3}\\sin 2x - \\cos 2x) \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{3} \\cdot \\frac{d}{dx}(\\sin 2x) - \\frac{d}{dx}(\\cos 2x) \\\\\n&= 2\\sqrt{3}\\cos 2x + 2\\sin 2x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where $f'(c) = 0$. We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x$:\n$2\\sqrt{3}\\cos 2x + 2\\sin 2x|= 0; 2\\sin 2x|=-2\\sqrt{3}\\cos 2x; \\frac{\\sin 2x}{\\cos 2x}|=-\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\cdot \\frac{\\cos 2x}{\\cos 2x}; \\tan 2x|=-\\sqrt{3}$\nThe general solution to our equation is\n$2x|= \\arctan (-\\sqrt{3}) + nπ; 2x|=-\\frac{π}{3} + nπ$.\nSolving for $x$, we get\n$x|=-\\frac{π}{6} + \\frac{nπ}{2}$.\nWe set $n=0, \\pm 1, \\pm 2,\\ldots$, until we have generated all solutions in the domain $-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\lt x \\lt \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$:setting $n=0$ gives $x = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$setting $n=1$ gives $x = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}$All other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\lt x \\lt \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$. So, the solutions to this equation for $x \\in \\left(-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)$ are\n$x =-\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}, \\qquad x = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}$.\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x =-\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$ and $x = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}$.\n- Second, we note that $f'(x)$ exists for all values of $x\\in \\left(-\\dfrac\\pi2, \\dfrac\\pi2\\right)$. So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical points of $f(x)$ for\n\n$$\nx\\in \\left(-\\dfrac\\pi2, \\dfrac\\pi2\\right)\n$$\n\nare\n\n$$\nx_1 =-\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}, \\qquad x_2 = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186083144 1074 "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either $f'(c) = 0$ or $f'(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x) \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where $f'(c) = 0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x & =0 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x & =-2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x} & =-\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡2x & =-\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\sqrt{3})+n\\pi  \\\\ 2x & =-\\frac{\\pi }{3}+n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{n\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0, \\pm 1, \\pm 2,\\ldots,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\lt x \\lt \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n- setting $n=0$ gives $x = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$\n\n- setting $n=1$ gives $x = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\lt x \\lt \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}.$ So, the solutions to this equation for $x \\in \\left(-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)$ are \n\n$$\nx =-\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}, \\qquad x =  \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x =-\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$ and $x = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}.$\n\n- Second, we note that $f'(x)$ exists for all values of $x\\in \\left(-\\dfrac\\pi2, \\dfrac\\pi2\\right).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical points of $f(x)$ for $x\\in \\left(-\\dfrac\\pi2, \\dfrac\\pi2\\right)$ are\n\n$$\nx_1 =-\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}, \\qquad x_2 =  \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}.\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186083148 1071 "A ball is projected vertically down from a building with a height of $110.4 \\, \\textrm{m}$. It takes $4\\,\\textrm{s}$ to hit the ground. What was the initial velocity of the ball?" 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186083148 1071 "A ball is projected vertically down from a building with a height of $110.4 \\, \\textrm{m}.$ It takes $4\\,\\textrm{s}$ to hit the ground. What was the initial velocity of the ball?" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186083148 1074 "To solve this problem, we use the formula\n\n$$\ns=ut+\\dfrac{1}{2}gt^2\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ is the displacement, measured from the starting position in the direction of the initial motion, $u$ is the initial velocity, $t$ is the time since the beginning of the motion, and $g$ is the acceleration due to gravity.\n\nLet's summarize the information we have:\n\n- The ball falls for $t=4\\,\\textrm{s}$.\n- The ball covers the distance of $s=110.4\\, \\textrm{m}$.\n- The acceleration due to gravity is $g=9.8\\,\\textrm{m/s}^2$.\n\nSubstituting these values into the formula and solving for $u$, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ns &= ut + \\frac{1}{2}gt^{2} 