592186087973 1068 #edb/ref 17592186084542 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186087973 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237757 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186087973 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186087973 1071 "The cubic polynomial $P(x)$ has roots $x_{1} = x_{2} =-1, x_{3} = 3$, and the coefficient of the cubic term is $2$. Find $P(x)$." 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186087973 1071 "The cubic polynomial $P(x)$ has roots ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=-1,{x}_{3}=3,$ and the coefficient of the cubic term is $2.$ Find $P(x).$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186087973 1074 "Since our cubic polynomial has roots ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=-1$ and ${x}_{3}=3,$ and the coefficient of the cubic term is $a=2,$ we can write the polynomial as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(x-{x}_{1})(x-{x}_{2})(x-{x}_{3}) & =2(x-(-1))(x-(-1))(x-3) \\\\  & =2(x+1)(x+1)(x-3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(x+1)(x+1)(x-3) & =2({x}^{2}+2x+1)(x-3) \\\\  & =2({x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+2{x}^{2}-6x+x-3) \\\\  & =2({x}^{3}-{x}^{2}-5x-3) \\\\  & =2{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-10x-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x)=2{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-10x-6.$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186092163 1071 "![](images/a0/a0eb20697f72b1ec5f06cc1ebfa4ba364c1023c915950b64cfab2367964db928.png)\n\nFind $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}$ for the polar curve $r = 2^{\\theta}$ (shown above) given that\n\n$$\ny'(\\theta) = 2^{\\theta}(\\ln 2\\sin\\theta+ \\cos\\theta), \\qquad\nx'(\\theta) = 2^{\\theta}(\\ln 2\\cos\\theta-\\sin\\theta) .\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186092163 1071 "![](images/a0/a0eb20697f72b1ec5f06cc1ebfa4ba364c1023c915950b64cfab2367964db928.png)\n\nFind $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}$ for the polar curve $r = 2^{\\theta}$ (shown above) given that\n$y'(\\theta) = 2^{\\theta}(\\ln 2\\sin\\theta+ \\cos\\theta), \\qquad x'(\\theta) = 2^{\\theta}(\\ln 2\\cos\\theta-\\sin\\theta)$." 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186092163 1074 "To find\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\n$$\n\nwe use the rule for differentiating parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{y'(\\theta)}{x'(\\theta)}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= (2^{θ}(\\ln 2\\sin θ + \\cos θ))/(2^{θ}(\\ln 2\\cos θ - \\sin θ)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\ln 2\\sin θ + \\cos θ}{\\ln 2\\cos θ - \\sin θ}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186092163 1074 "To find $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x},$ we use the rule for differentiating parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{y'(\\theta)}{x'(\\theta)}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{2}^{\\theta }(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}{{2}^{\\theta }(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186092164 1071 "Find the slope of the tangent line to the polar curve $r = 1-\\cos\\theta$ at $\\theta = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$, given that\n$y'(\\theta) = \\cos\\theta - \\cos2\\theta, \\qquad x'(\\theta) = -\\sin\\theta + \\sin2\\theta$." 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186092164 1071 "Find the slope of the tangent line to the polar curve $r = 1-\\cos\\theta$ at $\\theta = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4},$ given that\n\n$$\ny'(\\theta) = \\cos\\theta  - \\cos2\\theta, \\qquad\nx'(\\theta) = -\\sin\\theta + \\sin2\\theta.\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186092164 1074 "To find\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\n$$\n\nwe use the rule for differentiating parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{y'(\\theta)}{x'(\\theta)}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the numerator and denominator at\n\n$$\n\\theta=\\dfrac\\pi4\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{′}(\\frac{π}{4}) &= \\cos (\\frac{π}{4}) - \\cos (\\frac{π}{2}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} - 0 \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{′}(\\frac{π}{4}) &= -\\sin (\\frac{π}{4}) + \\sin (\\frac{π}{2}) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} + 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left.\\frac{dy}{dx}\\right|_{θ = π/4} &= (y^{′}(\\frac{π}{4}))/(x^{′}(\\frac{π}{4})) \\\\\n&= ((\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}))/((-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} + 1))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the numerator and denominator by $2$ and simplifying, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left.\\frac{dy}{dx}\\right|_{θ = π/4} &= ((\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}))/((-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} + 1)) \\\\\n&= (2 \\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}))/(2 \\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} + 1)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{-\\sqrt{2} + 2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2 - \\sqrt{2}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2 - \\sqrt{2}} \\cdot \\frac{2 + \\sqrt{2}}{2 + \\sqrt{2}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2\\sqrt{2} + 2}{2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{2} + 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186092164 1074 "To find $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x},$ we use the rule for differentiating parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{y'(\\theta)}{x'(\\theta)}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the numerator and denominator at $\\theta=\\dfrac\\pi4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nHence, the slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{\\theta =\\pi /4} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})}{{x}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}{(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nMultiplying the numerator and denominator by $2$ and simplifying, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{\\theta =\\pi /4} & =\\frac{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}{(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}{2\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{-\\sqrt{2}+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2-\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2-\\sqrt{2}}\\cdot \\frac{2+\\sqrt{2}}{2+\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}+2}{2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186092165 1071 "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $2$ and $8$, respectively, what is the degree of $P \\cdot Q$?" 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186092165 1071 "If $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are polynomials of degree $2$ and $8$, respectively, what is the degree of $P \\cdot Q?$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186092165 1074 "Since $P$ is a polynomial of degree $2$, the leading term contains an $x^2$.\n\nSince $Q$ is a polynomial of degree $8$, the leading term contains an $x^8$.\n\nIn $P \\cdot Q$, the $x^2$ term in $P$ multiplies with the $x^8$ term in $Q$ to form a term containing\n\n$$\nx^2 \\cdot x^8 = x^{2+8} = x^{10}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the degree of $P \\cdot Q$ must be $10$." 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186092165 1074 "Since $P$ is a polynomial of degree $2,$ the leading term contains an $x^2.$\n\nSince $Q$ is a polynomial of degree $8,$ the leading term contains an $x^8.$\n\nIn $P \\cdot Q,$ the $x^2$ term in $P$ multiplies with the $x^8$ term in $Q$ to form a term containing $x^2 \\cdot x^8 = x^{2+8} = x^{10}.$\n\nTherefore, the degree of $P \\cdot Q$ must be $10.$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186092189 1038 #edb/ref 17592186237937 13194139533391 17592186045