⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{2-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the double-angle formula for cosine, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )=1-2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .$ Substituting $\\theta =\\frac{t}{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{t}{2}) & =1-2{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{t}{2}) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t & =1-2{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{t}{2}) \\\\ 1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & =2{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{t}{2}) \\\\ 2-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & =4{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{t}{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{2-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{4{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{t}{2})}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{t}{2})\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-2\\cdot 2\\cdot {\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{t}{2})|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =-4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡0) \\\\  & =-4(0-1) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186092478 1068 #edb/ref 17592186089009 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186092478 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237493 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186092478 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186092478 1071 "![](images/51/5117030d23370b2167513b7a2eee5258aae4eb9f45ad428fd82e4334c4ce6638.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=1+3t,\\,y=2{t}^{3/2},\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186092478 1071 "![](images/51/5117030d23370b2167513b7a2eee5258aae4eb9f45ad428fd82e4334c4ce6638.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the curve defined parametrically as\n$x = 1 + 3t, y = 2t^{3/2}, t ∈ [0, 1]$." 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186092478 1074 "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+3t)=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{3/2})=3{t}^{1/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{(3{t}^{1/2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{9+9t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{1}(1+t)^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3\\cdot {\\frac{2}{3}(1+t)^{3/2}|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{8}-2 \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186096609 1071 "![](images/74/7431bd3281e4e49f4caaa5b701885fb64c58196b2256af9d8744decd9a0d0793.png)\n\n  \n\nConsider the diagram above, where $\\overrightarrow{PS}=\\mathbf{a},$ $\\overrightarrow{QS}=\\mathbf{b},$ $\\overrightarrow{PT}=\\mathbf{c},$ and $\\overrightarrow{TS}=\\mathbf{d}.$ Find $\\overrightarrow{TQ}$ in terms of $\\mathbf{a},\\mathbf{b}, \\mathbf{c},$ and $\\mathbf{d}.$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186096609 1071 "![](images/74/7431bd3281e4e49f4caaa5b701885fb64c58196b2256af9d8744decd9a0d0793.png)\n\nConsider the diagram above, where $\\overrightarrow{PS}=\\mathbf{a}$, $\\overrightarrow{QS}=\\mathbf{b}$, $\\overrightarrow{PT}=\\mathbf{c}$, and $\\overrightarrow{TS}=\\mathbf{d}$. Find $\\overrightarrow{TQ}$ in terms of $\\mathbf{a},\\mathbf{b}, \\mathbf{c}$, and $\\mathbf{d}$." 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186096609 1074 "We want to find a \"path\" from $T$ to $Q$ consisting only of the vectors $\\mathbf a, \\mathbf b, \\mathbf c$, and $\\mathbf d$.\n\nAccording to the triangle law of addition,\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{\\overrightarrow{TQ}}} = {\\color{blue}{\\overrightarrow{TS}}} + {\\color{blue}{\\overrightarrow{SQ}}}\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/49/4902397acd28e2e5f85063ba55c576d3768360225a705f31a66bd460405dd5a6.png)\n\nNow, from the original diagram, we have that\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}\\overrightarrow{TS}} = \\mathbf{d}, \\qquad {\\color{blue}{\\overrightarrow{SQ}}} = -\\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nLet's add these to our diagram.\n\n![](images/08/08086f7e09568f908aa4919a1156d52e465216221be8908e72a1fd90ec8843dd.png)\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nTQ^(→) &= TS^(→) + SQ^(→) \\\\\n&= d + (-b) \\\\\n&= d - b\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186096609 1074 "We want to find a \"path\" from $T$ to $Q$ consisting only of the vectors $\\mathbf a, \\mathbf b, \\mathbf c,$ and $\\mathbf d.$\n\nAccording to the triangle law of addition, \n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{\\overrightarrow{TQ}}} = {\\color{blue}{\\overrightarrow{TS}}} + {\\color{blue}{\\overrightarrow{SQ}}},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/49/4902397acd28e2e5f85063ba55c576d3768360225a705f31a66bd460405dd5a6.png)\n\n \n\nNow, from the original diagram, we have that\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}\\overrightarrow{TS}} = \\mathbf{d}, \\qquad {\\color{blue}{\\overrightarrow{SQ}}} = -\\mathbf{b}.\n$$\n\nLet's add these to our diagram.\n\n![](images/08/08086f7e09568f908aa4919a1156d52e465216221be8908e72a1fd90ec8843dd.png)\n\n  \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{TQ} & =\\overset{\\to }{TS}+\\overset{\\to }{SQ} \\\\  & =d+(-b) \\\\  & =d-b.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186096616 1038 #edb/ref 17592186237855 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186096616 1038 #edb/ref 17592186237856 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186096619 1038 #edb/ref 17592186237857 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186096619 1038 #edb/ref 17592186237858 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186096620 1038 #edb/ref 17592186237859 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186096622 1038 #edb/ref 17592186237860 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186096633 1038 #edb/ref 17592186237908 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186096633 1038 #edb/ref 17592186237910 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186096644 1038 #edb/ref 17592186237789 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186096645 1038 #edb/ref 17592186237791 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186096671 1038 #edb/ref 17592186237802 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186096675 1038 #edb/ref 17592186237863 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186096675 1038 #edb/ref 17592186237864 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186096675 1038 #edb/ref 17592186237866 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186096675 1038 #edb/ref 17592186237867 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186096675 1038 #edb/ref 17592186237869 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186096677 1038 #edb/ref 17592186237876 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186096689 1038 #edb/ref 17592186237904 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186096689 1038 #edb/ref 17592186237905 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186096689 1038 #edb/ref 17592186237907 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186096690 1038 #edb/ref 17592186237911 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186096690 1038 #edb/ref 17592186237912 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186096690 1038 #edb/ref 17592186237914 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186096690 1038 #edb/ref 17592186237915 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186096690 1038 #edb/ref 17592186237916 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186096763 1038 #edb/ref 17592186238066 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186096851 1038 #edb/ref 17592186237785 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186096930 1068 #edb/ref 17592186093524 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186096930 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237600 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186096930 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186096930 1071 "Solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & x+2y-z=10 \\\\  & y+5z=15 \\\\  & -z=5\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186096930 1071 "Solve the following system of equations:\n${x + 2y - z = 10; y + 5z = 15; - z = 5$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186096930 1074 "The third equation states that \n\n$$\n-z=5\\,⟹\\,z=-5.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\ny+5(-5)=15\\,⟹\\,y=15+25=40.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $z=-5$ and $y=40$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\nx+2(40)-(-5)=10\\,⟹\\,x=10-80-5=-75.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=-75,y=40$ and $z=-5.$ In vector form, the solution can be written as $⟨-75,40,-5⟩.$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186096931 1068 #edb/ref 17592186093530 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186096931 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237628 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186096931 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186096931 1071 "Solve the system of equations represented by the augmented matrix\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$." 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186096931 1071 "Solve the system of equations represented by the augmented matrix \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186096931 1074 "The matrix represents a system of three linear equations in three variables, say, $x$, $y$, and $z.$ We obtain the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & x+y+z=1 \\\\  & y+z=1 \\\\  & z=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe third equation states that $z=1.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\ny+(1)=1\\,⟹\\,y=1-1=0.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $z=1$ and $y=0$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\nx+(0)+(1)=1\\,⟹\\,x=1-1=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=0$, $y=0$, and $z=1.$ In vector form, the solution can be written as $⟨0,0,1⟩.$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186100802 1071 "Find the first three terms of the binomial expansion of ${( x - 3y )}^ 5.$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186100802 1071 "Find the first three terms of the binomial expansion of ${(x - 3y)}^ 5$." 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186100802 1074 "The binomial theorem states that for any positive integer $n$, we have\n\n$$\n{(a + b)^n} = {n \\choose 0}{a^n}{b^0} + {n \\choose 1}{a^{n - 1}}{b^1} + {n \\choose 2}{a^{n - 2}}{b^2} + \\cdots + {n \\choose n}{a^0}{b^n}\n$$\n\nIn the case of ${(x - 3y)}^ 5$, we have $a=x$, $b=-3y$, and $n=5$.\n\nTherefore, the first three terms of ${(x - 3y)}^ 5$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x - 3y)^{5} &= (\\frac{5}{0})(x)^{5}(-3y)^{0} + (\\frac{5}{1})(x)^{5 - 1}(-3y)^{1} + (\\frac{5}{2})(x)^{5 - 2}(-3y)^{2} + ⋯ \\\\\n&= 1(x)^{5} + 5(x)^{4}(-3y) + 10(x)^{3}(9y^{2}) + ⋯ \\\\\n&= x^{5} - 15x^{4}y + 90x^{3}y^{2} + ⋯\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186100802 1074 "The binomial theorem states that for any positive integer $n,$ we have\n\n \n\n$$\n{(a + b)^n} = {n \\choose 0}{a^n}{b^0} + {n \\choose 1}{a^{n - 1}}{b^1} + {n \\choose 2}{a^{n - 2}}{b^2} +  \\cdots  + {n \\choose n}{a^0}{b^n}.\n$$\n\nIn the case of ${(x - 3y)}^ 5,$ we have  $a=x,$ $b=-3y ,$ and $n=5.$ \n\nTherefore, the first three terms of ${(x - 3y)}^ 5$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3y)^{5} & =(\\frac{5}{0})(x)^{5}(-3y)^{0}+(\\frac{5}{1})(x)^{5-1}(-3y)^{1}+(\\frac{5}{2})(x)^{5-2}(-3y)^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1(x)^{5}+5(x)^{4}(-3y)+10(x)^{3}(9{y}^{2})+⋯ \\\\  & ={x}^{5}-15{x}^{4}y+90{x}^{3}{y}^{2}+⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186100807 1071 "Consider the differential equation\n$\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = y$.\n\nWhich of the following is a solution to this equation?\n\n1. $y = e^x + 1$\n2. $y = -e^x$\n3. $y = 2e^x$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186100807 1071 "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = y.\n$$\n\n Which of the following is a solution to this equation?\n\n- $y = e^x + 1$\n\n- $y = -e^x$\n\n- $y = 2e^x$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186100807 1074 "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWe check each proposed solution by differentiating and substituting it into the original equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y = e^x + 1$, we get $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = e^x$. Substituting into the original equation, we get\n$\\frac{dy}{dx}|= y; e^{x}|=^(?)e^{x} + 1; 0 \\mid \\ne 1.\\cdot$\nThis statement is *false*. Therefore, the function $y = e^x$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n- Differentiating $y = -e^x$, we get $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = -e^x$. Substituting into the original equation, we get\n$\\frac{dy}{dx}|= y; - e^{x}|=-e^{x}.✓$\nThis statement is *true*. Therefore, the function $y = -e^x$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n- Differentiating $y = 2e^x$, we get $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = 2e^x$. Substituting into the original equation, we get\n$\\frac{dy}{dx}|= y; 2e^{x}|= 2e^{x}.✓$\nThis statement is *true*. Therefore, the function $y = 2e^x$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186100807 1074 "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWe check each proposed solution by differentiating and substituting it into the original equation. \n\n- Differentiating $y = e^x + 1,$ we get $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = e^x.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y \\\\ {e}^{x} & \\overset{?}{=}{e}^{x}+1 \\\\ 0 & \\ne 1.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *false*. Therefore, the function $y = e^x+1$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y = -e^x,$ we get $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = -e^x.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y \\\\ -{e}^{x} & =-{e}^{x}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true*. Therefore, the function $y = -e^x$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y = 2e^x,$ we get $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = 2e^x.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y \\\\ 2{e}^{x} & =2{e}^{x}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true*. Therefore, the function $y = 2e^x$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186100878 1038 #edb/ref 17592186237996 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186100878 1038 #edb/ref 17592186238017 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186100885 1038 #edb/ref 17592186237942 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186100885 1038 #edb/ref 17592186237943 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186100885 1038 #edb/ref 17592186237944 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186100885 1038 #edb/ref 17592186237945 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186100895 1038 #edb/ref 17592186237922 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186100895 1038 #edb/ref 17592186237924 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186100896 1038 #edb/ref 17592186237930 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186100896 1038 #edb/ref 17592186237931 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186100896 1038 #edb/ref 17592186237932 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186100905 1038 #edb/ref 17592186237921 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186100906 1038 #edb/ref 17592186237918 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186100919 1038 #edb/ref 17592186238060 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186105697 1071 "The closed curve $C$ is defined as $x^2+y^2 = 2$. Find the outward-pointing unit normal vector on $C$." 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186105697 1071 "The closed curve $C$ is defined as $x^2+y^2 = 2.$ Find the outward-pointing unit normal vector on $C.$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186105697 1074 "We can parameterize $C$ as follows:\n\n![](images/0c/0c0a4b177a997ffb38489abc902a1c78d027d0da661b1498fc97d3d9a77862d9.png)\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, positively oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector $\\mathbf r'(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $\\mathbf r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented counterclockwise, we rotate $\\mathbf r'(t)$ by $90^\\circ$ *clockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a $90^\\circ$ clockwise rotation is\n\n![](images/0c/0cd188ffe544d454a3375df5f043096888f6dfd5243551d35502efda4bcfeb11.png)\n\nRotating the vector $\\mathbf r'(t)$ by $90^\\circ$ clockwise, we get\n\n![](images/5f/5f0d414a5388ae374f46f75825b9ffec7725649a97a45de24f8178d95a94b1ad.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of $\\mathbf r'(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation).\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n||\\mathbf r'(t)|| = \\sqrt{(-\\sqrt{2}\\sin t)^2 + (\\sqrt{2}\\cos t)^2} = \\sqrt{2},\n$$\n\nand since for our curve $x(t) = \\sqrt{2}\\cos{t}$ and $y(t) = \\sqrt{2}\\sin{t},$ it follows that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186105697 1074 "We can parameterize $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, positively oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector $\\mathbf r'(t)$.\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $\\mathbf r(t)$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(t) &= \\begin{bmatrix}x^{′}(t) \\\\ y^{′}(t)\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}-\\sqrt{2}\\sin t \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\cos t\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented counterclockwise, we rotate $\\mathbf r'(t)$ by $90^\\circ$ *clockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a $90^\\circ$ clockwise rotation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nRotating the vector $\\mathbf r'(t)$ by $90^\\circ$ clockwise, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of $\\mathbf r'(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation).\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\n\\mid \\mid \\mathbf r'(t) \\mid \\mid = \\sqrt{(-\\sqrt{2}\\sin t)^2 + (\\sqrt{2}\\cos t)^2} = \\sqrt{2}\n$$\n\nand since for our curve\n\n$$\nx(t) = \\sqrt{2}\\cos{t}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ny(t) = \\sqrt{2}\\sin{t}\n$$\n\nit follows that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nn &= (1)/(\\mid \\mid r^{′}(t) \\mid \\mid)\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{2}\\cos t \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\sin t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{2}\\cos t \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\sin t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186105698 1071 "The closed curve $C$ is defined as $\\mathbf r(t) =  12\\sin t\\,\\mathbf{i} + 9\\cos t \\,\\mathbf{j}$ for $t \\in [0,2\\pi).$ Find the outward-pointing unit normal vector on $C.$\n\n*Hint: Observe that $C$ is traversed clockwise.*" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186105698 1071 "The closed curve $C$ is defined as $\\mathbf r(t) = 12\\sin t\\,\\mathbf{i} + 9\\cos t \\,\\mathbf{j}$ for $t \\in [0,2\\pi)$. Find the outward-pointing unit normal vector on $C$.\n\n*Hint: Observe that $C$ is traversed clockwise.*" 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186105698 1074 "For convenience, we express $\\mathbf r(t)$ in column vector notation:\n\n![](images/00/007f19fb0c6e24115d719178a4b44e78c9881b9f71f52d0f2970480358e3faab.png)\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, *negatively* oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector $\\mathbf r'(t).$\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $\\mathbf r(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}12\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -9\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented clockwise, we rotate $\\mathbf r'(t)$ by $90^\\circ$ *counterclockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a $90^\\circ$ counterclockwise rotation is\n\n![](images/ec/ec0ddbbff34ed096bb872f23663f97005932a4ea8f368059cc4b511a5c9c76cc.png)\n\nRotating the vector $\\mathbf r'(t)$ by $90^\\circ$ counterclockwise, we get\n\n![](images/6f/6f74bda044d15ed504a0b95b1e60ae8542f2b304f1e2712ae8a1d840a45aeaad.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of $\\mathbf r'(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation). Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =\\frac{1}{||{r}^{′}(t)||}[\\begin{aligned}9\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 12\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{9\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+12\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j}{\\sqrt{144{cos}^{2}⁡t+81{sin}^{2}⁡t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186105698 1074 "For convenience, we express $\\mathbf r(t)$ in column vector notation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the outward-pointing normal vector of a closed, *negatively* oriented curve, we follow three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: We compute the tangent vector $\\mathbf r'(t)$.\n\nDifferentiating the expression for $\\mathbf r(t)$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(t) &= \\begin{bmatrix}x^{′}(t) \\\\ y^{′}(t)\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}12\\cos t \\\\ -9\\sin t\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Since $C$ is oriented clockwise, we rotate $\\mathbf r'(t)$ by $90^\\circ$ *counterclockwise* to obtain an outward-pointing normal vector.\n\nThe rotation matrix that corresponds to a $90^\\circ$ counterclockwise rotation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nRotating the vector $\\mathbf r'(t)$ by $90^\\circ$ counterclockwise, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Finally, we normalize the vector found in step 2.\n\nNote that the magnitude of the vector found in step 2 equals the magnitude of $\\mathbf r'(t)$ (since they differ only by a rotation). Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nn &= (1)/(\\mid \\mid r^{′}(t) \\mid \\mid)\\begin{bmatrix}9\\sin t \\\\ 12\\cos t\\end{bmatrix} = \\frac{9\\sin ti + 12\\cos tj}{\\sqrt{144\\cos^{2} t + 81\\sin^{2} t}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186105708 1038 #edb/ref 17592186237854 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186105721 1038 #edb/ref 17592186238006 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186105721 1038 #edb/ref 17592186