23 1038 #edb/ref 17592186237851 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186105923 1038 #edb/ref 17592186237852 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186105923 1038 #edb/ref 17592186237853 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186106032 1068 #edb/ref 17592186102048 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186106032 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237716 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186106032 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186106032 1071 "Consider the augmented matrix $M$ given by\n$M = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & -6 & 12 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 12 & -16 \\\\ 1 & -4 & -12 & 21\\end{bmatrix}$.\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce $M$ to row echelon form, and then find the general solution of the linear system represented by the matrix." 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186106032 1071 "Consider the augmented matrix $M$ given by \n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -6 & 12 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 12 & -16 \\\\ 1 & -4 & -12 & 21\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nUse the standard method of Gaussian elimination to reduce $M$ to row echelon form, and then find the general solution of the linear system represented by the matrix." 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186106032 1074 "Reducing $M$ to echelon form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -6 & 12 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 12 & -16 \\\\ 1 & -4 & -12 & 21\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -6 & 12 \\\\ 0 & 8 & 24 & -40 \\\\ 1 & -4 & -12 & 21\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -6 & 12 \\\\ 0 & 8 & 24 & -40 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -6 & 9\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{8}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -6 & 12 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & -5 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -6 & 9\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -6 & 12 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x-2y-6z=12 \\\\ 0x+y+3z=-5 \\\\ 0x+0y+0z=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince there are no such numbers $x,y,z$ that form a solution of the final equation $0x+0y+0z=-1,$ we can conclude that our system has no solution." 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186115166 1071 "![](images/33/33700b49845ba054447ce69356324d3e421d256344149b447756cdbf0cf5a905.png)\n\nConsider $\\triangle ABC$ shown above. The line segment $\\overline{CH}$ is the altitude corresponding to $\\overline{AB}$. If $m\\angle BAC = 115^\\circ$, calculate $m\\angle HCA$." 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186115166 1071 "![](images/33/33700b49845ba054447ce69356324d3e421d256344149b447756cdbf0cf5a905.png)\n\nConsider $\\triangle ABC$ shown above. The line segment $\\overline{CH}$ is the altitude corresponding to $\\overline{AB}.$ If $m\\angle BAC = 115^\\circ,$ calculate $m\\angle HCA.$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186115166 1074 "Since the straight angle must have a measure of $180^\\circ$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠HAC + m∠BAC &= 180^{∘} \\\\\nm∠HAC + 115^{∘} &= 180^{∘} \\\\\nm∠HAC &= 65^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\triangle ACH$ is a right triangle. Since its interior angles must sum to $180^\\circ$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠HCA + m∠HAC + m∠AHC &= 180^{∘} \\\\\nm∠HCA + 65^{∘} + 90^{∘} &= 180^{∘} \\\\\nm∠HCA + 155^{∘} &= 180^{∘} \\\\\nm∠HCA &= 25^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186115166 1074 "Since the straight angle must have a measure of $180^\\circ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠HAC+m∠BAC & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠HAC+{115}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠HAC & ={65}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\triangle ACH$ is a right triangle. Since its interior angles must sum to $180^\\circ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠HCA+m∠HAC+m∠AHC & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠HCA+{65}^{∘}+{90}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠HCA+{155}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠HCA & ={25}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186115206 1038 #edb/ref 17592186237795 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186115225 1038 #edb/ref 17592186237797 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186115225 1038 #edb/ref 17592186237798 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186115386 1038 #edb/ref 17592186237865 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186115386 1038 #edb/ref 17592186237868 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186115387 1038 #edb/ref 17592186237875 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186115387 1038 #edb/ref 17592186237878 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186115387 1038 #edb/ref 17592186237879 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186115388 1038 #edb/ref 17592186237870 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186115388 1038 #edb/ref 17592186237871 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186115388 1038 #edb/ref 17592186237872 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186115388 1038 #edb/ref 17592186237873 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186115388 1038 #edb/ref 17592186237874 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186120069 1068 #edb/ref 17592186116591 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186120069 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237711 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186120069 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186120069 1071 "Find a vector-valued function $r(u, v)$ that parametrizes the surface defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z = x^{3} - xy$." 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186120069 1071 "Find a vector-valued function $r(u,v)$ that parametrizes the surface defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z={x}^{3}-xy.$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186120069 1074 "A point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface has the corresponding position vector given by\n\n$$\nr=x\\,i+y\\,j+z\\,k=x\\,i+y\\,j+({x}^{3}-xy)\\,k.\n$$\n\nNow, setting $x=u$ and $y=v,$ we obtain the vector function that parametrizes the surface:\n\n$$\nr(u,v)=u\\,i+v\\,j+({u}^{3}-uv)\\,k\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186120070 1068 #edb/ref 17592186116597 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186120070 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237602 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186120070 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186120070 1071 "$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,\\text{_____}⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $r(u,v)$ parametrizes the surface defined in Cartesian coordinates as \n${x}^{3}+{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}=2$\nfor $z>0.$ What is the third component of $r?$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186120070 1071 "$r(u, v) = ⟨u, v, _____⟩$\n\nThe vector-valued function $r(u, v)$ parametrizes the surface defined in Cartesian coordinates as\n$x^{3} + y^{2} - z^{2} = 2$\nfor $z > 0$. What is the third component of $r$?" 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186120070 1074 "First, we write the equation of the surface in the form $z=f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}-{z}^{2} & =2 \\\\ {z}^{2} & ={x}^{3}+{y}^{2}-2 \\\\ z & =\\sqrt{{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe disregard the negative solution since we are given that $z>0.$\n\nNow, a point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface has the corresponding position vector given by\n\n$$\nr=⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩=⟨x,\\,y,\\,\\sqrt{{x}^{3}+{y}^{2}-2}⟩.\n$$\n\nFinally, setting $x=u$ and $y=v,$ we obtain the vector function that parametrizes the surface:\n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,\\sqrt{{u}^{3}+{v}^{2}-2}⟩\n$$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $r$ is $\\sqrt{{u}^{3}+{v}^{2}-2}.$\n\n**Note:** The parametrization is well-defined for $u$ and $v$ such that ${u}^{3}+{v}^{2}-2\\ge 0.$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186120071 1068 #edb/ref 17592186116603 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186120071 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237463 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186120071 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186120071 1071 "$$\nf(r,\\theta )=⟨r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\text{_____},\\,2{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(r,\\theta )$ parametrizes the surface defined in cylindrical polar coordinates as \n$z=2{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\nWhat is the second component of $f?$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186120071 1071 "$f(r, θ) = ⟨r\\cos θ, _____, 2r^{2}\\cos θ⟩$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(r, θ)$ parametrizes the surface defined in cylindrical polar coordinates as\n$z = 2r^{2}\\cos θ$.\nWhat is the second component of $f$?" 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186120071 1074 "If $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point and $(x,y,z)$ are the corresponding Cartesian coordinates of that point, then we have\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nNow, a point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface has the corresponding position vector given by\n\n$$\nf=⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩=⟨r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z⟩.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $z=2{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ we obtain the parametrization\n\n$$\nf(r,\\theta )=⟨r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,2{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second component of $f$ is $r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186120073 1068 #edb/ref 17592186116615 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186120073 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237608 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186120073 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186120073 1071 "$$\nf(\\theta ,ϕ)=⟨\\text{_____},\\,ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(\\theta ,ϕ)$ parametrizes the surface defined in spherical polar coordinates as \n$\\rho =ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\nWhat is the first component of $f?$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186120073 1071 "$f(θ, ϕ) = ⟨_____, ϕ\\cos θ\\sin θ\\sin ϕ, ϕ\\cos θ\\cos ϕ⟩$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(θ, ϕ)$ parametrizes the surface defined in spherical polar coordinates as\n$ρ = ϕ\\cos θ$.\nWhat is the first component of $f$?" 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186120073 1074 "If $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNow, a point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface has the corresponding position vector given by\n\n$$\nf=⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩=⟨\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $\\rho =ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\theta ,ϕ) & =⟨ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨ϕ{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $f$ is $ϕ{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ.$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186120074 1068 #edb/ref 17592186116621 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186120074 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237610 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186120074 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186120074 1071 "$$\nf(\\theta ,ϕ)=⟨ϕ\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\text{ _____ }⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(\\theta ,ϕ)$ parametrizes the surface defined in spherical polar coordinates as \n$ϕ=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$\nWhat is the third component of $f?$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186120074 1071 "$f(θ, ϕ) = ⟨ϕ\\cot θ\\sin ϕ, ϕ\\sin ϕ, _____⟩$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(θ, ϕ)$ parametrizes the surface defined in spherical polar coordinates as\n$ϕ = ρ\\sin θ$.\nWhat is the third component of $f$?" 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186120074 1074 "First, we write the equation of the surface in the form $\\rho =f(\\theta ,ϕ):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ϕ & =\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\rho  & =\\frac{ϕ}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNow, a point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface has the corresponding position vector given by\n\n$$\nf=⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩=⟨\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $\\rho =\\frac{ϕ}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta },$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\theta ,ϕ) & =⟨\\frac{ϕ}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\frac{ϕ}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,\\frac{ϕ}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨ϕ\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,ϕ\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $f$ is $ϕ\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ.$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186124438 1071 "Let $M$ be the set of all $2\\times2$ real matrices whose entries are non-negative. Then, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $I_2 \\in M$\n\n- For any $A \\in M$ and $\\alpha \\in \\mathbb{R}$, we have $\\alpha \\cdot A \\in M.$\n\n- The set is closed under scalar multiplication." 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186124438 1071 "Let $M$ be the set of all $2\\times2$ real matrices whose entries are non-negative. Then, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $I_2 \\in M$\n2. For any $A \\in M$ and $\\alpha \\in \\mathbb{R}$, we have $\\alpha \\cdot A \\in M$.\n3. The set is closed under scalar multiplication." 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186124438 1074 "Let's verify the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, ![](images/19/191451016703b3287c7e58fdb59e22effb919c97dcad7a9e3bec09efd115795a.png) because all its entries are non-negative.\n\n- Statement II is false. There exists a $2\\times2$ real matrix in $M$ that has a scalar multiple not in $M.$ \n\nFor example, let ![](images/3b/3b13b13c14eb335adb58b3d1ff3132b4e189065033481aaf371a9376188f2443.png) and $\\alpha=-1\\in\\mathbb{R}.$ Notice that $A$ belongs to $M$ since statement I is true. However,\n![](images/a9/a906a8520f0b2f676bcf97411232fa3303f87aec9dcae9abe19dca00ab6da1a7.png)\nSince the entries on the main diagonal are negative, we conclude that $-1 \\cdot A \\notin M.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, the set is *not* closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186124438 1074 "Let's verify the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$ because all its entries are non-negative.\n- Statement II is false. There exists a $2\\times2$ real matrix in $M$ that has a scalar multiple not in $M$. For example, let $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\alpha=-1\\in\\mathbb{R}$. Notice that $A$ belongs to $M$ since statement I is true. However,\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nSince the entries on the main diagonal are negative, we conclude that $-1 \\cdot A \\notin M$.\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, the set is *not* closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186124439 1071 "Consider the following sentence:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\textrm{Null}(A) = \\{\\mathbf{ 0}\\},$ then $\\text{__________}\\,.$*\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\n- $\\det(A) = 0$\n\n- $\\textrm{rank}(A) = n$\n\n- $A$ has $n$ pivot columns" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186124439 1071 "Consider the following sentence:\n\n> *If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\textrm{Null}(A) = \\{\\mathbf{0}\\}$, then $\\text{__________}\\,$.*\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\n1. $\\det(A) = 0$\n2. $\\textrm{rank}(A) = n$\n3. $A$ has $n$ pivot columns" 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186124439 1074 "The invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible ![](images/7a/7ab0bb5d64ace5e31ea02d022aa0d8696deb3b62555cfccac069ba74ff750526.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the options separately.\n\n- The following statement is false:\n\n$\\qquad$ *If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\textrm{Null}(A) = \\{\\mathbf{ 0}\\},$ then $\\det(A) = 0.$* $\\:{\\color{red}\\times}$\n\nSince $\\textrm{Null}(A) = \\{\\mathbf{ 0}\\},$ then according to the invertible matrix theorem, the matrix $A$ must be invertible, which means $\\det(A) \\ne 0.$\n\n- The following statement is true:\n\n$\\qquad$ *If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\textrm{Null}(A) = \\{\\mathbf{ 0}\\},$ then $\\textrm{rank}(A) = n.$* $\\:{\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n\nIndeed, since $A$ is invertible, then according to the invertible matrix theorem, we have $\\textrm{rank}(A)=n.$\n\n- The following statement is true:\n\n$\\qquad$ *If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\textrm{Null}(A) = \\{\\mathbf{ 0}\\},$ then $A$ has $n$ pivot columns.* $\\:{\\color{green}\\checkmark}$ \n\nIndeed, since $A$ is invertible, then according to the invertible matrix theorem, we have $\\textrm{dim(Col}(A))=n,$ which means that $A$ has exactly $n$ pivot columns.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186124439 1074 "The invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n> $A$ is invertible $Col(A) = R^{n}; dim(Col(A)) = n = rank(A); Null(A) = {0}; dim(Null(A)) = 0 = nullity(A)$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the options separately.\n\n- The following statement is false:$\\qquad$ *If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\textrm{Null}(A) = \\{\\mathbf{0}\\}$, then $\\det(A) = 0$.* $\\:{\\color{red}\\times}$Since $\\textrm{Null}(A) = \\{\\mathbf{0}\\}$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem, the matrix $A$ must be invertible, which means $\\det(A) \\ne 0$.\n- The following statement is true:$\\qquad$ *If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\textrm{Null}(A) = \\{\\mathbf{0}\\}$, then $\\textrm{rank}(A) = n$.* $\\:{\\color{green}\\checkmark}$Indeed, since $A$ is invertible, then according to the invertible matrix theorem, we have $\\textrm{rank}(A)=n$.\n- The following statement is true:$\\qquad$ *If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\textrm{Null}(A) = \\{\\mathbf{0}\\}$, then $A$ has $n$ pivot columns.* $\\:{\\color{green}\\checkmark}$ Indeed, since $A$ is invertible, then according to the invertible matrix theorem, we have $\\textrm{dim(Col}(A))=n$, which means that $A$ has exactly $n$ pivot columns.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186124440 1071 "![](images/0a/0ab76d251e530205b2fad22f1b4a4677370459e5cd7e19da4220c7c4baac51a3.png)\n\nConsider the equivalence above, where $A$ is an $n \\!\\times\\! n$ matrix. What should be placed int