4537bd087a91d86d86ffac29b14b1b677.png)\n\n \n\n- Statement II is false. The scatter plot has only one dot whose horizontal coordinate equals $1.4,$ namely, $(1.4,30).$ This means that only one location sold popcorn for $\\[math]1.40$ per bag.\n\n![](images/52/52427ff02f5e6df40c88ebe5fb7adb4088a3e5d4a6912a11dec84c02795289a2.png)\n\n- Statement III is false. From the scatter plot, one location sold more than $30$ bags of popcorn, represented by the dot above the horizontal line.\n\n![](images/50/506443dc2dd0de1e1dc9a4ca9a635bddfdecd6963d2f2e21177329abc30907ba.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186152051 1074 "![](images/02/02990da3d49bc34568536cfbfe94dbc8a1ab1ef15196991434805a4471004138.png)\n\nTo determine the number of hours of sleep the student with a GPA of $80\\%$ gets, we locate $80$ on the vertical axis and move in the horizontal direction until we find some points, as shown above.\n\nThe highlighted point has coordinates $(6,80)$. Therefore, the student with a GPA of $80\\%$ sleeps for $6$ hours per night." 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186152051 1074 "![](images/02/02990da3d49bc34568536cfbfe94dbc8a1ab1ef15196991434805a4471004138.png)\n\nTo determine the number of hours of sleep the student with a GPA of $80\\%$ gets, we locate $80$ on the vertical axis and move in the horizontal direction until we find some points, as shown above.\n\nThe highlighted point has coordinates $(6,80).$ Therefore, the student with a GPA of $80\\%$ sleeps for $6$ hours per night." 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186152052 1071 "![](images/79/793fd170c89b87cc168ead4bb92613135d503143a8b7f8dc7b7c39a3d2d065fa.png)\n\nWhich of the lines $p,q,r$, or $s$ is the trend line of the scatter plot above?" 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186152052 1071 "![](images/79/793fd170c89b87cc168ead4bb92613135d503143a8b7f8dc7b7c39a3d2d065fa.png)\n\nWhich of the lines $p,q,r,$ or $s$ is the trend line of the scatter plot above?" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186152053 1074 "Notice that the trend line passes through the points\n\n$$\n(x_1,y_1)=(-4,5)\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n(x_2,y_2)=(5,-4)\n$$\n\nWe will write the equation of the line in the slope-intercept form\n\n$$\ny=mx+b\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the slope $m$ of the line, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm &= \\frac{y_{2} - y_{1}}{x_{2} - x_{1}} \\\\\n&= (-4 - 5)/(5 - (-4)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{-9}{9} \\\\\n&=-1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $m=-1$ into the slope-intercept form above, we get\n\n$$\ny =-x + b\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the $y$-intercept $b$. We can find this by substituting the coordinates of a point on the line. Any point will do, so let's substitute $(5,-4)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= -x + b \\\\\n- 4 &= -5 + b \\\\\n1 &= b \\\\\nb &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the line is\n\n$$\ny =-x + 1\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186152053 1074 "Notice that the trend line passes through the points $(x_1,y_1)=(-4,5)$ and $(x_2,y_2)=(5,-4).$\n\nWe will write the equation of the line in the slope-intercept form \n\n$$\ny=mx+b.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the slope $m$ of the line, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4-5}{5-(-4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-9}{9} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $m=-1$ into the slope-intercept form above, we get\n\n$$\ny = - x + b.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the $y$-intercept $b.$ We can find this by substituting the coordinates of a point on the line. Any point will do, so let's substitute $(5,-4)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-x+b \\\\ -4 & =-5+b \\\\ 1 & =b \\\\ b & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the line is \n\n$$\ny = - x + 1.\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186152054 1071 "![](images/ad/ad0deee6d845c0241108567ce6b0c744604f1f6f50d9af0405523cd9501a96a0.png)\n\nEstimate the equation of the trend line for the scatter plot above. Round both the slope and the $y$-intercept to the nearest $0.5$." 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186152054 1071 "![](images/ad/ad0deee6d845c0241108567ce6b0c744604f1f6f50d9af0405523cd9501a96a0.png)\n\nEstimate the equation of the trend line for the scatter plot above. Round both the slope and the $y$-intercept to the nearest $0.5.$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186152054 1074 "First, let's draw an approximate trend line for our scatter plot:\n\n![](images/72/722b5393babd1405c0330e9b4ed08c5030ddf6c69bd8b9e3f6c6d138f02c098c.png)\n\nIt seems that the trend line passes through\n\n$$\n(x_1,y_1)\\approx(4,4)\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n(x_2,y_2)\\approx(6,7)\n$$\n\nWe will write the equation of the line in the slope-intercept form\n\n$$\ny=mx+b\n$$\n\nFirst, we estimate the slope $m$ of the line as follows:\n\n$$\nm \\mid \\approx \\frac{y_{2} - y_{1}}{x_{2} - x_{1}}; = \\frac{7 - 4}{6 - 4}; = \\frac{3}{2}; = 1.5\n$$\n\nSubstituting $m=1.5$ into the slope-intercept form above, we get\n\n$$\ny = 1.5 x + b\n$$\n\nWe now need to estimate the $y$-intercept $b$. We can do this by substituting the coordinates of a point on the line. Any point will do, so let's substitute $(4,4)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= 1.5x + b \\\\\n4 &= 1.5(4) + b \\\\\n4 &= 6 + b \\\\\n- 2 &= b \\\\\nb &= -2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the line is\n\n$$\ny = 1.5x - 2\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186152054 1074 "First, let's draw an approximate trend line for our scatter plot:\n\n![](images/72/722b5393babd1405c0330e9b4ed08c5030ddf6c69bd8b9e3f6c6d138f02c098c.png)\n\nIt seems that the trend line passes through $(x_1,y_1)\\approx(4,4)$ and $(x_2,y_2)\\approx(6,7).$\n\nWe will write the equation of the line in the slope-intercept form \n\n$$\ny=mx+b.\n$$\n\nFirst, we estimate the slope $m$ of the line as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ≈\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{7-4}{6-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =1.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $m=1.5$ into the slope-intercept form above, we get\n\n$$\ny = 1.5 x + b.\n$$\n\nWe now need to estimate the $y$-intercept $b.$ We can do this by substituting the coordinates of a point on the line. Any point will do, so let's substitute $(4,4)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =1.5x+b \\\\ 4 & =1.5(4)+b \\\\ 4 & =6+b \\\\ -2 & =b \\\\ b & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the line is \n\n$$\ny = 1.5x - 2.\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186152055 1071 "A body of mass $2 \\, \\textrm{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F$, measured in Newtons.\nAt time $t$ seconds, the position of the body is given by $s(t) = e^{3t-6} + t^2$ meters. Find the expression for the force acting on the body at time $t$." 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186152055 1071 "A body of mass $2 \\, \\textrm{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons.\nAt time $t$ seconds, the position of the body is given by $s(t) = e^{3t-6} + t^2$ meters. Find the expression for the force acting on the body at time $t.$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186152055 1074 "According to Newton’s second law\n\n$$\nF=ma,\n$$\n\n \nwhere $F$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a$ is the acceleration.\n\nTo find the velocity $v(t),$ we differentiate the displacement $s(t){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{3t-6}+{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =3{e}^{3t-6}+2t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{e}^{3t-6}+2t) \\\\  & =9{e}^{3t-6}+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force acting on the body, in Newtons, is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =ma \\\\  & =2\\cdot (9{e}^{3t-6}+2) \\\\  & =18{e}^{3t-6}+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186152055 1074 "According to Newton’s second law\n$F=ma$,\nwhere $F$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a$ is the acceleration.\n\nTo find the velocity $v(t)$, we differentiate the displacement $s(t){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv(t) &= \\frac{ds}{dt} \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dt}(e^{3t - 6} + t^{2}) \\\\\n&= 3e^{3t - 6} + 2t\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t)$, we differentiate the velocity $v(t){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na(t) &= \\frac{dv}{dt} \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dt}(3e^{3t - 6} + 2t) \\\\\n&= 9e^{3t - 6} + 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force acting on the body, in Newtons, is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF &= ma \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot (9e^{3t - 6} + 2) \\\\\n&= 18e^{3t - 6} + 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186152056 1071 "A body of mass $2 \\, \\textrm{kg}$ moves in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F$, measured in Newtons.\nAt time $t\\geq 0$ seconds, the velocity of the body is given by $v(t) =2t^3-12t^2-30t+5$ meters per second.\nCalculate the time when the force acting on the body has a magnitude of zero." 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186152056 1071 "A body of mass $2 \\, \\textrm{kg}$ moves in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons.\nAt time $t\\geq 0$ seconds, the velocity of the body is given by $v(t) =2t^3-12t^2-30t+5$ meters per second.\nCalculate the time when the force acting on the body has a magnitude of zero." 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186152056 1074 "According to Newton’s second law\n\n$$\nF=ma,\n$$\n\n \nwhere $F$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a$ is the acceleration. \n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{3}-12{t}^{2}-30t+5) \\\\  & =6{t}^{2}-24t-30\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the mass is always positive, the force has zero magnitude when the acceleration equals zero. So, we set $a(t)=0$ and solve for $t{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{t}^{2}-24t-30 & =0 \\\\ {t}^{2}-4t-5 & =0 \\\\ (t+1)(t-5) & =0 \\\\ t & =-1,\\,5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $t \\geq 0,$ we disregard the negative solution. \n\nTherefore, the force has zero magnitude when $t=5 \\, \\textrm{s}.$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186152056 1074 "According to Newton’s second law\n$F=ma$,\nwhere $F$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a$ is the acceleration.\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t)$, we differentiate the velocity $v(t){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na(t) &= \\frac{dv}{dt} \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dt}(2t^{3} - 12t^{2} - 30t + 5) \\\\\n&= 6t^{2} - 24t - 30\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the mass is always positive, the force has zero magnitude when the acceleration equals zero. So, we set $a(t)=0$ and solve for $t{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6t^{2} - 24t - 30 &= 0 \\\\\nt^{2} - 4t - 5 &= 0 \\\\\n(t + 1)(t - 5) &= 0 \\\\\nt &= -1, 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\nt \\geq 0\n$$\n\nwe disregard the negative solution.\n\nTherefore, the force has zero magnitude when\n\n$$\nt=5 \\, \\textrm{s}\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186157282 1038 #edb/ref 17592186237895 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186157282 1038 #edb/ref 17592186237896 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186157282 1038 #edb/ref 17592186237897 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186157282 1038 #edb/ref 17592186237899 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186157283 1038 #edb/ref 17592186237892 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186157283 1038 #edb/ref 17592186237894 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186157292 1038 #edb/ref 17592186237883 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186157292 1038 #edb/ref 17592186237884 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186157292 1038 #edb/ref 17592186237885 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186157331 1038 #edb/ref 17592186237970 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186157331 1038 #edb/ref 17592186237971 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186157331 1038 #edb/ref 17592186237972 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186157331 1038 #edb/ref 17592186237973 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186157332 1038 #edb/ref 17592186237974 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186157332 1038 #edb/ref 17592186237975 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186157332 1038 #edb/ref 17592186237976 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186157430 1038 #edb/ref 17592186237933 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186157443 1038 #edb/ref 17592186237812 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186157443 1038 #edb/ref 17592186237813 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186157444 1038 #edb/ref 17592186237814 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186157546 1068 #edb/ref 17592186153490 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186157546 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237572 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186157546 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186157546 1071 "Find $f^{″}(1, 2)$ for the function $f(x, y)$ given that\n$f^{′}(x, y) = \\begin{bmatrix}y - 2xy & x - x^{2}\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186157546 1071 "Find ${f}^{″}(1,2)$ for the function $f(x,y)$ given that\n${f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}y-2xy & x-{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}]$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186157546 1074 "For a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R,$ given by\n\n$$\nf:{{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}⟶f({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that since ${f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}y-2xy & x-{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}],$ \n we must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=y-2xy,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x-{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y-2xy)=-2y\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x-{x}^{2})=1-2x \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y-2xy)=1-2x\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x-{x}^{2})=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}-2y & 1-2x \\\\ 1-2x & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate this matrix at $(1,2).$ This gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(1,2)=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186161911 1071 "![](images/bd/bdc283cb936da0169970929ff4f078cc4b1ad58ae124625655e7f8d3a0a15b24.png)\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $W,$ and the vector $\\mathbf{b}$ above. If the normal equation for the corresponding weighted least-squares problem $W\\!A \\mathbf{x} = W\\mathbf{b}$ is given by\n\n![](images/c1/c149ecd1fe76187859c289443581b4b2999aa2bd1e409723253a33bee56fb100.png)\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b+c?$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186161911 1071 "$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $W$, and the vector $\\mathbf{b}$ above. If the normal equation for the corresponding weighted least-squares problem $W\\!A \\mathbf{x} = W\\mathbf{b}$ is given by\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}a & 18 \\\\ 18 & b\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b+c$?" 1319413