\\cos (\\frac{θ}{2})}dθ \\\\\n&= 2\\sqrt{2}\\int_{0}^{π}\\sqrt{(\\sin (\\frac{θ}{2}) + \\cos (\\frac{θ}{2}))^{2}}dθ \\\\\n&= 2\\sqrt{2}\\int_{0}^{π} \\mid \\sin (\\frac{θ}{2}) + \\cos (\\frac{θ}{2}) \\mid dθ \\\\\n&= 4\\sqrt{2}[- \\cos (\\frac{θ}{2}) + \\sin (\\frac{θ}{2})]_{0}^{π} \\\\\n&= 4\\sqrt{2}(-\\cos (\\frac{π}{2}) + \\sin (\\frac{π}{2})) - (-\\cos (0) + \\sin (0)) \\\\\n&= 4\\sqrt{2}((0 + 1) - (-1 + 0)) \\\\\n&= 8\\sqrt{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186171902 1074 "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\,\\textrm d s = L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\textrm d s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm ds = \\sqrt{r^2 +\\left(\\frac{\\textrm{d}r}{\\textrm{d}\\theta}\\right)^2 }\\,\\textrm{d}\\theta.\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r = r(\\theta)$ for $\\theta_1 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\theta_2,$ we have\n\n$$\nL=\t\\int_C \\,\\textrm d s =  \\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2} \\sqrt{r^2 +\\left(\\frac{\\textrm{d}r}{\\textrm{d}\\theta}\\right)^2 }\\,\\textrm{d}\\theta.\n$$\n\nSince $r=2(1+\\sin\\theta),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}r}{\\textrm{d}\\theta} = 2\\cos{\\theta}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we evaluate our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{4(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}+4{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{2+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the following identities:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{2})+{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{2})=1,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRewriting our integral using the above identities, we can evaluate it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =2\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{2})+{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{2})+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{2})}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{2})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{2}))}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }|\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{2})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{2})|\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}{[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{2})+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{2})]}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}))-(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}((0+1)-(-1+0)) \\\\  & =8\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186171903 1071 "Which of the following regions are simply connected?\n\n- $\\{(x,y) \\in \\mathbb R^2 \\: | \\: y-x\\geq 0\\}$\n\n- $\\{(x,y) \\in \\mathbb R^2 \\: | \\: (x,y)\\neq (0,0)\\}$\n\n- $\\{(x,y)\\in \\mathbb R^2 \\: | \\: x^2+y^2> 4\\}$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186171903 1071 "Which of the following regions are simply connected?\n\n1. $\\{(x,y) \\in \\mathbb R^2 \\: \\mid \\: y-x\\geq 0\\}$\n2. $\\{(x,y) \\in \\mathbb R^2 \\: \\mid \\: (x,y)\\neq (0,0)\\}$\n3. $\\{(x,y)\\in \\mathbb R^2 \\: \\mid \\: x^2+y^2> 4\\}$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186171903 1074 "A connected region $D$ is simply connected if every simple closed curve in $D$ can be contracted to a point.\n\n![](images/7d/7d6aea1a62fb290702630ef95ec86f4119e7c8fdd74b8148b62b66e5ac871b58.png)\n\nA connected *plane* region $D$ is simply connected if every simple closed curve in $D$ encloses only points in $D$. In other words, the region does not contain any holes. Plane regions containing holes are not simply connected, since some simple closed curves cannot be contracted to a point:\n\n![](images/c3/c3c3e7a60ff0dd917cf6c0b293dae7510e457a0455629add1c54cadac65248b7.png)\n\nLet's now examine our regions in turn.\n\n- Region I is simply connected. Indeed, it is the half-plane $y \\geq x$. So, it's a plane region without holes.\n- Region II is *not* simply connected since it's a plane region with a hole at $(0,0)$.\n- Region III is *not* simply connected since it's a plane region with a hole. In this case, the hole contains all points on the disc $x^2+y^2 \\leq 4$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186171903 1074 "A connected region $D$ is simply connected if every simple closed curve in $D$ can be contracted to a point.\n\n![](images/7d/7d6aea1a62fb290702630ef95ec86f4119e7c8fdd74b8148b62b66e5ac871b58.png)\n\nA connected *plane* region $D$ is simply connected if every simple closed curve in $D$ encloses only points in $D.$ In other words, the region does not contain any holes. Plane regions containing holes are not simply connected, since some simple closed curves cannot be contracted to a point:\n\n![](images/c3/c3c3e7a60ff0dd917cf6c0b293dae7510e457a0455629add1c54cadac65248b7.png)\n\nLet's now examine our regions in turn.\n\n- Region I is simply connected. Indeed, it is the half-plane $y \\geq x.$ So, it's a plane region without holes.\n\n- Region II is *not*  simply connected since it's a plane region with a hole at $(0,0).$\n\n- Region III is *not* simply connected since it's a plane region with a hole. In this case, the hole contains all points on the disc $x^2+y^2 \\leq 4$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186171904 1071 "Which of the following are affine transformations?\n\n- ![](images/3a/3a24d08f3fce05b0a69b361d96cde0766860e0fd466a2622d780572334b2deba.png)\n\n- ![](images/d9/d910373a10dd1d08aed3df8b6b40102f4f1cc42e94e099ba6c74e6c5065828a1.png)\n\n- ![](images/05/05831230ea1993c0df5f2dae6d6411da094f181006980ea261a5b55fc9f21b40.png)" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186171904 1071 "Which of the following are affine transformations?\n\n1. $\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}$\n2. $\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}$\n3. $\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186171904 1074 "An affine transformation $\\mathbf T:\\mathbb R^2\\rightarrow\\mathbb R^2$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf x = A \\mathbf u + \\mathbf b\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf x, \\mathbf u$, and $\\mathbf b$ are column vectors in $\\mathbb R^2$, and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nWith this in mind, let's look at each transformation in turn.\n\n- Transformation $\\mathbf{T}_1$ is affine. Notice that $\\mathbf{T}_1$ can be expressed as\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}|= \\begin{bmatrix}u + v \\\\ v - 2u + 1\\end{bmatrix}; = \\begin{bmatrix}u + v \\\\ -2u + v\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}; = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}_(⏟)_(A) \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}_(⏟)_(u) + \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}_(⏟)_(b)$.\n- Transformation $\\mathbf{T}_2$ is affine. Notice that $\\mathbf{T}_2$ can be expressed as\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}|= \\begin{bmatrix}u - v \\\\ v - u\\end{bmatrix}; = \\begin{bmatrix}u - v \\\\ -u + v\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}; = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}_(⏟)_(A) \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}_(⏟)_(u) + \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}_(⏟)_(b)$.\nIn fact, the transformation $\\mathbf{T}_2$ is a linear transformation.\n- Transformation $\\mathbf{T}_3$ is not affine since it contains the quadratic term $v^2$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$\\mathbf{T}_1$ and $\\mathbf{T}_2$ only.\"" 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186171904 1074 "An affine transformation $\\mathbf T:\\mathbb R^2\\rightarrow\\mathbb R^2$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf x = A \\mathbf u + \\mathbf b,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf x, \\mathbf u,$ and $\\mathbf b$ are column vectors in $\\mathbb R^2,$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nWith this in mind, let's look at each transformation in turn.\n\n- Transformation $\\mathbf{T}_1$ is affine. Notice that $\\mathbf{T}_1$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}u+v \\\\ v-2u+1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}u+v \\\\ -2u+v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Transformation $\\mathbf{T}_2$ is affine. Notice that $\\mathbf{T}_2$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}u-v \\\\ v-u\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}u-v \\\\ -u+v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn fact, the transformation $\\mathbf{T}_2$ is a linear transformation.\n\n- Transformation $\\mathbf{T}_3$ is not affine since it contains the quadratic term $v^2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$\\mathbf{T}_1$ and $\\mathbf{T}_2$ only.\"" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186171905 1071 "The matrix form of the affine transformation\n\n$$\nx = 5u + v - 6, \\qquad y = 2u + 4v\n$$\n\nis shown below. What is the value of $a+b+c?$\n![](images/f0/f017ccc60a7c1bb9ac09978492c9db217836099d0926ee009ffc44ba827c25d5.png)" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186171905 1071 "The matrix form of the affine transformation\n$x = 5u + v - 6, \\qquad y = 2u + 4v$\nis shown below. What is the value of $a+b+c$?\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186171905 1074 "An affine transformation $\\mathbf T:\\mathbb R^2\\rightarrow\\mathbb R^2$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf x = A \\mathbf u + \\mathbf b\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf x, \\mathbf u$, and $\\mathbf b$ are column vectors in $\\mathbb R^2$, and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nFirst, notice that we can express the given transformation as\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nSeparating the constants and expressing the linear part as a product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}5u + v - 6 \\\\ 2u + 4v\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}5u + v \\\\ 2u + 4v\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}5 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}_(⏟)_(A) \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}_(⏟)_(u) + \\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}_(⏟)_(b)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c = 1+2+0 = 3\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186171905 1074 "An affine transformation $\\mathbf T:\\mathbb R^2\\rightarrow\\mathbb R^2$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf x = A \\mathbf u + \\mathbf b,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf x, \\mathbf u,$ and $\\mathbf b$ are column vectors in $\\mathbb R^2,$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nFirst, notice that we can express the given transformation as\n![](images/51/514520062cf4564f58267774ba6b50027f9cd27dedfa392cb7038f94580b1e4a.png)\n\nSeparating the constants and expressing the linear part as a product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}5u+v-6 \\\\ 2u+4v\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5u+v \\\\ 2u+4v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={{[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{A}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{u}+{{[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c = 1+2+0 = 3.\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186171906 1071 "The matrix form of the affine transformation\n\n$$\nx = 3u-v + \\bbox[Gainsboro,2pt]{a}, \\qquad y = u + \\bbox[Gainsboro,2pt]{b}v + \\bbox[Gainsboro,2pt]{c}\n$$\n\nis shown below. What is the value of $a+b+c?$\n![](images/43/43bcde1806155410ba97b0b2e5807d4a0f9cee93ef04f2ccfb413552de74178b.png)" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186171906 1071 "The matrix form of the affine transformation\n$x = 3u-v + \\bbox[Gainsboro,2pt]{a}, \\qquad y = u + \\bbox[Gainsboro,2pt]{b}v + \\bbox[Gainsboro,2pt]{c}$\nis shown below. What is the value of $a+b+c$?\n$\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186171906 1074 "An affine transformation $\\mathbf T:\\mathbb R^2\\rightarrow\\mathbb R^2$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf x = A \\mathbf u + \\mathbf b\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf x, \\mathbf u$, and $\\mathbf b$ are column vectors in $\\mathbb R^2$, and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nWe can carry out the matrix operations using the matrix form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -5\\end{bmatrix}^(⏞)^(A) \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}^(⏞)^(u) + \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}^(⏞)^(b) \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}3u - v \\\\ u - 5v\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}3u - v + 1 \\\\ u - 5v\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding elements, we obtain\n\n$$\nx = 3u - v + \\bbox[Gainsboro,2pt]{1}, \\qquad y = u + (\\bbox[Gainsboro,2pt]{-5})v + \\bbox[Gainsboro,2pt]{0} \\,\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c = 1+(-5)+0 = -4\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186171906 1074 "An affine transformation $\\mathbf T:\\mathbb R^2\\rightarrow\\mathbb R^2$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf x = A \\mathbf u + \\mathbf b,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf x, \\mathbf u,$ and $\\mathbf b$ are column vectors in $\\mathbb R^2,$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nWe can carry out the matrix operations using the matrix form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}] & =\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -5\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot \\overset{u}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]}}+\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3u-v \\\\ u-5v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3u-v+1 \\\\ u-5v\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding elements, we obtain\n\n$$\nx = 3u - v + \\bbox[Gainsboro,2pt]{1}, \\qquad y = u + (\\bbox[Gainsboro,2pt]{-5})v + \\bbox[Gainsboro,2pt]{0} \\, .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+b+c = 1+(-5)+0 = -4.\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186171909 1071 "Consider the scalar field $f(x,y,z) = x^2 + y^2 + z^2$. Calculate $\\displaystyle{\\displaystyle \\int_C \\nabla f \\cdot \\textrm d\\mathbf r}$, where $C$ is any smooth path from $(-2, 1, 0)$ to $(-1, 0, -1)$ in $\\mathbb R^3$." 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186171909 1071 "Consider the scalar field $f(x,y,z) = x^2 + y^2 + z^2.$ Calculate $\\displaystyle{\\int_C \\nabla f \\cdot \\textrm d\\mathbf r},$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(-2, 1, 0)$ to $(-1, 0, -1)$ in $\\mathbb R^3.$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186171909 1074 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $\\mathbf r(t)$ for $t \\in [a,b]$. Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $\\nabla f$ is continuous on $C$. The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that\n\n$$\n\\int_{C} \\nabla f \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf r = f(\\mathbf r(b)) - f(\\mathbf r(a))\n$$\n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B$, and is independent of the path between them.\n\nIn our case, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path $C$ are $A(-2, 1, 0)$ and $B(-1, 0, -1)$.\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{C}∇f \\cdot dr &= f(-1, 0,-1) - f(-2, 1, 0) \\\\\n&= ((-1)^{2} + 0^{2} + (-1)^{2}) - ((-2)^{2} + 1^{2} + 0^{2}) \\\\\n&= 2 - 5 \\\\\n&=-3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186171909 1074 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $\\mathbf r(t)$ for $t \\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $\\nabla f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n\\int_{C} \\nabla f \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf r = f(\\mathbf r(b)) - f(\\mathbf r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nIn our case, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path $C$ are $A(-2, 1, 0)$ and $B(-1, 0, -1).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(-1,0,-1)-f(-2,1,0) \\\\  & =((-1)^{2}+{0}^{2}+(-1)^{2})-((-2)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}) \\\\  & =2-5 \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186171910 1071 "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf F(x, y) = -2x\\,\\mathbf i + 6y^2\\,\\mathbf j$. Calculate $\\displa