 surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r,\\theta ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2X,Y,Z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,Z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,r⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $r\\in [0,\\infty )$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $f$ is $2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186175884 1071 "![](images/e1/e12a47b603d2c4193453d0f4daf18f54e52ed973498f520feef32cfafbe28a9f.png)\n\n  \n\nEvaluate ![](images/69/696a0cf0c1864bfb945b4321a81525094319c9aa23491929e11dcd2b4bf2e842.png) where the path ![](images/41/41f045554bfb2c134d481db3d019376c7427e136ebd890f5537789d0c04be67d.png) is shown above." 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186175884 1071 "![](images/e1/e12a47b603d2c4193453d0f4daf18f54e52ed973498f520feef32cfafbe28a9f.png)\n\nEvaluate $\\displaystyle\\oint\\limits_{C} \\left(e^{-x^2}-y^3\\right) \\mathrm{d}x + (e^{-y^2}+x^3) \\: \\mathrm{d}y$, where the path $C$ is shown above." 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186175884 1074 "Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C} P\\,\\mathrm{d}x + Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y = \\iint\\limits_D \\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\textrm d A\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem.\n\n![](images/67/6706cc6b412a98761f0f3cd29e584f1705fa8a708a926b805a8b5702a4f508e8.png)\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nP(x,y) = e^{-x^2}-y^3, \\qquad Q(x,y) = e^{-y^2}+x^3\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y} =-3y^{2}, \\frac{∂Q}{∂x} = 3x^{2}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∮_(C)(e^{-x^{2}} - y^{3})dx + (e^{-y^{2}} + x^{3})dy &= ∬_(D)(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} - \\frac{∂P}{∂y})dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)(3x^{2} - (-3y^{2}))dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)3(x^{2} + y^{2})dA\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\Big\\{(r,\\theta) \\:: \\: 0 \\leq r \\leq 2, \\: 0 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\pi \\Big\\}\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d A = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(r \\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta) \\: r \\ \\mathrm d r \\mathrm d \\theta\n$$\n\nFinally, we can calculate our double integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(D)(3x^{2} + y^{2})dA &= ∬_(Δ)3(r^{2}\\cos^{2} θ + r^{2}\\sin^{2} θ)rdrdθ \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{π}\\int_{0}^{2}3r^{3}(\\cos^{2} θ + \\sin^{2} θ)drdθ \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{π}\\int_{0}^{2}3r^{3}drdθ \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{π}[\\frac{3r^{4}}{4}]_{0}^{2}dθ \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{π}12dθ \\\\\n&= 12[θ]_{0}^{π} \\\\\n&= 12π\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186175884 1074 "Green's theorem states that\n![](images/84/84bdabd8bc9134d577c789d22aa75d8df2b13646db10e6af742a9eba2bd0c6eb.png)\n\nIn our case, the region ![](images/28/28390f00cdb34a228868c36035ff66f1ea65bf7ff5e592fa473fca18d6addbcc.png) and its boundary ![](images/54/54c5a03048971806da4fc3eb5f51c9fdefb00abbb4a54b7bf9b94ba81ab0df37.png) satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem.\n\n![](images/67/6706cc6b412a98761f0f3cd29e584f1705fa8a708a926b805a8b5702a4f508e8.png)\n\nNow, since\n![](images/13/133135fbf95e74d572c8def38a8aea6c0ef3b2e2b569a197a9c48cccd906ef44.png)\nwe obtain\n\n![](images/25/253fa3e9cb25a6b92237374e3fbc51f7e3c10461a183c789cdb37a4c4b8f560e.png)\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n![](images/cc/cce6516260d79ee10d0343f341da2b8246ca12c5e9a8ee119d8a25a29476a281.png)\n\nNow, notice that the region ![](images/05/05ff793b0aee1f05a79216f9e7f119afa544749c81077937f8f06a353f6ef4eb.png) can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n![](images/5b/5b07149309edcf9c83af0282b7b8e34942ed3e6879d61f549317357d05748a45.png)\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n![](images/53/530d94860e23561ee4f296c5007cbe30a1d22028deb60fc11cb87cf5d3640497.png)\n\nFinally, we can calculate our double integral, as follows:\n\n![](images/c8/c8094d11eab9d06ae1997a2168ec694066f59ea16aa1ec0c4788978012e62022.png)" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186175885 1071 "Given that\n![](images/5b/5bf7df5fffeecf9bdaa888ebe196a194a6b6e24aa69c01ea74ee2aac1a3f4774.png)\nwhere $\\mathbf{F}(x,y) = \\left\\langle y^2+\\sin x, \\: 3xy-\\cos y  \\right\\rangle$ and $C$ is the circle $x^2+y^2=4$ oriented counterclockwise, what is the missing part of the equation?" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186175885 1071 "Given that\n$AAAAAAAA$\nwhere $\\mathbf{F}(x,y) = \\left\\langle y^2+\\sin x, \\: 3xy-\\cos y \\right\\rangle$ and $C$ is the circle $x^2+y^2=4$ oriented counterclockwise, what is the missing part of the equation?" 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186175885 1074 "First, notice that we can rewrite our integral as\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\oint\\limits_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}} = \\oint\\limits_{C} (y^2+\\sin x) \\: \\mathrm{d}x + (3xy-\\cos y) \\: \\mathrm{d}y\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C} P\\,\\mathrm{d}x + Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y = \\iint\\limits_D \\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\textrm d A\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/79/798665574b8f0e11d991bb0d934efd01bdeb65840c2e86498be4edbc59578439.png)\n\nSince\n\n$$\nP(x,y)= y^2+\\sin x\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nQ(x,y)= 3xy-\\cos y\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y} = 2y, \\frac{∂Q}{∂x} = 3y\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∮_(C)(y^{2} + \\sin x)dx + (xy - \\cos y)dy &= ∬_(D)(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} - \\frac{∂P}{∂y})dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)(3y - 2y)dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)ydA\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\big\\{(r,\\theta) \\:: \\: 0\\leq r \\leq 2,\\: 0 \\leq \\theta \\leq 2\\pi \\big\\}\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d A = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(r \\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta) \\: r \\ \\mathrm d r \\mathrm d \\theta\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(D)ydA &= ∬_(Δ)r\\sin θrdrdθ \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{2π}\\int_{0}^{2}r 2 sin ⁡ θdrdθ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the missing expression is $r^2\\sin\\theta$." 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186175885 1074 "First, notice that we can rewrite our integral as \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\oint\\limits_{C}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{r}}\n=\n\\oint\\limits_{C} (y^2+\\sin x) \\: \\mathrm{d}x + (3xy-\\cos y) \\: \\mathrm{d}y.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C} P\\,\\mathrm{d}x + Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y \n= \\iint\\limits_D \\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\textrm d A.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/79/798665574b8f0e11d991bb0d934efd01bdeb65840c2e86498be4edbc59578439.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)= y^2+\\sin x$ and $Q(x,y)= 3xy-\\cos y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=3y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}({y}^{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\,dx+(xy-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(3y-2y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\big\\{ (r,\\theta) \\: : \\: 0\\leq r \\leq 2,\\: 0 \\leq \\theta \\leq 2\\pi  \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d A = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(r \\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta) \\:  r \\ \\mathrm d r \\mathrm d \\theta.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}y\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the missing expression is $r^2\\sin\\theta.$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186175891 1074 "Speed is a special unit rate applied to moving objects. It is defined as *the distance traveled per unit of time*:\n\n$$\n\\text{speed} = \\dfrac{\\text{distance}}{\\text{time}}\n$$\n\nTo find the speed in feet per second, we divide the number of feet, $998$, by the number of seconds, $10$. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{998}{10} &= 99.8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the speed of the cheetah is $99.8$ feet per second." 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186175891 1074 "Speed is a special unit rate applied to moving objects. It is defined as *the distance traveled per unit of time*:\n\n$$\n\\text{speed} = \\dfrac{\\text{distance}}{\\text{time}}\n$$\n\nTo find the speed in feet per second, we divide the number of feet, $998,$ by the number of seconds, $10.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{998}{10} & =99.8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the speed of the cheetah is $99.8$ feet per second." 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186175892 1074 "Speed is a special unit rate applied to moving objects. It is defined as *the distance traveled per unit of time*:\n\n$$\n\\text{speed} = \\dfrac{\\text{distance}}{\\text{time}}\n$$\n\nTo find the speed, in centimeters per second, we divide the number of centimeters, $200$, by the number of seconds, $8$. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{200}{8} &= 200 \\div 8 \\\\\n&= 25\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the speed of the fish is $25$ centimeters per second.\n\nTo find the distance covered in $3$ seconds, we multiply the speed by $3$. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ndistance &= speed \\cdot time \\\\\n&= 25 \\cdot 3 \\\\\n&= 75\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fish can cover $75$ centimeters in $3$ seconds." 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186175892 1074 "Speed is a special unit rate applied to moving objects. It is defined as *the distance traveled per unit of time*:\n\n$$\n\\text{speed} = \\dfrac{\\text{distance}}{\\text{time}}\n$$\n\nTo find the speed, in centimeters per second, we divide the number of centimeters, $200,$ by the number of seconds, $8.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{200}{8} & =200÷8 \\\\  & =25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the speed of the fish is $25$ centimeters per second. \n\nTo find the distance covered in $3$ seconds, we multiply the speed by $3.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{distance} & =\\text{speed}\\times \\text{time} \\\\  & =25\\times 3 \\\\  & =75.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fish can cover $75$ centimeters in $3$ seconds." 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186175893 1074 "Speed is a special unit rate applied to moving objects. It is defined as *the distance traveled per unit of time*:\n\n$$\n\\text{speed} = \\dfrac{\\text{distance}}{\\text{time}}\n$$\n\nTo find the speed, in millions of kilometers per day, we divide the number of million kilometers, $13$, by the number of days, $5$. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{13}{5} &= 13 \\div 5 \\\\\n&= 2.6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the speed of the Earth is $2.6$ million kilometers per day.\n\nTo find the time it takes the Earth to cover $52$ million kilometers, we divide the distance by $2.6$. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ntime &= \\frac{distance}{speed} \\\\\n&= \\frac{52}{2.6} \\\\\n&= \\frac{52 \\cdot 10}{2.6 \\cdot 10} \\\\\n&= \\frac{520}{26} \\\\\n&= 520 \\div 26 \\\\\n&= 20\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, it takes the Earth $20$ days to travel $52$ million kilometers around the Sun." 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186175893 1074 "Speed is a special unit rate applied to moving objects. I