f $\\displaystyle\\int_{C} f(x,y) \\,\\textrm d x= 8$ and $\\displaystyle\\int_{-C_1} f(x,y) \\,\\textrm d x= -2,$ calculate $\\displaystyle\\int_{-C_2} f(x,y) \\,\\textrm d x$ given that $C = C_1\\cup C_2.$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186179601 1074 "Let\n\n$$\nC = C_1\\cup C_2\n$$\n\nbe a piecewise smooth path in the $xy$-plane, and let $-C$ be the curve consisting of the same points as $C$ but with the opposite orientation.\n\nFor line integrals with respect to $x$, we have the following properties:\n\n- $\\displaystyle\\int_{-C} f(x,y)\\,\\textrm d x = -\\displaystyle \\int_{C} f(x,y)\\,\\textrm d x$\n- $\\displaystyle\\int_{C} f(x,y)\\,\\textrm d x = \\displaystyle \\int_{C_1} f(x,y)\\,\\textrm d x + \\displaystyle \\int_{C_2} f(x,y)\\,\\textrm d x$\n\nIn our case, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{C}f(x, y)dx &= \\int_{C_{1}}f(x, y)dx + \\int_{C_{2}}f(x, y)dx \\\\\n8 &= -\\int_{-C_{1}}f(x, y)dx - \\int_{-C_{2}}f(x, y)dx \\\\\n8 &= - (-2) - \\int_{-C_{2}}f(x, y)dx \\\\\n8 &= 2 - \\int_{-C_{2}}f(x, y)dx \\\\\n6 &= -\\int_{-C_{2}}f(x, y)dx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\int_{-C_2} f(x,y) \\,\\textrm d x = -6\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186179601 1074 "Let $C = C_1\\cup C_2$ be a piecewise smooth path in the $xy$-plane, and let $-C$ be the curve consisting of the same points as $C$ but with the opposite orientation. \n\nFor line integrals with respect to $x,$ we have the following properties:\n\n- $\\displaystyle\\int_{-C} f(x,y)\\,\\textrm d x = -\\int_{C} f(x,y)\\,\\textrm d x$\n\n- $\\displaystyle\\int_{C} f(x,y)\\,\\textrm d x = \\int_{C_1} f(x,y)\\,\\textrm d x + \\int_{C_2} f(x,y)\\,\\textrm d x$\n\nIn our case, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ 8 & =-{\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{-{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ 8 & =-(-2)-{\\int }_{-{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ 8 & =2-{\\int }_{-{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ 6 & =-{\\int }_{-{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\int_{-C_2} f(x,y) \\,\\textrm d x = -6.\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186179602 1071 "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int\\limits_C y\\,\\mathrm{d}x -x\\,\\mathrm{d}y ,$ where $C$ is a portion of the ellipse parametrized by $\\mathbf{r}(t)=\\cos t\\,\\mathbf{i} + 3\\sin t\\,\\mathbf{j}$ for $t\\in[0,\\pi].$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186179602 1071 "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int\\limits_C y\\,\\mathrm{d}x -x\\,\\mathrm{d}y$, where $C$ is a portion of the ellipse parametrized by $\\mathbf{r}(t)=\\cos t\\,\\mathbf{i} + 3\\sin t\\,\\mathbf{j}$ for $t\\in[0,\\pi]$." 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186179602 1074 "Along the curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t) = \\cos t, \\qquad\ny(t) = 3\\sin t.\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $\\mathbf r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}y\\,dx-x\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }y\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt-x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(y\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }-3{sin}^{2}⁡t-3{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,dt \\\\  & =-3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,dt \\\\  & =-3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\,dt \\\\  & =-3{t|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =-3\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186179602 1074 "Along the curve, we have\n\n$$\nx(t) = \\cos t, \\qquad y(t) = 3\\sin t\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $\\mathbf r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(t) &= \\frac{dx}{dt}i + \\frac{dy}{dt}j \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dt}(\\cos t)i + \\frac{d}{dt}(3\\sin t)j \\\\\n&=-\\sin ti + 3\\cos tj\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the line integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_(C)ydx - xdy &= \\int_{0}^{π}y \\cdot \\frac{dx}{dt}dt - x \\cdot \\frac{dy}{dt}dt \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{π}(y \\cdot \\frac{dx}{dt} - x \\cdot \\frac{dy}{dt})dt \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{π}3\\sin t \\cdot (-\\sin t) - (\\cos t) \\cdot 3\\cos tdt \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{π} - 3\\sin^{2} t - 3\\cos^{2} tdt \\\\\n&=-3\\int_{0}^{π}\\sin^{2} t + \\cos^{2} tdt \\\\\n&=-3\\int_{0}^{π}dt \\\\\n&= \\left.- 3t\\right|_{0}^{π} \\\\\n&=-3π\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186179603 1071 "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\oint\\limits_{C} \\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}) \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r}$, where $\\mathbf F(x, y) = y\\,\\mathbf{i} + x\\,\\mathbf{j}$ and $C$ is a circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin, traversed once in the counterclockwise direction." 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186179603 1071 "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\oint\\limits_{C} \\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}) \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r},$ where $\\mathbf F(x, y) = y\\,\\mathbf{i} + x\\,\\mathbf{j}$ and $C$ is a circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin, traversed once in the counterclockwise direction." 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186179603 1074 "The notation $\\displaystyle\\oint$ is used to remind us that we're dealing with a line integral over a *closed* curve.\n\nWe will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r} = \\int_a^b \\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\,\\textrm{d}t\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to parameterize a circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin. It can be parameterized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\cos{t}\\,\\mathbf{i} + \\sin{t}\\,\\mathbf{j}, \\qquad 0 \\leq t \\lt 2\\pi\n$$\n\nSo, along the curve $C$, we have\n\n$$\nx = \\cos{t}, \\qquad y = \\sin{t}\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(r(t)) &= yi + xj = \\sin ti + \\cos tj\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(t) &= \\frac{dx}{dt}i + \\frac{dy}{dt}j \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dt}(\\cos t)i + \\frac{d}{dt}(\\sin t)j \\\\\n&=-\\sin ti + \\cos tj\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(r(t)) \\cdot r^{′}(t) &= (\\sin ti + \\cos tj) \\cdot (-\\sin ti + \\cos tj) \\\\\n&=-\\sin^{2} t + \\cos^{2} t \\\\\n&= - (1 - \\cos^{2} t) + \\cos^{2} t \\\\\n&= 2\\cos^{2} t - 1 \\\\\n&= \\cos 2t\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∮_(C)F(r) \\cdot dr &= \\int_{0}^{2π}F(r(t)) \\cdot r^{′}(t)dt \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{2π}\\cos 2tdt \\\\\n&= [\\frac{1}{2}\\sin 2t]_{0}^{2π} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\\sin 4π - \\frac{1}{2}\\sin (0) \\\\\n&= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186179603 1074 "The notation $\\displaystyle\\oint$ is used to remind us that we're dealing with a line integral over a *closed* curve.\n\nWe will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r} = \\int_a^b \\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\,\\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to parameterize a circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin. It can be parameterized as \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\cos{t}\\,\\mathbf{i} + \\sin{t}\\,\\mathbf{j},\n\\qquad 0 \\leq t \\lt 2\\pi.\n$$\n\nSo, along the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx = \\cos{t}, \\qquad\ny = \\sin{t}.\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =y\\,i+x\\,j=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j) \\\\  & =-{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =-(1-{cos}^{2}⁡t)+{cos}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =2{cos}^{2}⁡t-1 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F(r)\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,dt \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t]}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\pi -\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186179604 1071 "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int\\limits_{C} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r}$, where $\\mathbf F(x, y) = y\\,\\mathbf{i} + x\\,\\mathbf{j}$ and $C$ is the curve $y^3 = x-5$ traversed from the point $A(5,0)$ to the point $B(6,1)$." 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186179604 1071 "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int\\limits_{C} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r},$ where $\\mathbf F(x, y) = y\\,\\mathbf{i} + x\\,\\mathbf{j}$ and $C$ is the curve $y^3 = x-5$ traversed from the point $A(5,0)$ to the point $B(6,1).$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186179604 1074 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r} = \\int_a^b \\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\,\\textrm{d}t\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to parameterize the curve. Making $x$ the subject of the equation, we have\n\n$$\ny^3 = x-5 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad x = y^3 + 5\n$$\n\nSo, we can parametrize the curve using\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr(t) &= (t^{3} + 5)i + tj, 0 \\le t \\le 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, along the curve $C$, we have\n\n$$\nx = t^3+5, \\qquad y = t\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(r(t)) &= yi + xj \\\\\n&= ti + (t^{3} + 5)j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(t) &= \\frac{dx}{dt}i + \\frac{dy}{dt}j \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dt}(t^{3} + 5)i + \\frac{d}{dt}(t)j \\\\\n&= 3t^{2}i + j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(r(t)) \\cdot r^{′}(t) &= (ti + (t^{3} + 5)j) \\cdot (3t^{2}i + j) \\\\\n&= t \\cdot 3t^{2} + (t^{3} + 5) \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= 3t^{3} + t^{3} + 5 \\\\\n&= 4t^{3} + 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_(C)F \\cdot dr &= \\int_{0}^{1}F(r(t)) \\cdot r^{′}(t)dt \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}4t^{3} + 5dt \\\\\n&= [t^{4} + 5t]_{0}^{1} \\\\\n&= 1 + 5 - 0 \\\\\n&= 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186179604 1074 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r} = \\int_a^b \\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\,\\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to parameterize the curve. Making $x$ the subject of the equation, we have\n\n$$\ny^3 = x-5\n\\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad\nx = y^3 + 5.\n$$\n\nSo, we can parametrize the curve using\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =({t}^{3}+5)\\,i+t\\,j,\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, along the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx = t^3+5, \\qquad\ny = t.\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =y\\,i+x\\,j \\\\  & =t\\,i+({t}^{3}+5)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}+5)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =3{t}^{2}\\,i+j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(t\\,i+({t}^{3}+5)\\,j)\\cdot (3{t}^{2}\\,i+j) \\\\  & =t\\cdot 3{t}^{2}+({t}^{3}+5)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =3{t}^{3}+{t}^{3}+5 \\\\  & =4{t}^{3}+5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}4{t}^{3}+5\\,dt \\\\  & ={[{t}^{4}+5t]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =1+5-0 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186179605 1071 "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int\\limits_{C} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r}$, where $\\mathbf F(x, y) =(1-y)^2\\,\\mathbf{i} + x\\,\\mathbf{j}$ and $C$ is the line segment traversed from the point $A(0, 2)$ to the point $B(1,0)$." 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186179605 1071 "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int\\limits_{C} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r},$ where $\\mathbf F(x, y) =(1-y)^2\\,\\mathbf{i} + x\\,\\mathbf{j}$ and $C$ is the line segment traversed from the point $A(0, 2)$ to the point $B(1,0).$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186179605 1074 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r} = \\int_a^b \\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\,\\textrm{d}t\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to parameterize the line segment.\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a} = 2\\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{b} = \\mathbf{i}\n$$\n\nSo, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr(t) &= a + t(b - a) \\\\\n&= 2j + t[i - 2j] \\\\\n&= ti + (2 - 2t)j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ varies from $t=0$ (at the point $A$) to $t = 1$ (at the point $B$).\nThus, along the curve $C$, we have\n\n$$\nx = t, \\qquad y = 2-2t\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(r(t)) &= (1 - y)^{2}i + xj \\\\\n&= (1 - (2 - 2t))^{2}i + tj \\\\\n&= (2t - 1)^{2}i + tj \\\\\n&= (4t^{2} - 4t + 1)i + tj\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(t) &= \\frac{dx}{dt}i + \\frac{dy}{dt}j \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dt}(t)i + \\frac{d}{dt}(2