e the force $\\mathbf F$ acts perpendicular to the motion of the particle as it moves around $C,$ it neither helps nor hinders its motion. \n\n![](images/06/063836a4780ecb4be6e8c6149a422e3c8e9f2770304e659a8116691b91e3d7f1.png)\n\nSo, $\\displaystyle\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r = 0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. If we reverse the orientation of $C,$ then the situation with regards to the work done by $\\mathbf F$ remains unchanged.\n\n![](images/70/704915d2852e1654aa31d17bd2dfb07a3022fe3d8b597f37bf98bd5af2de1d1b.png)\n\nSo, $\\displaystyle\\oint_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r = 0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186179609 1071 "Calculate the circulation $\\Gamma$ of the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y) = -y\\,\\mathbf{i} +x\\,\\mathbf{j}$ along the circle $C$ with equation $x^2 + y^2 = 1$, oriented counterclockwise." 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186179609 1071 "Calculate the circulation $\\Gamma$ of the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y) = -y\\,\\mathbf{i} +x\\,\\mathbf{j}$ along the circle $C$ with equation $x^2 + y^2 = 1,$ oriented counterclockwise." 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186179609 1074 "The closed curve $C$ is the unit circle. It can be parameterized in the counterclockwise direction as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\cos{t}\\,\\mathbf{i} + \\sin{t}\\,\\mathbf{j},\n\\qquad 0 \\leq t \\lt 2\\pi.\n$$\n\nThe circulation $\\Gamma$ measures the total work done by a force $\\mathbf F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\Gamma = \\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\textrm{d} \\mathbf r\n= \\int_a^b \\mathbf F(r(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf r'(t) \\; \\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx = \\cos t, \\qquad\ny = \\sin t.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))=-y\\,i+x\\,j=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf r'(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j) \\\\  & ={sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Γ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186179609 1074 "The closed curve $C$ is the unit circle. It can be parameterized in the counterclockwise direction as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\cos{t}\\,\\mathbf{i} + \\sin{t}\\,\\mathbf{j}, \\qquad 0 \\leq t \\lt 2\\pi\n$$\n\nThe circulation $\\Gamma$ measures the total work done by a force $\\mathbf F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C$.\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\Gamma = \\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\textrm{d} \\mathbf r = \\int_a^b \\mathbf F(r(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf r'(t) \\; \\textrm{d}t\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C$, we have\n\n$$\nx = \\cos t, \\qquad y = \\sin t\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\nF(r(t)) =-yi + xj =-\\sin ti + \\cos tj\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf r'(t)$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(t) &= \\frac{d}{dt}(\\cos t)i + \\frac{d}{dt}(\\sin t)j \\\\\n&=-\\sin ti + \\cos tj\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(r(t)) \\cdot r^{′}(t) &= (-\\sin ti + \\cos tj) \\cdot (-\\sin ti + \\cos tj) \\\\\n&= \\sin^{2} t + \\cos^{2} t \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nΓ = ∮_(C)F \\cdot dr &= \\int_{0}^{2π}F(r(t)) \\cdot r^{′}(t)dt \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{2π}dt \\\\\n&= 2π\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186179610 1071 "![](images/29/298b7b4293ff104a62107670fa2aba603b8a23e9ad042f7e270a4acf6267a2fa.png)\n\n \n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y)$ shown above, and let $C$ be the path where the curve is traversed from $A$ to $B.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\displaystyle\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r <  0$\n\n- $\\displaystyle\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r =         0$\n\n- $\\displaystyle\\int_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r >  0$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186179610 1071 "![](images/29/298b7b4293ff104a62107670fa2aba603b8a23e9ad042f7e270a4acf6267a2fa.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y)$ shown above, and let $C$ be the path where the curve is traversed from $A$ to $B$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $\\displaystyle\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r < 0$\n2. $\\displaystyle\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r = 0$\n3. $\\displaystyle\\int_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r > 0$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186179610 1074 "Remember that $\\displaystyle\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r$ gives the total work done by a force $\\mathbf F$ in moving a particle along $C$:\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then $\\displaystyle\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r > 0$.\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then $\\displaystyle\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r < 0$.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. Since the force $\\mathbf F$ acts perpendicular to the motion of the particle as it moves from $A$ to $B$, it neither helps nor hinders its motion.![](images/cd/cd71f379a02405bc21aecfb0bb14e9798cfbc4d7fd2c1d3a1d5221aa8d64638f.png)\nSo, $\\displaystyle\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r = 0$.\n\n- Statement III is false. If we reverse the orientation of $C$, then the situation with regards to the work done by $\\mathbf F$ is similar to the previous case.![](images/b2/b2284e7ffa962fd46333c4eca47a77473007fdd96e0813b9928f0303df8170f3.png)\nSo, $\\displaystyle\\int_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r = 0$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186179610 1074 "Remember that $\\displaystyle\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r$ gives the total work done by a force $\\mathbf F$ in moving a particle along $C\\mathbin{:}$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then $\\displaystyle\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r > 0.$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion  of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then $\\displaystyle\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r <                     0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. Since the force $\\mathbf F$ acts perpendicular to the motion of the particle as it moves from $A$ to $B,$ it neither helps nor hinders its motion. \n\n![](images/cd/cd71f379a02405bc21aecfb0bb14e9798cfbc4d7fd2c1d3a1d5221aa8d64638f.png)\n\n \nSo, $\\displaystyle\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r = 0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. If we reverse the orientation of $C,$ then the situation with regards to the work done by $\\mathbf F$ is similar to the previous case.\n\n![](images/b2/b2284e7ffa962fd46333c4eca47a77473007fdd96e0813b9928f0303df8170f3.png)\n\n \nSo, $\\displaystyle\\int_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r = 0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186179611 1071 "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y) =( e^y+x)\\,\\mathbf i +(xe^y+y^2)\\,\\mathbf j.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\mathbf F$ is conservative\n\n- $\\displaystyle\\int\\limits_C\\mathbf F \\cdot \\textrm d\\mathbf r = 0$ for any piecewise-smooth path $C\\in\\mathbb R^2$\n\n- $\\displaystyle\\oint\\limits_C\\mathbf F \\cdot \\textrm d\\mathbf r =0$ for any closed path $C\\in\\mathbb R^2$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186179611 1071 "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y) =(e^y+x)\\,\\mathbf i +(xe^y+y^2)\\,\\mathbf j$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $\\mathbf F$ is conservative\n2. $\\displaystyle\\int\\limits_C\\mathbf F \\cdot \\textrm d\\mathbf r = 0$ for any piecewise-smooth path $C\\in\\mathbb R^2$\n3. $\\displaystyle\\oint\\limits_C\\mathbf F \\cdot \\textrm d\\mathbf r =0$ for any closed path $C\\in\\mathbb R^2$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186179611 1074 "If $\\mathbf F$ is a conservative vector field on $\\mathbb R^2$, then the circulation $\\Gamma = 0$ for any *closed* path $C\\in\\mathbb R^2$, that is\n\n$$\n\\Gamma = \\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\textrm{d} \\mathbf r = 0\n$$\n\nWe notice that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F = P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j\n$$\n\nis a vector field with domain\n\n$$\nD = \\mathbb R^2\n$$\n\nComputing the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} = e^y\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true. The vector field $\\mathbf F$ is conservative since the partial derivatives are equal and continuous everywhere on $\\mathbb R^2$.\n- Statement II is false, while statement III is true. The result $\\Gamma = 0$ applies to closed curves only.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186179611 1074 "If $\\mathbf F$ is a conservative vector field on $\\mathbb R^2,$ then the circulation $\\Gamma = 0$ for any *closed* path $C\\in\\mathbb R^2,$ that is \n\n$$\n\\Gamma = \\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\textrm{d} \\mathbf r = 0.\n$$\n\nWe notice that $\\mathbf F = P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j$ is a vector field with domain $D = \\mathbb R^2.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} =  e^y.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true. The vector field $\\mathbf F$ is conservative since the partial derivatives are equal and continuous everywhere on $\\mathbb R^2.$\n\n- Statement II is false, while statement III is true. The result $\\Gamma = 0$ applies to closed curves only.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186179612 1071 "Calculate the flux of the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y) = (y - x)\\,\\mathbf{i} - (x + y )\\,\\mathbf{j}$ across the closed curve $C$ given by $\\mathbf r(t) = 4\\cos t\\,\\mathbf{i} + 4\\sin t\\,\\mathbf{j}$ for $t \\in [0,2\\pi).$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186179612 1071 "Calculate the flux of the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y) = (y - x)\\,\\mathbf{i} - (x + y)\\,\\mathbf{j}$ across the closed curve $C$ given by $\\mathbf r(t) = 4\\cos t\\,\\mathbf{i} + 4\\sin t\\,\\mathbf{j}$ for $t \\in [0,2\\pi)$." 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186179612 1074 "If\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F} = P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j\n$$\n\nis a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s = \\oint\\limits_{C}P\\,\\textrm d y - Q\\,\\textrm d x\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{n}$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C$.\n\nAlong the curve $C$, we have\n\n$$\nx = 4\\cos t, \\qquad y = 4\\sin t\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(x, y) &= y - x = 4(\\sin t - \\cos t), \\\\\nQ(x, y) &= - (x + y) =-4(\\cos t + \\sin t)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dx}{dt} &= \\frac{d}{dt}(4\\cos t) =-4\\sin t, \\\\\n\\frac{dy}{dt} &= \\frac{d}{dt}(4\\sin t) = 4\\cos t\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∮_(C)F \\cdot nds &= ∮_(C)Pdy - Qdx \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{2π}(P \\cdot \\frac{dy}{dt} - Q \\cdot \\frac{dx}{dt})dt \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{2π}(4(\\sin t - \\cos t)) \\cdot (4\\cos t) - (-4(\\cos t + \\sin t)) \\cdot (-4\\sin t)dt \\\\\n&= 16\\int_{0}^{2π}\\sin t\\cos t - \\cos^{2} t - \\sin t\\cos t - \\sin^{2} tdt \\\\\n&= 16\\int_{0}^{2π}(-1)dt \\\\\n&= \\left.- 16t\\right|_{0}^{2π} \\\\\n&=-16(2π - 0) \\\\\n&=-32π\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186179612 1074 "If $\\mathbf{F} = P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s = \\oint\\limits_{C}P\\,\\textrm d y - Q\\,\\textrm d x,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{n}$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ \n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx = 4\\cos t, \\qquad\n\ty = 4\\sin t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =y-x=4(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t), \\\\ Q(x,y) & =-(x+y)=-4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(4(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t))\\cdot (4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-(-4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t))\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =16{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-{cos}^{2}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =16{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(-1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-16t{|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =-16(2\\pi -0) \\\\  & =-32\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186179613 1071 "Calculate the flux of the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y) = xy\\,\\mathbf{i} + x^2\\,\\mathbf{j}$ across the ellipse $C$ with equation $9x^2 + y^2 = 9$." 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186179613 1071 "Calculate the flux of the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y) = xy\\,\\mathbf{i} + x^2\\,\\mathbf{j}$ across the ellipse $C$ with equation $9x^2 + y^2 = 9.$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186179613 1074 "If\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F} = P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j\n$$\n\nis a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s = \\oint\\limits_{C}P\\,\\textrm d y - Q\\,\\textrm d x\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{n}$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C$.\n\nFirst, we write the equation of our curve as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{1^2}+\\dfrac{y^2}{3^2} = 1\n$$\n\nThe closed curve $C$ is an ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal and vertical semiaxes $1$ and $3$, respectively. It can be parameterized in the counterclockwise direction as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\cos{t}\\,\\mathbf{i} +3\\sin{t}\\,\\mathbf{j}, \\qquad 0 \\leq t \\leq 2\\pi\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C$, we have\n\n$$\nx = \\cos t, \\qquad y = 3\\sin t\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(x, y) &= xy = 3\\sin t\\cos t, \\\\\nQ(x, y) &= x^{2} = \\cos^{2} t\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dx}{dt} &= \\frac{d}{dt}(\\cos t) =-\\sin t, \\\\\n\\frac{dy}{dt} &= \\frac{d}{dt}(3\\sin t) = 3\\cos t\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral using the substitution\n\n$$\nu=\\cos t, \\textrm d u = -\\sin t\\,\\textrm d t\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∮_(C)F \\cdot nds &= ∮_(C)Pdy - Qdx \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{2π}(P \\cdot \\frac{dy}{dt} - Q \\cdot \\frac{dx}{dt})dt \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{2π}(3\\sin t\\cos t) \\cdot (3\\cos t) - (\\cos^{2} t) \\cdot (-\\sin t)dt \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{2π}9\\sin t\\cos^{2} t + \\sin t\\cos^{2} tdt \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{2π}10\\sin t\\cos^{2} tdt \\\\\n&=-\\int_{1}^{1}10u^{2}du \\\\\n&= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186179613 1074 "If $\\mathbf{F} = P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s = \\oint\\limits_{C}P\\,\\textrm d y - Q\\,\\textrm d x,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{n}$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ \n\nFirst, we write the equation of our curve as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{1^2}+\\dfrac{y^2}{3^2} = 1.\n$$\n\nThe closed curve $C$ is an ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal and vertical semiaxes $1$ and $3,$ respectively. It can be parameterized in the counterclockwise direction as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\cos{t}\\,\\mathbf{i} +3\\sin{t}\\,\\mathbf{j},\n\\qquad 0 \\leq t \\leq 2\\pi.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx = \\cos t, \\qquad\ny = 3\\sin t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =xy=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t, \\\\ Q(x,y) & ={x}^{2}={cos}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral using the substitution $u=\\cos t, \\textrm d u = -\\sin t\\,\\textrm d t,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot (3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-({cos}^{2}⁡t)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }9\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }10\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{1}^{1}10{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186179614 1071 "![](images/6e/6eebbc5ae9f548f5b00de9a12d454fa788884bf73ad46a1065eab0d26330a921.png)\n\nThe curve $C$ is given by $y = \\dfrac14x^2$ from the point $\\left(0,0\\right)$ to the point $\\left(4,4\\right)$. Calculate the flux of the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y) = -2\\,\\mathbf{i} + x\\,\\mathbf{j}$ through $C$, measured with respect to the unit normal vector $\\mathbf n$ on $C$, as shown above." 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186179614 1071 "![](images/6e/6eebbc5ae9f548f5b00de9a12d454fa788884bf73ad46a1065eab0d26330a921.png)\n\nThe curve $C$ is given by $y = \\dfrac14x^2$ from the point $\\left(0,0\\right)$ to the point $\\left(4,4\\right).$ Calculate the flux of the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y) = -2\\,\\mathbf{i} + x\\,\\mathbf{j}$ through $C,$ measured with respect to the unit normal vector $\\mathbf n$ on $C,$ as shown above." 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186179614 1074 "If\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F} = P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j\n$$\n\nis a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ measured with respect to a unit normal vector $\\mathbf n$ on $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s = \\int\\limits_{C}P\\,\\textrm d y - Q\\,\\textrm d x\n$$\n\nTo apply this formula,\n\n- the curve should be parameterized as $\\mathbf r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b]$, and\n- the unit normal vector $\\mathbf n$ *must* be a $90^\\circ$ clockwise rotation of the unit tangent vector $\\mathbf T(t)$.\n\nParameterizing the curve $C$, we have\n\n$$\nx = t, \\qquad y = \\dfrac{t^2}{4}, \\qquad t\\in \\left[0,4\\right]\n$$\n\nThis parameterization traverses the curve from the point $(0,0)$ to the point $(4,4)$. Therefore, $\\mathbf n$ is indeed a $90^\\circ$ clockwise rotation of $\\mathbf T$, so we can apply the above formula for the flux.\n\n![](images/2b/2b94e24344f600fc02ec5ecac6c1e0ee4c6a5450132753a0a5ac5fceaa9aa1fc.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(x, y) &= -2, \\\\\nQ(x, y) &= x = t\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dx}{dt} &= \\frac{d}{dt}(t) = 1, \\\\\n\\frac{dy}{dt} &= \\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{t^{2}}{4}) = \\frac{t}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_(C)F \\cdot nds &= \\int_(C)Pdy - Qdx \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{4}(P \\cdot \\frac{dy}{dt} - Q \\cdot \\frac{dx}{dt})dt \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{4}(-2) \\cdot (\\frac{t}{2}) - (t) \\cdot (1)dt \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{4} - 2tdt \\\\\n&= \\left.- t^{2}\\right|_{0}^{4} \\\\\n&=-16 - 0 \\\\\n&=-16\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186179614 1074 "If $\\mathbf{F} = P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ measured with respect to a unit normal vector $\\mathbf n$ on $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\: \\textrm{d}s = \\int\\limits_{C}P\\,\\textrm d y - Q\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nTo apply this formula, \n\n- the curve should be parameterized as $\\mathbf r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b],$ and\n\n- the unit normal vector $\\mathbf n$ *must* be a $90^\\circ$ clockwise rotation of the unit tangent vector $\\mathbf T(t).$\n\nParameterizing the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx = t, \\qquad\ny = \\dfrac{t^2}{4},\n\\qquad\nt\\in \\left[0,4\\right].\n$$\n\n \n\nThis parameterization traverses the curve from the point $(0,0)$ to the point $(4,4).$ Therefore, $\\mathbf n$ is indeed a $90^\\circ$ clockwise rotation of $\\mathbf T,$ so we can apply the above formula for the flux. \n\n![](images/2b/2b94e24344f600fc02ec5ecac6c1e0ee4c6a5450132753a0a5ac5fceaa9aa1fc.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =-2, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =x=t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)=1, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{t}^{2}}{4})=\\frac{t}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}(-2)\\cdot (\\frac{t}{2})-(t)\\cdot (1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}-2t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-{t}^{2}{|}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =-16-0 \\\\  & =-16\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186179622 1071 "![](images/51/51ecea6126e2bc887610e5a775f6cc82906c4eb1f569277bbc626d0502bd9efa.png)\n\nThe scatter plot shows the value of an artwork, in thousands of dollars, several decades after it was created. Assuming this trend continues, estimate the time it takes for the work of art to be worth $\\[math]5\\,000$.\n\n*Hint: Assume the trend is linear and that the trend line passes through the points $(1,1.5)$ and $(4,3)$.*" 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186179622 1071 "![](images/51/51ecea6126e2bc887610e5a775f6cc82906c4eb1f569277bbc626d0502bd9efa.png)\n\nThe scatter plot shows the value of an artwork, in thousands of dollars, several decades after it was created. Assuming this trend continues, estimate the time it takes for the work of art to be worth $\\[math]5\\,000.$\n\n*Hint: Assume the trend is linear and that the trend line passes through the points $(1,1.5)$ and $(4,3).$*" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186179622 1074 "First, using the hint, let's draw an approximate trend line for our scatter plot.\n\n![](images/76/761a2d3608402bb18b3574040556618d5bb0fa2c9bb53ddcb382f3779ae1af0b.png)\n\nThe trend line passes through\n\n$$\n(x_1,y_1) = (1,1.5)\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n(x_2,y_2) = (4,3)\n$$\n\nWe will write the equation of the line in the slope-intercept form\n\n$$\ny = mx + b\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the slope $m$ of the line as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm &= \\frac{y_{2} - y_{1}}{x_{2} - x_{1}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3 - 1.5}{4 - 1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1.5}{3} \\\\\n&= 0.5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $m = 0.5$ into the slope-intercept form above, we get\n\n$$\ny = 0.5x + b\n$$\n\nWe now need to calculate the $y$-intercept $b$. We can do this by substituting the coordinates of a point on the line. Any point will do, so let's substitute $(4,3)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= 0.5x + b \\\\\n3 &= 0.5(4) + b \\\\\n3 &= 2 + b \\\\\nb &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the line is\n\n$$\ny = 0.5x + 1\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y = 5$ into the equation above and solve for $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= 0.5x + 1 \\\\\n5 &= 0.5x + 1 \\\\\n0.5x &= 4 \\\\\nx &= 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, according to this model, the artwork will be worth $\\[math]5\\,000$ approximately $8$ decades after it was created.\n\n**Note:** The point where $y=5$ is not shown on our trend line. Therefore, our result *may* be unreliable." 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186179622 1074 "First, using the hint, let's draw an approximate trend line for our scatter plot.\n\n![](images/76/761a2d3608402bb18b3574040556618d5bb0fa2c9bb53ddcb382f3779ae1af0b.png)\n\nThe trend line passes through $(x_1,y_1) = (1,1.5)$ and $(x_2,y_2) = (4,3).$\n\nWe will write the equation of the line in the slope-intercept form \n\n$$\ny = mx + b.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the slope $m$ of the line as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3-1.5}{4-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1.5}{3} \\\\  & =0.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $m = 0.5$ into the slope-intercept form above, we get\n\n$$\ny = 0.5x + b.\n$$\n\nWe now need to calculate the $y$-intercept $b.$ We can do this by substituting the coordinates of a point on the line. Any point will do, so let's substitute $(4,3)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =0.5x+b \\\\ 3 & =0.5(4)+b \\\\ 3 & =2+b \\\\ b & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the line is \n\n$$\ny = 0.5x + 1.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y = 5$ into the equation above and solve for $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =0.5x+1 \\\\ 5 & =0.5x+1 \\\\ 0.5x & =4 \\\\ x & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, according to this model, the artwork will be worth $\\[math]5\\,000$ approximately $8$ decades after it was created.\n\n**Note:** The point where $y=5$ is not shown on our trend line. Therefore, our result *may* be unreliable." 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186179623 1071 "![](images/ec/ecce4e60594a69e040328cc8f65300f7ad1ecfe20806d9be08f154fe2beb1246.png)\n\nThe scatter plot above shows the relationship between a book’s spine thickness (in millimetres) and the total number of pages for a random sample of children's books. The equation of the trend line is $y = 10x$. Assuming this trend continues, estimate the thickness of a book with $200$ pages." 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186179623 1071 "![](images/ec/ecce4e60594a69e040328cc8f65300f7ad1ecfe20806d9be08f154fe2beb1246.png)\n\nThe scatter plot above shows the relationship between a book’s spine thickness (in millimetres) and the total number of pages for a random sample of children's books. The equation of the trend line is $y = 10x.$ Assuming this trend continues, estimate the thickness of a book with $200$ pages." 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186179623 1074 "To predict the thickness in a book with $200$ pages, we need to substitute $y = 200$ into the equation of the trend line and solve for $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= 10x \\\\\n200 &= 10x \\\\\n\\frac{200}{10} &= x \\\\\n20 &= x \\\\\nx &= 20\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, according to this model, a book with $200$ pages should have approximately a thickness of $20\\text{mm}$.\n\n**Note:** The point where $y=200$ is not shown on our trend line. Therefore, our result *may* be unreliable." 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186179623 1074 "To predict the thickness in a book with $200$ pages, we need to substitute $y = 200$ into the equation of the trend line and solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =10x \\\\ 200 & =10x \\\\ \\frac{200}{10} & =x \\\\ 20 & =x \\\\ x & =20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, according to this model, a book with $200$ pages should have approximately a thickness of $20\\text{ mm}.$\n\n**Note:** The point where $y=200$ is not shown on our trend line. Therefore, our result *may* be unreliable." 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186179795 1038 #edb/ref 17592186237799 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186179795 1038 #edb/ref 17592186237800 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186179795 1038 #edb/ref 17592186237801 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186184109 1071 "Determine the coefficient of $r^3$ in the expansion of $\\left( 1 - 3r \\right)^{10}.$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186184109 1071 "Determine the coefficient of $r^3$ in the expansion of $\\left(1 - 3r \\right)^{10}$." 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186184109 1074 "The binomial expansion for $(1 + x)^n$ is given by\n\n$$\n(1 + x)^n = 1 + nx + \\frac{n(n - 1)}{2!} x^2 + \\cdots\n$$\n\nIn particular, the $x^3$ term is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{n(n-1)(n-2)}{3!} x^3\n$$\n\nReplacing $x$ with $-3r$ and $n$ with $10$ in the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(10(10 - 1)(10 - 2))/(3!)(-3r)^{3} &= (10(9)(8))/(6) \\cdot (-3)^{3} \\cdot r^{3} \\\\\n&= (10(9)(8))/(6) \\cdot (-27)r^{3} \\\\\n&=-3240r^{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coefficient of $r^3$ is $-3\\,240$." 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186184109 1074 "The binomial expansion for $(1 + x)^n$ is given by\n\n$$\n(1 + x)^n = 1 + nx + \\frac{n(n - 1)}{2!} x^2 + \\cdots .\n$$\n\nIn particular, the $x^3$ term is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{n(n-1)(n-2)}{3!} x^3.\n$$\n\nReplacing $x$ with $-3r$ and $n$ with $10$ in the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{10(10-1)(10-2)}{3!}(-3r)^{3} & =\\frac{10(9)(8)}{6}\\cdot (-3)^{3}\\cdot {r}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{10(9)(8)}{6}\\cdot (-27){r}^{3} \\\\  & =-3\\,240{r}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coefficient of $r^3$ is $-3\\,240.$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186184110 1071 "Find the first $3$ terms in ascending powers of $m$ of the binomial expansion of $\\left(1 - \\dfrac{4m}{3}\\right)^7$." 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186184110 1071 "Find the first $3$ terms in ascending powers of $m$ of the binomial expansion of $\\left(1 - \\dfrac{4m}{3}\\right)^7.$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186184110 1074 "The first $3$ terms of the binomial expansion for ${(1 + x)^n}$ are given by\n\n$$\n{(1 + x)}^n = 1 + nx + \\frac{{n(n - 1)}}{{2!}}{x^2}  +  \\cdots .\n$$\n\nBy substituting $x=-\\dfrac{4m}{3}$ and $n=7$ into the above formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(1-\\frac{4m}{3})}^{7} & =1+7(-\\fr