act* ${\\color{blue} 5}^2$, writing the constant term ${\\color{purple}20}$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{x^2- {\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue} 5}\\cdot x + {\\color{blue} 5}^2} - {\\color{blue} 5}^2 + {\\color{purple}20}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left(x-5\\right)^2 -{\\color{blue} 5}^2 + {\\color{purple}20}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\left(x-5\\right)^2 - 5\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-2(x^{2} - 10x + 20) &= -2((x - 5)^{2} - 5) \\\\\n&=-2(x - 5)^{2} + (-2) \\cdot (-5) \\\\\n&=-2(x - 5)^{2} + 10\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186184122 1074 "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-2.$ So, we take out a factor of $-2$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-2\\left(x^2 - 10x + 20\\right)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\nx^2 - 10x  + {\\color{purple}20}\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with ${\\color{red}2}$ as a factor: \n\n$$\nx^2- {\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue} 5}\\cdot x + {\\color{purple}20}\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${\\color{blue} 5}^2,$ writing the constant term ${\\color{purple}20}$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{x^2- {\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue} 5}\\cdot x + {\\color{blue} 5}^2} - {\\color{blue} 5}^2 + {\\color{purple}20}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left(x-5\\right)^2 -{\\color{blue} 5}^2 + {\\color{purple}20}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\left(x-5\\right)^2 - 5.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2({x}^{2}-10x+20) & =-2({(x-5)}^{2}-5) \\\\  & =-2{(x-5)}^{2}+(-2)\\cdot (-5) \\\\  & =-2{(x-5)}^{2}+10\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186184123 1071 "Find the solution of $2