e write $x \\in \\left(-2, 0\\right).$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186184130 1071 "Consider a $8 \\times 6$ matrix $A$. If $\\textrm{rank}(A)=5$ then what is $\\textrm{nullity}(A)$?" 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186184130 1071 "Consider a $8 \\times 6$ matrix $A.$ If $\\textrm{rank}(A)=5$ then what is $\\textrm{nullity}(A)?$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186184130 1074 "According to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then\n\n$$\n\\textrm{rank}(A) + \\textrm{nullity}(A) = n\n$$\n\nFor the given matrix, we have $n=6$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nrank(A) + nullity(A) &= n \\\\\n5 + nullity(A) &= 6 \\\\\nnullity(A) &= 6 - 5 \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186184130 1074 "According to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then  \n\n$$\n\\textrm{rank}(A) + \\textrm{nullity}(A) = n.\n$$\n\nFor the given matrix, we have $n=6.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ 5+\\text{nullity}(A) & =6 \\\\ \\text{nullity}(A) & =6-5 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186184131 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $h(x)$, defined below?\n${e^{x - 1},\\begin{vmatrix}x \\le 1, \\\\ 1 - x,\\end{vmatrix}x > 1$\n\n1. $h(x)$ is left-continuous at $x=1$\n2. $h(x)$ is right-continuous at $x=1$\n3. $h(x)$ is continuous at $x=1$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186184131 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $h(x),$ defined below?\n![](images/ba/ba95d95494b67b2da7f5ebeb108fd75a42945291bae5440ff12d2edaa73f2798.png)\n\n- $h(x)$ is left-continuous at $x=1$\n\n- $h(x)$ is right-continuous at $x=1$\n\n- $h(x)$ is continuous at $x=1$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186184131 1074 "We're required to determine the left and right continuity properties of $h(x)$ at $x = 1$.\n\nLet's start by computing $h(1){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nh(1) &= e^{1 - 1} \\\\\n&= e^{0} \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. The function is left-continuous at $x=1$ since the left-sided limit is equal to the function value:\n$lim_(x → 1^{-})h(x)|= e^{1 - 1}; = e^{0}; = 1; = h(1)$\n- Statement II is false. The function is *not* right-continuous at $x=1$ since the right-sided limit is *not* equal to the function value:\n$lim_(x → 1^{+})h(x)|= 1 - 1; = 0; \\ne h(1)$\n\nStatement III is false. For the function to be continuous at $x=1$, it must be both left-continuous and right-continuous at this point. However, we have already determined that the function is not right-continuous at $x=1$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186184131 1074 "We're required to determine the left and right continuity properties of $h(x)$ at $x = 1.$\n\nLet's start by computing $h(1){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(1) & ={e}^{1-1} \\\\  & ={e}^{0} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. The function is left-continuous at $x=1$ since the left-sided limit is equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}h(x) & ={e}^{1-1} \\\\  & ={e}^{0} \\\\  & =1 \\\\  & =h(1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. The function is *not* right-continuous at $x=1$ since the right-sided limit is *not* equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}h(x) & =1-1 \\\\  & =0 \\\\  & \\ne h(1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStatement III is false. For the function to be continuous at $x=1,$ it must be both left-continuous and right-continuous at this point. However, we have already determined that the function is not right-continuous at $x=1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186184135 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $f(x)$, defined below?\n${2 - x,\\begin{vmatrix}x < 0 \\\\ 2,\\end{vmatrix}x = 0; \\frac{4}{x + 2}, \\mid x > 0$\n\n1. $f(x)$ is left-continuous at $x = 0$\n2. $f(x)$ is right-continuous at $x = 0$\n3. $f(x)$ is continuous at $x = 0$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186184135 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $f(x),$ defined below?\n![](images/49/4985f1b43abee44c927a619c1d8965c98a2283f65d5723429477f7e29dbdc74c.png)\n\n- $f(x)$ is left-continuous at $x = 0$\n\n- $f(x)$ is right-continuous at $x = 0$\n\n- $f(x)$ is continuous at $x = 0$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186184135 1074 "We're required to determine the left and right continuity properties of $f(x)$ at $x = 0$.\n\nFirst, we note that\n\n$$\nf(0) = 2\n$$\n\nNow, let's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. The function is left-continuous at $x = 0$ since the left-sided limit is equal to the function value:\n$lim_(x → 0^{-})f(x)|= 2 - 0; = 2$\n- Statement II is true. The function is right-continuous at $x = 0$ since the right-sided limit is equal to the function value:\n$lim_(x → 0^{+})f(x)|= \\frac{4}{0 + 2}; = \\frac{4}{2}; = 2; = f(0)$\n- Statement III is true. The function is both left and right-continuous at $x=0$, which implies that the function is continuous at $x=0$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186184135 1074 "We're required to determine the left and right continuity properties of $f(x)$ at $x = 0.$\n\nFirst, we note that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0)=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's look at each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. The function is left-continuous at $x = 0$ since the left-sided limit is equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x) & =2-0 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. The function is right-continuous at $x = 0$ since the right-sided limit is equal to the function value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x) & =\\frac{4}{0+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{2} \\\\  & =2 \\\\  & =f(0)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. The function is both left and right-continuous at $x=0,$ which implies that the function is continuous at $x=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186184136 1071 "Find the solution of $-x^2 + 7 x - 10 \\leq 0$." 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186184136 1071 "Find the solution of $-x^2 + 7 x - 10 \\leq 0.$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186184136 1074 "To solve the inequality\n\n$$\n-x^2 + 7 x - 10 \\leq 0\n$$\n\nwe need to find the values of $x$ for which the parabola\n\n$$\ny = -x^2 + 7 x - 10\n$$\n\nlies *below* the $x$-axis or touches this axis.\n\nFirst, let's find the roots of the parabola:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-x^{2} + 7x - 10 &= 0 \\\\\n- (x^{2} - 7x + 10) &= 0 \\\\\n- (x - 2)(x - 5) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the roots are $x=2$ and $x = 5$, and we can graph the parabola as follows:\n\n![](images/13/13e108b6383e99c5840789389378fd58cd8704484296485cb6483112ecf4ae25.png)\n\nThe values marked in blue show all $x$-values where the parabola lies *below* the $x$-axis or touches this axis.\n\nSo, the solution to the inequality is\n\n$$\nx \\leq 2\n$$\n\nor\n\n$$\nx \\geq 5\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186184136 1074 "To solve the inequality \n\n$$\n-x^2 + 7 x - 10 \\leq 0,\n$$\n\nwe need to find the values of $x$ for which the parabola \n\n$$\ny = -x^2 + 7 x - 10\n$$\n\nlies *below* the $x$-axis or touches this axis.\n\nFirst, let's find the roots of the parabola:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}+7x-10 & =0 \\\\ -({x}^{2}-7x+10) & =0 \\\\ -(x-2)(x-5) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the roots are $x=2$ and $x = 5,$ and we can graph the parabola as follows:\n\n![](images/13/13e108b6383e99c5840789389378fd58cd8704484296485cb6483112ecf4ae25.png)\n\nThe values marked in blue show all $x$-values where the parabola lies *below* the $x$-axis or touches this axis.\n\nSo, the solution to the inequality is $x \\leq 2$ or $x \\geq 5.$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186184138 1071 "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v$, we have\n$\\cos u = \\dfrac{7}{25}, \\qquad \\sin v = \\dfrac{4}{5}, \\qquad \\cos v = \\dfrac {3} {5}$.\n\nGiven that $u$ lies in the fourth quadrant, find the value of $\\sin(u-v)$." 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186184138 1071 "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\cos u = \\dfrac{7}{25}, \\qquad \\sin v = \\dfrac{4}{5}, \\qquad \\cos v = \\dfrac {3} {5}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $u$ lies in the fourth quadrant, find the value of $\\sin(u-v).$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186184138 1074 "First, recall the difference formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\sin(u-v) = \\sin u \\cos v- \\cos u \\sin v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\cos{u}$, $\\sin{v}$, and $\\cos{v}$. To find the missing value of $\\sin{u}$, we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n\\sin^2 u + \\cos^2 u= 1 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\sin^2 u = 1- \\cos^2 u\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin^{2} u &= 1 - \\cos^{2} u \\\\\n&= 1 - (\\frac{7}{25})^{2} \\\\\n&= 1 - \\frac{7^{2}}{25^{2}} \\\\\n&= 1 - \\frac{49}{625} \\\\\n&= \\frac{625}{625} - \\frac{49}{625} \\\\\n&= \\frac{576}{625}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin u &= ± \\sqrt{\\frac{576}{625}} \\\\\n&= ± \\frac{24}{25}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $u$ lies in the fourth quadrant, where sine is negative. So, we disregard the positive value, and we have\n\n$$\n\\sin u= -\\dfrac{24}{25}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the difference formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\sin(u - v) &= \\sin u \\cos v - \\cos u \\sin v \\\\[5pt] &= \\left(-\\dfrac {24}{25} \\right)\\left(\\dfrac {3} {5}\\right) - \\left(\\dfrac {7}{25}\\right) \\left(\\dfrac {4} {5}\\right) \\\\[5pt] &= -\\dfrac {72} {125} - \\dfrac {28} {125} \\\\[5pt] &= -\\dfrac {100} {125} \\\\[5pt] &= -\\dfrac {4} {5}. \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186184138 1074 "First, recall the difference formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\sin(u-v) = \\sin u \\cos v- \\cos u \\sin v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\cos{u}$, $\\sin{v}$, and $\\cos{v}.$ To find the missing value of $\\sin{u}$, we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n\\sin^2 u + \\cos^2 u= 1 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\sin^2 u = 1- \\cos^2 u\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡u & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡u \\\\  & =1-{(\\frac{7}{25})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{7}^{2}}{{25}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{49}{625} \\\\  & =\\frac{625}{625}-\\frac{49}{625} \\\\  & =\\frac{576}{625}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{576}{625}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{24}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $u$ lies in the fourth quadrant, where sine is negative. So, we disregard the positive value, and we have \n\n$$\n\\sin u= -\\dfrac{24}{25}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the difference formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\sin(u - v) \n\t\t\t&= \\sin u \\cos v - \\cos u \\sin v \\[5pt]\n\t\t\t&= \\left( -\\dfrac {24}{25} \\right)\\left(\\dfrac {3} {5}\\right)\n \t\t\t\t -  \\left(\\dfrac {7}{25}\\right) \\left( \\dfrac {4} {5}\\right) \\[5pt]\n\t\t\t\n \t\t\t&= -\\dfrac {72} {125} -  \\dfrac {28} {125} \\[5pt]\n\t\t\t&= -\\dfrac {100} {125}\\[5pt]\n\t\t\t&= -\\dfrac {4} {5}.\n\t}\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186184140 1071 "Consider the sequence $a_n = \\dfrac{x^{2n+1}}{x^{n-2}}$, where $x$ is a real positive number and $n\\geq 1$. What are the values of $x$ for which the sequence converges?" 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186184140 1071 "Consider the sequence $a_n = \\dfrac{x^{2n+1}}{x^{n-2}},$ where $x$ is a real positive number and $n\\geq 1.$ What are the values of $x$ for which the sequence converges?" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186184140 1074 "Suppose we have a geometric sequence with the first term $a_1$ and common ratio $r$. Then, we have the following convergence properties:\n\n- if $\\mid r \\mid < 1$, the sequence is convergent and converges to $0$\n- if $\\mid r \\mid > 1$, the sequence is divergent\n\nMoreover, we have the following edge cases:\n\n- if $r=1$, the sequence is convergent and converges to $a_1$\n- if $r = -1$, the sequence is divergent\n\nFirst, we rewrite the sequence in the form\n\n$$\na_n = a \\cdot r^{n}\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{n} &= \\frac{x^{2n + 1}}{x^{n - 2}} \\\\\n&= x^{2n + 1 - n + 2} \\\\\n&= x^{n + 3} \\\\\n&= x^{3} \\cdot x^{n}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that this is a geometric sequence with common ratio $r = x$. Since $x$ is positive, $r$ is also positive. So, the convergence condition is\n\n$$\n0 \\lt r \\leq 1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sequence converges for\n\n$$\n0 \\lt x \\leq 1\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186184140 1074 "Suppose we have a geometric sequence with the first term $a_1$ and common ratio $r.$ Then, we have the following convergence properties:\n\n- if $|r| <              1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to $0$\n\n- if $|r| > 1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nMoreover, we have the following edge cases:\n\n- if $r=1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to $a_1$\n\n- if $r = -1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nFirst, we rewrite the sequence in the form $a_n = a \\cdot r^{n},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =\\frac{{x}^{2n+1}}{{x}^{n-2}} \\\\  & ={x}^{2n+1-n+2} \\\\  & ={x}^{n+3} \\\\  & ={x}^{3}\\cdot {x}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nWe see that this is a geometric sequence with common ratio $r = x.$ Since $x$ is positive,  $r$ is also positive. So, the convergence condition is $0 \\lt  r \\leq 1.$ \n\nTherefore, the  sequence converges for \n\n$$\n0 \\lt  x \\leq 1 .\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186184141 1071 "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v$, we have\n$\\sin u = \\dfrac {4} {5}, \\qquad \\cos u = \\dfrac {3} {5}, \\qquad \\sin v = \\dfrac {9} {15}$.\nGiven that $v$ lies in the first quadrant, find the value of $\\cos\\left(u - v\\right)$." 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186184141 1071 "Suppose that for the angles $u$ and $v,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\sin u = \\dfrac {4} {5}, \\qquad\n\\cos u = \\dfrac {3} {5}, \\qquad \n\\sin v = \\dfrac {9} {15}.\n$$\n\nGiven that $v$ lies in the first quadrant, find the value of $\\cos\\left(u - v\\right).$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186184141 1074 "First, we recall the difference formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\cos(u - v) = \\cos u \\cos v + \\sin u \\sin v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\sin{u}$, $\\cos{u}$, and $\\sin{v}$. To find the missing value of $\\cos{v,}$ we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n\\sin^2 v + \\cos^2 v= 1 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\cos^2 v = 1- \\sin^2 v\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\cos^{2} v &= 1 - \\sin^{2} v \\\\\n&= 1 - (\\frac{9}{15})^{2} \\\\\n&= 1 - \\frac{9^{2}}{15^{2}} \\\\\n&= 1 - \\frac{81}{225} \\\\\n&= \\frac{225}{225} - \\frac{81}{225} \\\\\n&= \\frac{144}{225}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\cos v &= ± \\sqrt{\\frac{144}{225}} \\\\\n&= ± \\frac{12}{15}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $v$ lies in the first quadrant, where cosine is positive. So, we disregard the negative value, and we have\n\n$$\n\\cos v= \\dfrac{12}{15}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the difference formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\cos (u - v) &= \\cos u\\cos v + \\sin u\\sin v \\\\\n&= (\\frac{3}{5})(\\frac{12}{15}) + (\\frac{4}{5})(\\frac{9}{15}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{36}{75} + \\frac{36}{75} \\\\\n&= \\frac{72}{75} \\\\\n&= \\frac{24}{25}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186184141 1074 "First, we recall the difference formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\cos(u - v) = \\cos u \\cos v + \\sin u \\sin v\n$$\n\nWe know the values of $\\sin{u}$, $\\cos{u}$, and $\\sin{v}.$ To find the missing value of $\\cos{v,}$ we can use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n\\sin^2 v + \\cos^2 v= 1 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad \\cos^2 v = 1- \\sin^2 v\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values into the Pythagorean identity, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡v & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡v \\\\  & =1-{(\\frac{9}{15})}^{2} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{9}^{2}}{{15}^{2}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{81}{225} \\\\  & =\\frac{225}{225}-\\frac{81}{225} \\\\  & =\\frac{144}{225}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{144}{225}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{12}{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $v$ lies in the first quadrant, where cosine is positive. So, we disregard the negative value, and we have\n\n$$\n\\cos v= \\dfrac{12}{15}.\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the difference formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v \\\\  & =(\\frac{3}{5})(\\frac{12}{15})+(\\frac{4}{5})(\\frac{9}{15}) \\\\  & =\\frac{36}{75}+\\frac{36}{75} \\\\  & =\\frac{72}{75} \\\\  & =\\frac{24}{25}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186192722 1038 #edb/ref 17592186237794 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186192723 1038 #edb/ref 17592186237792 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186192944 1068 #edb/ref 17592186189520 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186192944 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237606 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186192944 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186192944 1071 "Evaluate the integral $\\displaystyle \\int_{- ∞}^{1/2}\\frac{1}{1 + 4t^{2}}dt =$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186192944 1071 "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\sqrt{3}/6}\\frac{1}{1+4{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t=$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186192944 1074 "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+(kx)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(kx)+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\sqrt{3}/6}\\frac{1}{1+4{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{\\sqrt{3}/6}\\frac{1}{1+4{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{\\sqrt{3}/6}\\frac{1}{1+(2t)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2t)]}_{a}^{\\sqrt{3}/6} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2a)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{\\pi }{6}-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2a)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x),$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x)]=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x)]=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n![](images/46/4612e37c6c611cbaa44c09f4dcc47d006ad45403fff9af5b4df7dca71693dd6e.png)\n\n \n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\sqrt{3}/6}\\frac{1}{1+4{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{\\pi }{6}-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2a)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{6}-(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186192945 1068 #edb/ref 17592186189526 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186192945 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237776 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186192945 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186192945 1071 "${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{x}^{2}\\cdot {2}^{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186192945 1071 "What is $\\displaystyle \\int_{- ∞}^{0}x^{2} \\cdot 2^{x^{3}}dx$?" 13194139533391 17592186045585 false] [17592186192945 1074 "First, we substitute $u={x}^{3}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3{x}^{2}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u={x}^{2}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to -\\infty $ when $x\\to -\\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u={x}^{3}$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $-\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $-\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{x}^{2}\\cdot {2}^{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{2}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{x}^{2}\\cdot {2}^{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{3}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}{2}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{{2}^{u}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[{2}^{u}]_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(1-{2}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}(1-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{2}^{a})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall the end behavior of the function $y={2}^{a},$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{2}^{a}=0$\n\n- ${lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{2}^{a}=\\infty $\n\n![](images/b9/b9a29cbd8a029e0f26e3da6a042ada0f8a809053233979bb4ff73db3dcf0f10d.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{x}^{2}\\cdot {2}^{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}(1-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{2}^{a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}(1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\operator