#edb/ref 17592186236060 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237778 1075 #edb/ref 17592186236129 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237778 1075 #edb/ref 17592186236171 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237778 1075 #edb/ref 17592186236375 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237778 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237778 1077 "selection" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237778 1079 #edb/ref 17592186236375 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237778 1080 #uuid "fe185be0-4aea-5d12-8405-2c55fa532bf9" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237779 1075 #edb/ref 17592186236177 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237779 1075 #edb/ref 17592186236264 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237779 1075 #edb/ref 17592186236507 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237779 1075 #edb/ref 17592186236637 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237779 1075 #edb/ref 17592186237323 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237779 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237779 1077 "selection" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237779 1079 #edb/ref 17592186236264 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237779 1080 #uuid "fe30c037-d401-593e-a43e-6b8a2fcfc035" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237780 1075 #edb/ref 17592186235863 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237780 1075 #edb/ref 17592186236311 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237780 1075 #edb/ref 17592186236585 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237780 1075 #edb/ref 17592186236915 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237780 1075 #edb/ref 17592186237140 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237780 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237780 1077 "selection" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237780 1079 #edb/ref 17592186235863 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237780 1080 #uuid "fedcade9-b1f8-5679-9a46-1f9cee2963e7" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237781 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237528 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237781 1069 "q-100" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237781 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237781 1071 "$[\\begin{aligned}6 & -1 \\\\ 7 & 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 9 \\\\ 1 & 8\\end{aligned}]=$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237781 1072 #uuid "12625ed7-6c5b-551e-b832-297996750c0c" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237781 1074 "We add the corresponding entries:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}6 & -1 \\\\ 7 & 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 9 \\\\ 1 & 8\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}6+(-2) & (-1)+9 \\\\ 7+1 & 0+8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 8 \\\\ 8 & 8\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237782 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237471 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237782 1069 "q-10004" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237782 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237782 1071 "The system of equations\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}y+2z=0 \\\\ x+2y+3z=2 \\\\ 3x+y+z=-3\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ncan be represented using matrix notation as\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & a & 2 \\\\ b & 2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1 & c\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat are the values of $a,b$ and $c?$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237782 1072 #uuid "9047fc55-30cd-5bc7-96fe-e8f5b01aa182" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237782 1074 "First, let's write the system so that $x$, $y$, and $z$ appear in each equation:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}0x+y+2z=0 \\\\ x+2y+3z=2 \\\\ 3x+y+z=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can write the system in the form $Ax=b,$ where\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis the coefficient matrix,\n\n$$\nb=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis the column vector of constants, and\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis the column vector of variables.\n\nSo, the equation $Ax=b$ becomes\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=1,\\,b=1,\\,c=1.$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237783 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237667 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237783 1069 "q-100084" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237783 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237783 1071 "Consider the following set of polynomials:\n\n$$\n{p}_{1}(t)=t-2,\\,{p}_{2}(t)=2-2t,\\,{p}_{3}(t)=2t.\n$$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space ${R}_{1}[t]$ of polynomials of degree at most $1$ in the variable $t$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨p(t),q(t)⟩=p(1)q(1)+p(2)q(2),\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n- ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n- $S={{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{3}(t)}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237783 1072 #uuid "68d1e936-89eb-56ca-bff3-c6afc2d2434a" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237783 1074 "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t)⟩ & =⟨t-2,2-2t⟩ \\\\  & =(t-2){|}_{1}\\cdot (2-2t){|}_{1}+(t-2){|}_{2}\\cdot (2-2t){|}_{2} \\\\  & =(1-2)\\cdot (2-2(1))+(2-2)\\cdot (2-2(2)) \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is false since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{3}(t)⟩ & =⟨t-2,2t⟩ \\\\  & =(t-2){|}_{1}\\cdot (2t){|}_{1}+(t-2){|}_{2}\\cdot (2t){|}_{2} \\\\  & =(1-2)\\cdot (2(1))+(2-2)\\cdot (2(2)) \\\\  & =-2\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${p}_{1}(t)⊥̸{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${p}_{1}(t)⊥̸{p}_{3}(t),$ the set $S$ is *not* orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237784 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237550 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237784 1069 "q-100089" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237784 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237784 1071 "Consider the following set of polynomials:\n\n$$\n{p}_{1}(t)=2t-1,\\,{p}_{2}(t)=3t,\\,{p}_{3}(t)=1-t\n$$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space ${R}_{1}[t]$ of polynomials of degree at most $1$ in the variable $t$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨p(t),q(t)⟩=p(0)q(0)+p(1)q(1),\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n- ${p}_{2}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n- $S={{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{3}(t)}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237784 1072 #uuid "794f079c-000d-5b83-a34d-0c1430e5e296" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237784 1074 "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t)⟩ & =⟨2t-1,3t⟩ \\\\  & =(2t-1){|}_{0}\\cdot (3t){|}_{0}+(2t-1){|}_{1}\\cdot (3t){|}_{1} \\\\  & =(2(0)-1)\\cdot (3(0))+(2(1)-1)\\cdot (3(1)) \\\\  & =3\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${p}_{1}(t)⊥̸{p}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{3}(t)⟩ & =⟨3t,1-t⟩ \\\\  & =(3t){|}_{0}\\cdot (1-t){|}_{0}+(3t){|}_{1}\\cdot (1-t){|}_{1} \\\\  & =(3(0))\\cdot (1-0)+(3(1))\\cdot (1-1) \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${p}_{2}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${p}_{1}(t)⊥̸{p}_{2}(t),$ the set $S$ is *not* orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237785 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237648 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237785 1069 "q-10009" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237785 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237785 1071 "Find the slope of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237785 1072 #uuid "bc8a355a-ee17-5da1-9a5f-fc0c8e06bcf1" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237785 1074 "To find the slope at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nThis question requires the product rule. Let $u(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x$ and $v(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x.$ Then\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}(x)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x,\\,{v}^{′}(x)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate $u$ and $v.$\n\nNow, applying the product rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)(\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x)+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})|}_{x=\\pi /2} & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237786 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237561 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237786 1069 "q-100091" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237786 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237786 1071 "Consider the following set of polynomials:\n\n$$\n{p}_{1}(t)=t-1,\\,{p}_{2}(t)=2,\\,{p}_{3}(t)=t+1.\n$$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space ${R}_{1}[t]$ of polynomials of degree at most $1$ in the variable $t$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨p(t),q(t)⟩=p(-1)q(-1)+p(1)q(1),\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n- ${p}_{2}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n- $S={{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{3}(t)}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237786 1072 #uuid "e68e3d26-aa65-5eb7-b898-7c00e9b7271e" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237786 1074 "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{3}(t)⟩ & =⟨t-1,t+1⟩ \\\\  & =(t-1){|}_{-1}\\cdot (t+1){|}_{-1}+(t-1){|}_{1}\\cdot (t+1){|}_{1} \\\\  & =(-1-1)\\cdot ((-1)+1)+(1-1)\\cdot (1+1) \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is false since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{3}(t)⟩ & =⟨2,t+1⟩ \\\\  & =(2){|}_{-1}\\cdot (t+1){|}_{-1}+(2){|}_{1}\\cdot (t+1){|}_{1} \\\\  & =(2)\\cdot ((-1)+1)+(2)\\cdot (1+1) \\\\  & =4\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${p}_{2}(t)⊥̸{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${p}_{2}(t)⊥̸{p}_{3}(t),$ the set $S$ is *not* orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237787 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237734 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237787 1069 "q-100114" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237787 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237787 1071 "Consider the following set of polynomials:\n\n$$\n{p}_{1}(t)=2t+1,\\,{p}_{2}(t)=3t,\\,{p}_{3}(t)=t+1.\n$$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space ${R}_{1}[t]$ of polynomials of degree at most $1$ in the variable $t$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨p(t),q(t)⟩=p(0)q(0)+p(-1)q(-1),\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${p}_{1}(t)⊥̸{p}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n- ${p}_{2}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n- $S={{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{3}(t)}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237787 1072 #uuid "ea1a91d4-93f7-5e8d-b2ce-c7694a823cec" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237787 1074 "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t)⟩ & =⟨2t+1,3t⟩ \\\\  & =(2t+1){|}_{0}\\cdot (3t){|}_{0}+(2t+1){|}_{-1}\\cdot (3t){|}_{-1} \\\\  & =(2(0)+1)\\cdot (3(0))+(2(-1)+1)\\cdot (3(-1)) \\\\  & =3\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${p}_{1}(t)⊥̸{p}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{3}(t)⟩ & =⟨3t,t+1⟩ \\\\  & =(3t){|}_{0}\\cdot (t+1){|}_{0}+(3t){|}_{-1}\\cdot (t+1){|}_{-1} \\\\  & =(3(0))\\cdot (0+1)+(3(-1))\\cdot ((-1)+1) \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${p}_{2}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${p}_{1}(t)⊥̸{p}_{2}(t),$ the set $S$ is *not* orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237788 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237537 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237788 1069 "q-100119" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237788 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237788 1071 "Consider the following set of polynomials:\n\n$$\n{p}_{1}(t)=t-2,\\,{p}_{2}(t)=t+2,\\,{p}_{3}(t)=2t+4.\n$$\n\nGiven that the inner product in the vector space ${R}_{1}[t]$ of polynomials of degree at most $1$ in the variable $t$ is defined as \n\n$$\n⟨p(t),q(t)⟩=p(-2)q(-2)+p(2)q(2),\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n- ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product\n- $S={{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{3}(t)}$ is an orthogonal set with respect to the given inner product" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237788 1072 #uuid "0e262e4d-a912-58d1-aa39-b29d3810a43d" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237788 1074 "Recall that a set of vectors in an inner product space is orthogonal if all vectors in the set are mutually orthogonal with respect to the given inner product. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t)⟩ & =⟨t-2,t+2⟩ \\\\  & =(t-2){|}_{-2}\\cdot (t+2){|}_{-2}+(t-2){|}_{2}\\cdot (t+2){|}_{2} \\\\  & =(-2-2)\\cdot ((-2)+2)+(2-2)\\cdot (2+2) \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{2}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{3}(t)⟩ & =⟨t-2,2t+4⟩ \\\\  & =(t-2){|}_{-2}\\cdot (2t+4){|}_{-2}+(t-2){|}_{2}\\cdot (2t+4){|}_{2} \\\\  & =(-2-2)\\cdot (2(-2)+4)+(2-2)\\cdot (2(2)+4) \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${p}_{1}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product.\n\n- Statement III is false. Let's check if ${p}_{2}(t)⊥{p}_{3}(t).$ Computing the inner product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{3}(t)⟩ & =⟨t+2,2t+4⟩ \\\\  & =(t+2){|}_{-2}\\cdot (2t+4){|}_{-2}+(t+2){|}_{2}\\cdot (2t+4){|}_{2} \\\\  & =((-2)+2)\\cdot (2(-2)+4)+(2+2)\\cdot (2(2)+4) \\\\  & =32\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that ${p}_{2}(t)⊥̸{p}_{3}(t)$ with respect to the given inner product. So, the set $S$ is *not* an orthogonal set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237789 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237481 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237789 1069 "q-10025" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237789 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237789 1071 "Find the area bounded between the curve $x=(y-1)^{2},$ the $y$-axis, and the lines $y=0$ and $y=1.$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237789 1072 #uuid "2a9116c0-f205-5e1b-a6e8-af81ec36a619" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237789 1074 "To find the area, we compute the corresponding definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{c}^{d}x(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(y-1)^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({y}^{2}-2y+1)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y-2{\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}|}_{0}^{1}-2\\cdot {\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}|}_{0}^{1}+y{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{3}-0)-2(\\frac{1}{2}-0)+(1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}-1+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237790 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237708 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237790 1069 "q-100284" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237790 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237790 1071 "![](images/4c/4c309c0066e807d9a4bab21c3f1d6d2e967d1d502562f19a6e0f0a26d6c5118e.png)\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to -2}\\frac{(4-3f(x))^{5}}{(6-f(x))^{3}}$ for the function $f(x)$ plotted above." 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237790 1072 #uuid "a26a4563-0d3b-5964-8fb4-446ae19148cf" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237790 1074 "Using the power and quotient rules, we can rewrite the given limit as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -2}\\frac{(4-3f(x))^{5}}{(6-f(x))^{3}} & =\\frac{{({lim}_{x\\to -2}(4-3f(x)))}^{5}}{{({lim}_{x\\to -2}(6-f(x)))}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above is true provided that  ${lim}_{x\\to -2}f(x)$ exists.\n\nWe find from the graph that ${lim}_{x\\to -2}f(x)=2.$ We calculate the limits of the numerator and denominator, separately.\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -2}(4-3f(x))=4-3(2)=-2\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -2}(6-f(x))=6-2=4\n$$\n\nSo we have\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -2}\\frac{(4-3f(x))^{5}}{(6-f(x))^{3}} & =\\frac{{({lim}_{x\\to -2}(4-3f(x)))}^{5}}{{({lim}_{x\\to -2}(6-f(x)))}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-2)^{5}}{{4}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-32}{64} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237791 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237539 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237791 1069 "q-10031" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237791 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237791 1071 "![](images/b7/b77e20a705956f80379158fd88de70f95c94989c6bd7640d1f3c20d8cca7b4f5.png)\n\nFind the area of the finite region bounded between the curve $x=y-{y}^{3}$ and the $y$-axis, as shown above." 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237791 1072 #uuid "6f29bfa0-dc37-515c-81cd-e8b0cf96041d" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237791 1074 "First, we find the $y$-intercepts of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}^{3} & =0 \\\\ y(1-{y}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ y & =0,\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that we want to find the area between the curve $x=y-{y}^{3}$ and the $y$-axis from $y=0$ to $y=1.$ To find the area, we compute the corresponding definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{c}^{d}x(y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(y-{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}|}_{0}^{1}-{\\frac{{y}^{4}}{4}|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{2}-0)-(\\frac{1}{4}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237792 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237459 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237792 1069 "q-100378" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237792 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237792 1071 "${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }2{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237792 1072 #uuid "3de2b1c8-40e7-52d0-981a-62ca7ed3c790" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237792 1074 "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int {e}^{px+q}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{p}{e}^{px+q}+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }2{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}2{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-3x}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{e}^{-3x}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{e}^{3a}}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\frac{1}{{e}^{3x}},$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{e}^{3x}}=\\infty $\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{e}^{3x}}=0$\n\n![](images/45/45e8ddd8ddb33dc8d6a701ed75878271b331ce6b3591ba8fae9f4fa49020c12b.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }2{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{e}^{3a}}-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot (0-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237793 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237678 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237793 1069 "q-10038" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237793 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237793 1071 "Solve the following system of linear equations by finding the inverse of the coefficient matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}z+2y+7x=1 \\\\ 3y-z=-2 \\\\ 4y-2z-3x=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237793 1072 #uuid "062c7a4a-d791-5879-9bf4-0c469a15394c" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237793 1074 "We organize the system so that it’s in the correct order first:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}7x+2y+z=1 \\\\ 3y-z=-2 \\\\ -3x+4y-2z=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to write this system in the form $Ax=b$, where $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $x$ and $b$ are $3\\times 1$ column vectors. We can do this as follows:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}7 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 4 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, the solution to $Ax=b$ is given by $x={A}^{-1}b.$ So, we compute ${A}^{-1}$ as follows:\n\n*Step 1:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}7 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 4 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =7|\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{aligned}|-2|\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{aligned}|+1|\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =7(-6+4)-2(-3)+1(9) \\\\  & =-14+6+9 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 2:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}7 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}7 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}7 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}7 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -3 & 9 \\\\ -8 & -11 & 34 \\\\ -5 & -7 & 21\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 3:*\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 & 9 \\\\ 8 & -11 & -34 \\\\ -5 & 7 & 21\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n*Step 4:*\n\n$$\n{C}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 8 & -5 \\\\ 3 & -11 & 7 \\\\ 9 & -34 & 21\\text{ }\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n*Step 5:*\n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T}=\\frac{1}{1}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 8 & -5 \\\\ 3 & -11 & 7 \\\\ 9 & -34 & 21\\text{ }\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nHence\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 8 & -5 \\\\ 3 & -11 & 7 \\\\ 9 & -34 & 21\\text{ }\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-18 \\\\ 25 \\\\ 77\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence the solution is $x=-18,\\,y=25,\\,z=77.$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237794 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237697 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237794 1069 "q-100424" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237794 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237794 1071 "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }x{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237794 1072 #uuid "6b1ae06b-fd9b-5498-b170-8acb123e5ca6" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237794 1074 "First, we substitute $u={x}^{2}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2x\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u=x\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u={x}^{2}$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }x{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }x{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-{e}^{-u}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[{e}^{-u}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{e}^{a}}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\frac{1}{{e}^{x}},$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{e}^{x}}=\\infty $\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{e}^{x}}=0$\n\n![](images/48/4844d6220d04a6a1fffdd5a485a9e6724aa6065397b8c3a009157a7a2dafc3cf.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }x{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot ({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{e}^{a}}-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (0-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237795 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237651 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237795 1069 "q-100449" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237795 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237795 1071 "If ${8}^{5a-12}=\\frac{1}{{64}^{a+4}}$ then $a=$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237795 1072 #uuid "47c3c6e7-da82-558f-8732-889800a1c1ed" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237795 1074 "To solve this equation, we need both exponential terms to have a common base.\n\nTo do this, we write $64$ as ${8}^{2},$ and then we rewrite the right-hand side using the power rule of exponents:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{8}^{5a-12} & =\\frac{1}{{64}^{a+4}} \\\\ {8}^{5a-12} & =\\frac{1}{{({8}^{2})}^{a+4}} \\\\ {8}^{5a-12} & =\\frac{1}{{8}^{2(a+4)}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we rewrite the right-hand side using a negative exponent: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{8}^{5a-12} & ={8}^{-2(a+4)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since both sides of the equation share the same base $(8)$, we equate the exponents and solve for $a:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5a-12 & =-2(a+4) \\\\ 5a-12 & =-2a-8 \\\\ 7a & =4 \\\\ a & =\\frac{4}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237796 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237519 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237796 1069 "q-10045" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237796 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237796 1071 "${\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237796 1072 #uuid "1f3bfc39-323e-5886-ac57-446859b38bb7" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237796 1074 "Notice that the numerator is proportional to the derivative of the denominator, because\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nSo, let $u=1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$ Differentiating, we have \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nCalculating the limits for $u$ gives\n\n| $x$ | $-1$ | $1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1)$ | $1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1)$ |\n\nWe now substitute and evaluate, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{1}{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1)}^{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1)}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|{|}_{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1)}^{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1)} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1))-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1))\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237797 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237748 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237797 1069 "q-100462" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237797 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237797 1071 "Find the value of $d$ that satisfies the equation ${9}^{d-4}={243}^{d+3}.$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237797 1072 #uuid "91addebc-01e7-5c7d-9c23-4b85b8f8d5e6" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237797 1074 "To solve this equation, we need both exponential terms to have a common base. To do this, we rewrite the equation using $9={3}^{2}$ and $243={3}^{5}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{({3}^{2})}^{d-4}={({3}^{5})}^{d+3}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the power rule of exponents. This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{2(d-4)}={3}^{5(d+3)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since both sides of the equation share the same base $(3)$, we equate the exponents and solve for $d:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(d-4) & =5(d+3) \\\\ 2d-8 & =5d+15 \\\\ -3d & =23 \\\\ d & =-\\frac{23}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237798 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237464 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237798 1069 "q-100468" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237798 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237798 1071 "Find the value of $d$ that satisfies the equation ${25}^{d-7}={625}^{d+10}.$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237798 1072 #uuid "f95496e5-4ca7-5eb2-bb5b-56a9a05ac9d8" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237798 1074 "To solve this equation, we need both exponential terms to have a common base. To do this, we rewrite the equation using $25={5}^{2}$ and $625={5}^{4}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{({5}^{2})}^{d-7}={({5}^{4})}^{d+10}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the power rule of exponents. This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{5}^{2(d-7)}={5}^{4(d+10)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since both sides of the equation share the same base $(5)$, we equate the exponents and solve for $d:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(d-7) & =4(d+10) \\\\ 2d-14 & =4d+40 \\\\ -2d & =54 \\\\ d & =-27\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237799 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237560 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237799 1069 "q-100471" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237799 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237799 1071 "Find the solution of ${3}^{x+5}={7}^{6-2x}.$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237799 1072 #uuid "dd5c6b81-63d3-5a9b-b188-f8be38d27f58" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237799 1074 "First, we take the natural logarithm $(ln)$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{x+5} & ={7}^{6-2x} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({3}^{x+5}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({7}^{6-2x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the power law of logarithms to simplify both sides of the equation. This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+5)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) & =(6-2x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+5)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) & =(6-2x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7) \\\\ x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)+5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) & =6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7)-2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7) \\\\ x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)+2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7) & =6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7)-5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) \\\\ x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7)) & =6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7)-5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) \\\\ x & =\\frac{6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7)-5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7)}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237800 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237755 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237800 1069 "q-100472" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237800 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237800 1071 "Find the solution of ${2}^{x+3}={9}^{8-5x}.$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237800 1072 #uuid "dcc0527a-26f5-59e1-b907-c26cf0c06b40" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237800 1074 "First, we take the natural logarithm $(ln)$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{x+3} & ={9}^{8-5x} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({2}^{x+3}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({9}^{8-5x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the power law of logarithms to simplify both sides of the equation. This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+3)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2) & =(8-5x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+3)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2) & =(8-5x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9) \\\\ x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2) & =8\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9)-5x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9) \\\\ x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+5x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9) & =8\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9)-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2) \\\\ x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9)) & =8\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9)-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2) \\\\ x & =\\frac{8\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9)-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9)}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237801 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237695 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237801 1069 "q-100482" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237801 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237801 1071 "Find the solution of ${4}^{x+7}={5}^{8-4x}.$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237801 1072 #uuid "c5e59768-04fa-52af-900c-149a320b3f72" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237801 1074 "First, we take the natural logarithm $(ln)$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{4}^{x+7} & ={5}^{8-4x} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({4}^{x+7}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({5}^{8-4x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the power law of logarithms to simplify both sides of the equation. This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+7)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4) & =(8-4x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+7)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4) & =(8-4x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5) \\\\ x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)+7\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4) & =8\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)-4x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5) \\\\ x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)+4x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5) & =8\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)-7\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4) \\\\ x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)) & =8\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)-7\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4) \\\\ x & =\\frac{8\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)-7\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237802 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237521 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237802 1069 "q-10049" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237802 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237802 1071 "${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237802 1072 #uuid "87190271-ed83-5382-8782-23fae8c59d6d" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237802 1074 "In this case, we use the double-angle formula for sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,⇒\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\n \n\nLetting $\\theta =4x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡8x & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡8x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{16}\\operatorname{cos}⁡8x{|}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{16}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\pi -\\operatorname{cos}⁡0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{16}(1-1) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237803 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237680 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237803 1069 "q-100569" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237803 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237803 1071 "![](images/37/375647b4e2527841b48da5405effe2f15502f1d972586556796a55bcff441b94.png)\n\nConsider the phase line shown above. Which of the following differential equations could have this phase line?\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-y(y+1)^{2}$\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={y}^{3}-y$\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-y(y+1)(y-2)$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237803 1072 #uuid "74569aa4-36e3-5e4d-99a5-eeb148ed4156" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237803 1074 "First, notice that all three differential equations are of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).\n$$\n\nThe phase line gives us the following information:\n\n- The equilibrium solutions are $y=-1,$ $y=0$ and $y=2.$\n\n- $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-1)\\cup (0,2).$\n\n- $f(y)$ is negative for $y\\in (-1,0)\\cup (2,\\infty ).$\n\nWith this in mind, let's study each of the given equations.\n\n- We start by considering ${y}^{′}=-y(y+1)^{2}.$\n\nIn this case, $f(y)=-y(y+1)^{2},$ and the equilibrium solutions are $y=-1,0$ only. \n\nTherefore, equation I does not have the given phase line.\n\n- Next, we consider ${y}^{′}={y}^{3}-y.$\n\nIn this case, $f(y)=y(y-1)(y+1),$ and the equilibrium solutions are $y=-1,0,1$ only. \n\nTherefore, equation II does not have the given phase line.\n\n- Finally, we consider ${y}^{′}=-y(y+1)(y-2).$\n\nFirst, we see that $f(y)=-y(y+1)(y-2),$ so $f(y)=0$ if $y=-1,0,2.$ So, the equilibrium solutions correspond to those shown in the phase line.\n\nNow, we can construct a sign table for $f(y)$ as follows: \n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-1)$ | $(-1,0)$ | $(0,2)$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y+1$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $-y$ | $+$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $y-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $f(y)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n\nSo, we have that $f(y)$ is negative for $y\\in (-1,0)\\cup (2,\\infty ),$ and $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-1)\\cup (0,2).$ This corresponds to the arrows shown on the phase line.\n\nTherefore, equation III has the given phase line.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237804 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237605 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237804 1069 "q-10061" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237804 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237804 1071 "Calculate $\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237804 1072 #uuid "9d8a7144-2e70-5c2c-b1c3-3e9ae0cdcfc0" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237804 1074 "If we let $u=2x,$ then\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237805 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237687 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237805 1069 "q-10064" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237805 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237805 1071 "Calculate $\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3-9x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237805 1072 #uuid "c7faa25b-953f-5de3-b7b5-5d74cb936a93" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237805 1074 "If we let $u=3-9x,$ then \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-9\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{1}{9}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3-9x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{9}\\,\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{9}\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3-9x)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237806 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237692 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237806 1069 "q-100642" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237806 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237806 1071 "${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{\\sqrt[3]{2z+1}}\\,\\text{d}z=$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237806 1072 #uuid "702f5890-f962-5297-b8db-429117ade5e4" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237806 1074 "We proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{\\sqrt[3]{2z+1}}\\,\\text{d}z & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt[3]{2z+1}}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{3}{2}(2z+1)^{2/3}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[(2z+1)^{2/3}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }((2a+1)^{2/3}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\infty  \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the integral is divergent." 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237807 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237763 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237807 1069 "q-100643" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237807 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237807 1071 "Evaluate ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\sqrt{2-3t}}.$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237807 1072 #uuid "a8508ef3-0736-5531-a2ca-192705b437b6" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237807 1074 "We proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\sqrt{2-3t}} & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-1}\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\sqrt{2-3t}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-\\frac{2}{3}\\sqrt{2-3t}]}_{a}^{-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\sqrt{2+3}-\\sqrt{2-3a}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{3}+\\frac{2}{3}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }\\sqrt{2-3a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{3}+\\infty  \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the integral is divergent." 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237808 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237634 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237808 1069 "q-10069" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237808 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237808 1071 "Find the first four non-zero terms of the Maclaurin expansion of $\\frac{1}{1-{x}^{3}}.$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237808 1072 #uuid "a7aa9158-ce31-5e3b-8eb0-9c7615d7017c" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237808 1074 "The standard Maclaurin expansion for $\\frac{1}{1-x}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{1-x} & =1+x+{x}^{2}+{x}^{3}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReplacing $x$ with ${x}^{3}$ in the above expansion and simplifying gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{1-{x}^{3}} & =1+({x}^{3})+{({x}^{3})}^{2}+{({x}^{3})}^{3}+⋯ \\\\  & =1+{x}^{3}+{x}^{6}+{x}^{9}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237809 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237589 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237809 1069 "q-10074" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237809 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237809 1071 "Solve the following system of linear equations by finding the inverse of the coefficient matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2y+3z=-1-x \\\\ 4y=-5z \\\\ x+6z-3=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237809 1072 #uuid "5a8a38c1-8d83-5c15-8052-a8f7706f20a7" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237809 1074 "We organize the system so that it’s in the correct order first:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}x+2y+3z=-1 \\\\ 4y+5z=0 \\\\ x+6z=3\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to write this system in the form $Ax=b$, where $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $x$ and $b$ are $3\\times 1$ column vectors. We can do this as follows:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, the solution to $Ax=b$ is given by $x={A}^{-1}b.$ So, we compute ${A}^{-1}$ as follows:\n\n*Step 1:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 6\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1|\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}|-2|\\begin{aligned}0 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 6\\end{aligned}|+3|\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1(24)-2(-5)+3(0-4) \\\\  & =24+10-12 \\\\  & =22.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 2:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}0 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 6\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 6\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 5\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}24 & -5 & -4 \\\\ 12 & 3 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 5 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 3:*\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}24 & 5 & -4 \\\\ -12 & 3 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -5 & 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n*Step 4:*\n\n$$\n{C}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}24 & -12 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 3 & -5 \\\\ -4 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n*Step 5:*\n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T}=\\frac{1}{22}[\\begin{aligned}24 & -12 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 3 & -5 \\\\ -4 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nHence\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{22}[\\begin{aligned}24 & -12 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 3 & -5 \\\\ -4 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{22}[\\begin{aligned}-30 \\\\ -20 \\\\ 16\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{15}{11} \\\\ -\\frac{10}{11} \\\\ \\frac{8}{11}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence the solution is $x=-\\frac{15}{11},y=-\\frac{10}{11},z=\\frac{8}{11}.$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237810 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237534 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237810 1069 "q-10075" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237810 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237810 1071 "Solve the following system of linear equations by finding the inverse of the coefficient matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}3z-y+x-5=0 \\\\ y+2x+2z=1 \\\\ z-2x-2y=1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237810 1072 #uuid "e779cc8b-bc48-5f02-aa7a-6ab946e667e0" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237810 1074 "We organize the system so that it’s in the correct order first:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}x-y+3z=5 \\\\ 2x+y+2z=1 \\\\ -2x-2y+z=1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to write this system in the form $Ax=b$, where $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $x$ and $b$ are $3\\times 1$ column vectors. We can do this as follows:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, the solution to $Ax=b$ is given by $x={A}^{-1}b.$ So, we compute ${A}^{-1}$ as follows:\n\n*Step 1:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}|-(-1)|\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}|+3|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1(1+4)+1(2+4)+3(-4+2) \\\\  & =5+6-6 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 2:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 6 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 7 & -4 \\\\ -5 & -4 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 3:*\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -6 & -2 \\\\ -5 & 7 & 4 \\\\ -5 & 4 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n*Step 4:*\n\n$$\n{C}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -5 & -5 \\\\ -6 & 7 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 4 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n*Step 5:*\n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T}=\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}5 & -5 & -5 \\\\ -6 & 7 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 4 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nHence\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}5 & -5 & -5 \\\\ -6 & 7 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 4 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -\\frac{19}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence the solution is $x=3,y=-\\frac{19}{5},z=-\\frac{3}{5}.$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237811 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237714 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237811 1069 "q-10076" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237811 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237811 1071 "Solve the following system of linear equations by finding the inverse of the coefficient matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}4x-2y+3z=0 \\\\ 8x-3y+5z=9 \\\\ 7x-2y+4z=-1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237811 1072 #uuid "e8e08af1-fca9-5664-99a6-119c31e3f669" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237811 1074 "We wish to write this system in the form $Ax=b$, where $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $x$ and $b$ are $3\\times 1$ column vectors. We can do this as follows:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 8 & -3 & 5 \\\\ 7 & -2 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 9 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, the solution to $Ax=b$ is given by $x={A}^{-1}b.$ So, we compute ${A}^{-1}$ as follows:\n\n*Step 1:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 8 & -3 & 5 \\\\ 7 & -2 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =4|\\begin{aligned}-3 & 5 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}|-(-2)|\\begin{aligned}8 & 5 \\\\ 7 & 4\\end{aligned}|+3|\\begin{aligned}8 & -3 \\\\ 7 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =4(-12+10)+2(32-35)+3(-16+21) \\\\  & =-8-6+15 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 2:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}-3 & 5 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}8 & 5 \\\\ 7 & 4\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}8 & -3 \\\\ 7 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ 7 & 4\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ 7 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 5\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ 8 & 5\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ 8 & -3\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -3 & 5 \\\\ -2 & -5 & 6 \\\\ -1 & -4 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 3:*\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 & 5 \\\\ 2 & -5 & -6 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n*Step 4:*\n\n$$\n{C}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -5 & 4 \\\\ 5 & -6 & 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n*Step 5:*\n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T}=\\frac{1}{1}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -5 & 4 \\\\ 5 & -6 & 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nHence\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -5 & 4 \\\\ 5 & -6 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 9 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}19 \\\\ -49 \\\\ -58\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence the solution is $x=19,y=-49,z=-58.$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237812 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237622 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237812 1069 "q-100781" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237812 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237812 1071 "What is the product of solutions of the equation ${x}^{2}\\cdot {7}^{x-3}-8x\\cdot {7}^{x-3}+12\\cdot {7}^{x-3}=0?$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237812 1072 #uuid "19bca5be-bffd-5da5-b2f8-88bf9c5fec74" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237812 1074 "First, we factor the equation:\n\n  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot {7}^{x-3}-8x\\cdot {7}^{x-3}+12\\cdot {7}^{x-3} & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-8x+12){7}^{x-3} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-8x+12=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{7}^{x-3}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation ${x}^{2}-8x+12=0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n(x-2)(x-6)=0,\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=2,6.$\n\n- The equation ${7}^{x-3}=0$ has no solutions because ${7}^{x-3}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, the original equation has the solutions $x=2,6,$ and their product is $2\\cdot 6=12.$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237813 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237752 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237813 1069 "q-100785" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237813 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237813 1071 "What is the product of solutions of the equation ${x}^{2}{e}^{9x}-36{e}^{9x}=0?$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237813 1072 #uuid "731d88c0-04ba-5bfa-af89-56694fda5912" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237813 1074 "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{e}^{9x}-36{e}^{9x} & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-36){e}^{9x} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-36=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{e}^{9x}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation ${x}^{2}-36=0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n(x+6)(x-6)=0,\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=-6,6.$\n\n- The equation ${e}^{9x}=0$ has no solutions because ${e}^{9x}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, the original equation has the solutions $x=-6,6,$ and their product is $(-6)\\cdot 6=-36.$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237814 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237544 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237814 1069 "q-100797" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237814 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237814 1071 "What is the product of solutions of the equation $5{x}^{2}\\cdot {8}^{x}=13x\\cdot {8}^{x}+6\\cdot {8}^{x}?$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237814 1072 #uuid "39d52e4f-2b82-55ff-af5e-28f93f87917e" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237814 1074 "First, we factor the equation:\n\n  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{x}^{2}\\cdot {8}^{x} & =13x\\cdot {8}^{x}+6\\cdot {8}^{x} \\\\ 5{x}^{2}\\cdot {8}^{x}-13x\\cdot {8}^{x}-6\\cdot {8}^{x} & =0 \\\\ (5{x}^{2}-13x-6)\\cdot {8}^{x} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n5{x}^{2}-13x-6=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{8}^{x}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation $5{x}^{2}-13x-6=0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n(5x+2)(x-3)=0,\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=-\\frac{2}{5},3.$\n\n- The equation ${8}^{x}=0$ has no solutions because ${8}^{x}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, the original equation has the solutions $x=-\\frac{2}{5},3,$ and their product is $(-\\frac{2}{5})\\cdot 3=-\\frac{6}{5}.$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237815 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237643 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237815 1069 "q-100997" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237815 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237815 1071 "Let $d=9i+7j+4k,$ $e=-i+2k$ and $f=i+7j-9k.$ Find $d-(3e+f).$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237815 1072 #uuid "e885ac8e-f3bb-53b3-a246-2803868db421" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237815 1074 "To compute $d-(3e+f),$ we just need to perform the required operations with each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d-(3e+f) & =9i+7j+4k-(3(-i+2k)+(i+7j-9k)) \\\\  & =9i+7j+4k-((-3i+6k)+(i+7j-9k)) \\\\  & =9i+7j+4k-(-2i+7j-3k) \\\\  & =11i+7k\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237816 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237569 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237816 1069 "q-10100" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237816 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237816 1071 "Solve the following system of linear equations by finding the inverse of the coefficient matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2x+3y+z=6 \\\\ 4x+7y+2z=0 \\\\ 3x+y+z=1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237816 1072 #uuid "c7f748e9-7cb4-573b-a324-159e0e3861ab" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237816 1074 "We wish to write this system in the form $Ax=b$, where $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $x$ and $b$ are $3\\times 1$ column vectors. We can do this as follows:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 7 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, the solution to $Ax=b$ is given by $x={A}^{-1}b.$ So, we compute ${A}^{-1}$ as follows:\n\n*Step 1:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 7 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2|\\begin{aligned}7 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|-3|\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}|+1|\\begin{aligned}4 & 7 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2(7-2)-3(-2)+1(4-21) \\\\  & =10+6-17 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 2:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}7 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}4 & 7 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 7 & 2\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 7\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 & -17 \\\\ 2 & -1 & -7 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 3:*\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 & -17 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 7 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n*Step 4:*\n\n$$\n{C}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -17 & 7 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n*Step 5:*\n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T}=-\\frac{1}{1}[\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -17 & 7 & 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 17 & -7 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nHence\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 17 & -7 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-29 \\\\ -12 \\\\ 100\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence the solution is $x=-29,y=-12,z=100.$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237817 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237749 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237817 1069 "q-101003" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237817 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237817 1071 "Let $A(5,0,9)$ and $B(3,9,8).$ Find $|a-b|,$ where $a$ and $b$ are position vectors of $A$ and $B$, respectively." 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237817 1072 #uuid "8bbb2b6e-0b95-5606-bafd-7bf576ba737f" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237817 1074 "We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\overset{\\to }{OA}=a=5i+9k, \\\\  & \\overset{\\to }{OB}=b=3i+9j+8k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the coordinates of $a-b$, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a-b & =(5i+9k)-(3i+9j+8k) \\\\  & =2i-9j+k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|a-b| & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-9)^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+81+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{86}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237818 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237515 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237818 1069 "q-10101" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237818 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237818 1071 "Solve the following system of linear equations by finding the inverse of the coefficient matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}x-z=0 \\\\ x+y+3z=2 \\\\ 3x-2y+z=4\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237818 1072 #uuid "a8cc406f-67fe-55bc-b55e-b6858ce9be1b" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237818 1074 "We wish to write this system in the form $Ax=b$, where $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $x$ and $b$ are $3\\times 1$ column vectors. We can do this as follows:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, the solution to $Ax=b$ is given by $x={A}^{-1}b.$ So, we compute ${A}^{-1}$ as follows:\n\n*Step 1:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1|\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}|-0|\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}|-1|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1(1-3(-2))+0-1(-2-3) \\\\  & =1+6+2+3 \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 2:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & -8 & -5 \\\\ -2 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 3:*\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 8 & -5 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -4 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n*Step 4:*\n\n$$\n{C}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 8 & 4 & -4 \\\\ -5 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n*Step 5:*\n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T}=\\frac{1}{12}[\\begin{aligned}7 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 8 & 4 & -4 \\\\ -5 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nHence\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}[\\begin{aligned}7 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 8 & 4 & -4 \\\\ -5 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence the solution is $x=\\frac{2}{3},y=-\\frac{2}{3},z=\\frac{2}{3}.$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237819 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237715 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237819 1069 "q-101015" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237819 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237819 1071 "Given that $c=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$, find a unit vector that is parallel to $c.$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237819 1072 #uuid "59367f68-a841-50e9-bd5f-679eeaf283d9" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237819 1074 "Remember that to find a unit vector that is parallel to a given vector, we need to divide the given vector by its length:\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{c}{|\\,c\\,|}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\,c\\,| & =\\sqrt{{c}_{x}^{2}+{c}_{y}^{2}+{c}_{z}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide $c$ by its length and get the following unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =\\frac{c}{|\\,c\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1}c \\\\  & =1\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237820 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237702 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237820 1069 "q-10102" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237820 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237820 1071 "Solve the following system of linear equations by finding the inverse of the coefficient matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2y+2z=1 \\\\ x+y+z=3 \\\\ y+2z=-3\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237820 1072 #uuid "267865d4-e8c6-50f1-8813-4866781fbd07" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237820 1074 "We wish to write this system in the form $Ax=b$, where $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $x$ and $b$ are $3\\times 1$ column vectors. We can do this as follows:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, the solution to $Ax=b$ is given by $x={A}^{-1}b.$ So, we compute ${A}^{-1}$ as follows:\n\n*Step 1:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =0|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}|-2|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}|+2|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =0-2(2)+2(1) \\\\  & =-4+2 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 2:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 3:*\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n*Step 4:*\n\n$$\n{C}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n*Step 5:*\n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T}=-\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nHence\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 8 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ 4 \\\\ -\\frac{7}{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence the solution is $x=\\frac{5}{2},y=4,z=-\\frac{7}{2}.$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237821 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237736 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237821 1069 "q-101288" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237821 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237821 1071 "${lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{x-2}{\\sqrt{3x+1}-\\sqrt{7}}=$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237821 1072 #uuid "6df53e19-d535-55b0-b903-86ff970515ef" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237821 1074 "If we attempt to evaluate the limit by simply evaluating the function at $x=2$, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{x-2}{\\sqrt{3x+1}-\\sqrt{7}} & =\\frac{2-2}{\\sqrt{3(2)+1}-\\sqrt{7}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2-2}{\\sqrt{7}-\\sqrt{7}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the denominator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the denominator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{x-2}{\\sqrt{3x+1}-\\sqrt{7}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{(x-2)(\\sqrt{3x+1}+\\sqrt{7})}{(\\sqrt{3x+1}-\\sqrt{7})(\\sqrt{3x+1}+\\sqrt{7})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{(x-2)(\\sqrt{3x+1}+\\sqrt{7})}{(\\sqrt{3x+1})^{2}-(\\sqrt{7})^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{(x-2)(\\sqrt{3x+1}+\\sqrt{7})}{3x+1-7} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{(x-2)(\\sqrt{3x+1}+\\sqrt{7})}{3x-6} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{(x-2)(\\sqrt{3x+1}+\\sqrt{7})}{3(x-2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{\\sqrt{3x+1}+\\sqrt{7}}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3(2)+1}+\\sqrt{7}}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{7}+\\sqrt{7}}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{7}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237822 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237707 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237822 1069 "q-101310" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237822 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237822 1071 "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y,z)=3-y+5z$ on the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=26.$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237822 1072 #uuid "4df6773c-7996-5e96-920f-edaf9d38b1f2" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237822 1074 "We need to find the minimum value of $f$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=26.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y,z)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y,z)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z),\\,g(x,y,z)=26\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y,z),{f}_{y}(x,y,z),{f}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y,z),{g}_{y}(x,y,z),{g}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ \\\\ ⟨0,-1,5⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y,2z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 0,$ we have the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}0=2\\lambda x \\\\ -1=2\\lambda y \\\\ 5=2\\lambda z\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}x=0 \\\\ y=-\\frac{1}{2\\lambda } \\\\ z=\\frac{5}{2\\lambda }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $x,\\,y$ and $z$ into the condition $g(x,y,z)=26$ and solve for $\\lambda ,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =26 \\\\ 0+\\frac{1}{4{\\lambda }^{2}}+\\frac{25}{4{\\lambda }^{2}} & =26 \\\\ \\frac{26}{4{\\lambda }^{2}} & =26 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\pm \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find $y$ and $z.$ This gives\n\n$$\ny=∓1\\,z=\\pm 5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points \n\n$$\n(0,-1,5),\\,(0,1,-5).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $\\,(x,y,z)\\,$ | $\\,(0,-1,5)\\,$ | $\\,(0,1,-5)\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,f(x,y,z)\\,$ | $\\,29\\,$ | $\\,-23\\,$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $(0,1,-5),$ and its minimum value is $-23.$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237823 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237631 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237823 1069 "q-101312" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237823 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237823 1071 "${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{3}-x}{{x}^{2}-3}=$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237823 1072 #uuid "d1c1c46c-a871-5ff0-9e6c-d412da5bcf59" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237823 1074 "First, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{2}.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{3}-x}{{x}^{2}-3} & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{(x-\\frac{1}{x})}{(1-\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{x-0}{1-0} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }x \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237824 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237498 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237824 1069 "q-101316" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237824 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237824 1071 "${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2{x}^{6}+7{x}^{3}-9}{2{x}^{3}-1}=$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237824 1072 #uuid "13e1ae6a-783c-594e-b16d-9b94ff980ccc" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237824 1074 "First, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{3}.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2{x}^{6}+7{x}^{3}-9}{2{x}^{3}-1} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{2{x}^{6}}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{7{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{9}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(2{x}^{3}+7-\\frac{9}{{x}^{3}})}{(2-\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2{x}^{3}+7-0}{2-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(2{x}^{3}+7) \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237825 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237732 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237825 1069 "q-101319" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237825 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237825 1071 "Find the horizontal asymptote of $y=f(x)$ where $f(x)=\\frac{2{x}^{2}+x-1}{3x+2}.$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237825 1072 #uuid "ee5c05dd-afd4-5a6c-96ce-78c0f481e26e" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237825 1074 "The horizontal asymptote is $y={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is $x.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2{x}^{2}+x-1}{3x+2} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{x}+\\frac{x}{x}-\\frac{1}{x})}{(\\frac{3x}{x}+\\frac{2}{x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(2x+1-\\frac{1}{x})}{(3+\\frac{2}{x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(2x+1-0)}{(3+0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(2x+1) \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe limit does not come out to a finite number. Therefore, there is no horizontal asymptote." 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237826 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237516 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237826 1069 "q-101321" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237826 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237826 1071 "Find the horizontal asymptote of $y=f(x)$ where $f(x)=\\frac{8{x}^{4}+x-2}{6x-5}.$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237826 1072 #uuid "c64f5a85-aa62-5620-809a-0476c074fff3" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237826 1074 "The horizontal asymptote is $y={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x).$ To find the horizontal asymptote, we apply the usual procedure of dividing the numerator and denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is $x.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{8{x}^{4}+x-2}{6x-5} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{8{x}^{4}}{x}+\\frac{x}{x}-\\frac{2}{x})}{(\\frac{6x}{x}-\\frac{5}{x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(8{x}^{3}+1-\\frac{2}{x})}{(6-\\frac{5}{x})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(8{x}^{3}+1-0)}{(6-0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(8{x}^{3}+1) \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe limit does not come out to a finite number. Therefore, there is no horizontal asymptote." 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237827 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237640 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237827 1069 "q-101381" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237827 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237827 1071 "Let $y={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}$ be a series solution to the equation $3{y}^{′}-4y=0.$ \n\nWhat is the recurrence relation for the coefficients ${a}_{n},$ where $n=0,1,2,…?$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237827 1072 #uuid "a722028d-f5b8-59f8-94f1-217a3ab7dcf8" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237827 1074 "First, we find ${y}^{′}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & ={({\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n})}^{′} \\\\  & ={({a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n})}^{′} \\\\  & =0+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we substitute expressions for $y(x)$ and ${y}^{′}(x)$ into the given differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1}-4{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to make the powers of $x$ the same. Therefore, in the first sum, we change the index of the summation by writing $m=n-1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{\\sum }_{m=0}^{\\infty }(m+1){a}_{m+1}{x}^{m}-4{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReplacing $m$ with $n$ in the first summation (since it does not matter which letter we use) gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(n+1){a}_{n+1}{x}^{n}-4{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince both sums now start with $n=0,$ we can combine them as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }[3(n+1){a}_{n+1}-4{a}_{n}]{x}^{n} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting each coefficient of $x$ equal to zero, we obtain\n\n$$\n3(n+1){a}_{n+1}-4{a}_{n}=0.\n$$\n\nFinally, the recursion formula is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}=\\frac{4{a}_{n}}{3(n+1)},\\,n=0,1,2,….\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237828 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237545 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237828 1069 "q-101385" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237828 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237828 1071 "Let $y={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}$ be a series solution to the equation $3{y}^{″}-2y=0.$  Find $f(n),$ given that the recurrence relation for ${a}_{n}$ can be written as\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+2}=\\frac{{a}_{n}}{f(n)},\\,n=0,1,2…\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237828 1072 #uuid "1758a5b8-1f66-5511-a42b-0971bcd5d3a4" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237828 1074 "First, we find ${y}^{′}(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & ={({\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n})}^{′} \\\\  & ={({a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n})}^{′} \\\\  & =0+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we find ${y}^{″}(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″}(x) & ={({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1})}^{′} \\\\  & ={({a}_{1}+{\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1})}^{′} \\\\  & =0+{\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }n(n-1){a}_{n}{x}^{n-2} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }n(n-1){a}_{n}{x}^{n-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we substitute the expressions for $y(x)$ and ${y}^{″}(x)$ into the given differential equation:\n\n$$\n3{\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }n(n-1){a}_{n}{x}^{n-2}-2{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}=0\n$$\n\nWe want to make the powers of $x$ the same. Therefore, in the first sum, we change the index of the summation by writing $m=n-2.$ This gives\n\n$$\n3{\\sum }_{m=0}^{\\infty }(m+2)(m+1){a}_{m+2}{x}^{m}-2{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}=0.\n$$\n\nReplacing $m$ with $n$ in the first summation (since it does not matter which letter we use) gives\n\n$$\n3{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(n+2)(n+1){a}_{n+2}{x}^{n}-2{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}=0.\n$$\n\nSince both sums now start with $n=0,$ we can combine them as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }[3(n+2)(n+1){a}_{n+2}-2{a}_{n}]{x}^{n}=0\n$$\n\nSetting each coefficient of $x$ equal to zero, we obtain\n\n$$\n3(n+2)(n+1){a}_{n+2}-2{a}_{n}=0.\n$$\n\nFinally then, the recursion formula is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+2}=\\frac{2{a}_{n}}{3(n+1)(n+2)},\\,n=0,1,2…\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(n)=\\frac{3(n+1)(n+2)}{2}.$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237829 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237669 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237829 1069 "q-101389" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237829 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237829 1071 "Let $y={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}$ be a series solution to the equation $18{y}^{″}-9y=0.$  Find $f(n),$ given that the recurrence relation for ${a}_{n}$ can be written as\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+2}=\\frac{{a}_{n}}{f(n)},\\,n=0,1,2…\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237829 1072 #uuid "08c526e0-cfb6-56c7-8371-fe98abe5db60" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237829 1074 "First, we find ${y}^{′}(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & ={({\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n})}^{′} \\\\  & ={({a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n})}^{′} \\\\  & =0+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we find ${y}^{″}(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″}(x) & ={({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1})}^{′} \\\\  & ={({a}_{1}+{\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1})}^{′} \\\\  & =0+{\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }n(n-1){a}_{n}{x}^{n-2} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }n(n-1){a}_{n}{x}^{n-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we substitute the expressions for $y(x)$ and ${y}^{″}(x)$ into the given differential equation:\n\n$$\n18{\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }n(n-1){a}_{n}{x}^{n-2}-9{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}=0\n$$\n\nWe want to make the powers of $x$ the same. Therefore, in the first sum, we change the index of the summation by writing $m=n-2.$ This gives\n\n$$\n18{\\sum }_{m=0}^{\\infty }(m+2)(m+1){a}_{m+2}{x}^{m}-9{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}=0.\n$$\n\nReplacing $m$ with $n$ in the first summation (since it does not matter which letter we use) gives\n\n$$\n18{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(n+2)(n+1){a}_{n+2}{x}^{n}-9{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}=0.\n$$\n\nSince both sums now start with $n=0,$ we can combine them as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }[18(n+2)(n+1){a}_{n+2}-9{a}_{n}]{x}^{n}=0\n$$\n\nSetting each coefficient of $x$ equal to zero, we obtain\n\n$$\n18(n+2)(n+1){a}_{n+2}-9{a}_{n}=0.\n$$\n\nFinally then, the recursion formula is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+2}=\\frac{{a}_{n}}{2(n+1)(n+2)},\\,n=0,1,2…\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(n)=2(n+1)(n+2).$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237830 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237737 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237830 1069 "q-101402" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237830 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237830 1071 "Let $y={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}$ be a series solution to the equation $6{y}^{′}+3xy=0.$\n\nWhat is the recurrence relation for the coefficients ${a}_{n},$ where $n=1,2,3,…$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237830 1072 #uuid "87f67649-c372-5e7a-bd3f-54664d608fc3" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237830 1074 "First, we find ${y}^{′}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & ={({\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n})}^{′} \\\\  & ={({a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n})}^{′} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we substitute the expressions for $y(x)$ and ${y}^{′}(x)$ into the given differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1}+3x{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n} & =0 \\\\ {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }6n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1}+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }3{a}_{n}{x}^{n+1} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to make the powers of $x$ the same. Therefore, in the first sum, we change the index of the summation by writing $m=n-1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{m=0}^{\\infty }6(m+1){a}_{m+1}{x}^{m}+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }3{a}_{n}{x}^{n+1}=0.\n$$\n\nNow, in the second series, we change the index of the summation by writing $m=n+1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{m=0}^{\\infty }6(m+1){a}_{m+1}{x}^{m}+{\\sum }_{m=1}^{\\infty }3{a}_{m-1}{x}^{m}=0.\n$$\n\nReplacing $m$ with $n$ in both summations (since it does not matter which letter we use) gives\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }6(n+1){a}_{n+1}{x}^{n}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }3{a}_{n-1}{x}^{n}=0.\n$$\n\nNext, we need to get two series starting with the same value of $n.$ So, we take out the term $n=0$ from the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n6{a}_{1}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }6(n+1){a}_{n+1}{x}^{n}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }3{a}_{n-1}{x}^{n}=0\n$$\n\nWe now combine the series, as follows:\n\n$$\n6{a}_{1}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }[6(n+1){a}_{n+1}+3{a}_{n-1}]{x}^{n}=0\n$$\n\nFinally, by equating coefficients of ${x}^{n},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=0,\\,6(n+1){a}_{n+1}=-3{a}_{n-1}.\n$$\n\nSolving for ${a}_{n+1}$ gives\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}=-\\frac{{a}_{n-1}}{2(n+1)},\\,n=1,2,3,….\n$$\n\nNote that every coefficient with an odd index is equal to $0,$ since ${a}_{1}=0.$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237831 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237773 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237831 1069 "q-101403" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237831 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237831 1071 "Let $y={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}$ be a series solution to the equation $5{y}^{′}-2xy=0.$\n\nWhat is the recurrence relation for the coefficients ${a}_{n},$ where $n=1,2,3,…$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237831 1072 #uuid "42c9a9d2-94f2-5cb3-8d2d-b2a2cb1e1c8f" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237831 1074 "First, we find ${y}^{′}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & ={({\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n})}^{′} \\\\  & ={({a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n})}^{′} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we substitute the expressions for $y(x)$ and ${y}^{′}(x)$ into the given differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1}-2x{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n} & =0 \\\\ {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }5n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1}-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }2{a}_{n}{x}^{n+1} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to make the powers of $x$ the same. Therefore, in the first sum, we change the index of the summation by writing $m=n-1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{m=0}^{\\infty }5(m+1){a}_{m+1}{x}^{m}-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }2{a}_{n}{x}^{n+1}=0.\n$$\n\nNow, in the second series, we change the index of the summation by writing $m=n+1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{m=0}^{\\infty }5(m+1){a}_{m+1}{x}^{m}-{\\sum }_{m=1}^{\\infty }2{a}_{m-1}{x}^{m}=0.\n$$\n\nReplacing $m$ with $n$ in both summations (since it does not matter which letter we use) gives\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }5(n+1){a}_{n+1}{x}^{n}-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }2{a}_{n-1}{x}^{n}=0.\n$$\n\nNext, we need to get two series starting with the same value of $n.$ So, we take out the term $n=0$ from the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n5{a}_{1}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }5(n+1){a}_{n+1}{x}^{n}-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }2{a}_{n-1}{x}^{n}=0\n$$\n\nWe now combine the series, as follows:\n\n$$\n5{a}_{1}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }[5(n+1){a}_{n+1}-2{a}_{n-1}]{x}^{n}=0\n$$\n\nFinally, by equating coefficients of ${x}^{n},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=0,\\,5(n+1){a}_{n+1}=2{a}_{n-1}.\n$$\n\nSolving for ${a}_{n+1}$ gives\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}=\\frac{2{a}_{n-1}}{5(n+1)},\\,n=1,2,3,….\n$$\n\nNote that every coefficient with an odd index is equal to $0,$ since ${a}_{1}=0.$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237832 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237499 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237832 1069 "q-101406" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237832 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237832 1071 "Let $p=⟨1,-2⟩$ and $q=⟨4,-1⟩.$ Find $p\\cdot q.$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237832 1072 #uuid "424bd239-6062-5739-8f7b-4979cfdd4107" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237832 1074 "Summing up the products of corresponding components, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\cdot q & ={p}_{x}\\cdot {q}_{x}+{p}_{y}\\cdot {q}_{y} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 4+(-2)\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =4+2 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237833 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237527 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237833 1069 "q-101417" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237833 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237833 1071 "Let $u=[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 2x\\end{aligned}]$ and $v=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ x\\end{aligned}].$ Given that $u⊥v$ and $x<0$, find the value of $x.$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237833 1072 #uuid "dfd0ffab-79f2-5643-9530-8f55bce579c3" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237833 1074 "We know that $u⊥v$ ($u$ and $v$ are perpendicular) if and only if $u\\cdot v=0.$\n\nComputing the dot product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot v & ={u}_{x}\\cdot {v}_{x}+{u}_{y}\\cdot {v}_{y} \\\\  & =9\\cdot (-2)+2x\\cdot x \\\\  & =-18+2{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot v & =0 \\\\ -18+2{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2} & =18 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =9 \\\\ x & =\\pm 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe disregard the positive solution since we are told that $x<0.$ Therefore, $x=-3.$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237834 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237566 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237834 1069 "q-101501" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237834 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237834 1071 "Find the angle between $a=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Round your answer to $1$ decimal place." 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237834 1072 #uuid "1c0bf9dc-454a-56a5-bcdd-445536550323" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237834 1074 "Rearranging the dot product formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{a\\cdot b}{|\\,a\\,||\\,b\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{a}_{x}{b}_{x}+{a}_{y}{b}_{y}}{\\sqrt{{a}_{x}^{2}+{a}_{y}^{2}}\\sqrt{{b}_{x}^{2}+{b}_{y}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot 4+(-6)\\cdot 1}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-6)^{2}}\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{37}\\cdot \\sqrt{17}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{629}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{629}})≈{94.6}^{∘}.\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237835 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237513 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237835 1069 "q-101502" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237835 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237835 1071 "Find the angle between $a=⟨7,2⟩$ and $b=⟨0,6⟩.$ Round your answer to $1$ decimal place." 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237835 1072 #uuid "4e426e37-8c07-5ee5-8ef8-255e5e023aff" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237835 1074 "Rearranging the dot product formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{a\\cdot b}{|\\,a\\,||\\,b\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{a}_{x}{b}_{x}+{a}_{y}{b}_{y}}{\\sqrt{{a}_{x}^{2}+{a}_{y}^{2}}\\sqrt{{b}_{x}^{2}+{b}_{y}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\cdot 0+2\\cdot 6}{\\sqrt{{7}^{2}+{2}^{2}}\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{6}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{\\sqrt{53}\\cdot \\sqrt{36}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{53}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{53}})≈{74.1}^{∘}.\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237836 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237594 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237836 1069 "q-101504" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237836 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237836 1071 "Find the angle between $a=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$ Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237836 1072 #uuid "36bc5a94-57a6-56e7-857e-178854ad126d" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237836 1074 "Rearranging the dot product formula, we have: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{a\\cdot b}{|\\,a\\,||\\,b\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{a}_{x}{b}_{x}+{a}_{y}{b}_{y}+{a}_{z}{b}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{a}_{x}^{2}+{a}_{y}^{2}+{a}_{z}^{2}}\\sqrt{{b}_{x}^{2}+{b}_{y}^{2}+{b}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-1)\\cdot 4+4\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 3}{\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{4}^{2}+{2}^{2}}\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{3}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{21}\\cdot \\sqrt{26}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{546}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{546}})≈{75.12}^{∘}.\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237837 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237738 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237837 1069 "q-101505" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237837 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237837 1071 "Find the angle between $a=[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].$ Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237837 1072 #uuid "5bb24474-02b6-5a79-8993-e3afc25833c1" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237837 1074 "Rearranging the dot product formula, we have: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{a\\cdot b}{|\\,a\\,||\\,b\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{a}_{x}{b}_{x}+{a}_{y}{b}_{y}+{a}_{z}{b}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{a}_{x}^{2}+{a}_{y}^{2}+{a}_{z}^{2}}\\sqrt{{b}_{x}^{2}+{b}_{y}^{2}+{b}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\cdot 3+0\\cdot 6+6\\cdot (-2)}{\\sqrt{{8}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{6}^{2}}\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{6}^{2}+(-2)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{\\sqrt{100}\\cdot \\sqrt{49}} \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{10\\cdot 7} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{35}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{6}{35})≈{80.13}^{∘}.\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237838 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237660 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237838 1069 "q-101506" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237838 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237838 1071 "Find $∠QPR$ given the points $P(1,5,-1)$, $Q(5,1,1)$, and $R(2,5,0).$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237838 1072 #uuid "4443b852-41a5-5a76-994c-081bd2938800" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237838 1074 "Note that the position vectors of the points $P$, $Q$, $R$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p=⟨1,5,-1⟩,\\,q=⟨5,1,1⟩,\\,r=⟨2,5,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $△PQR$ is shown (schematically) in the diagram below, where we denote $∠QPR=\\theta .$ \n\n![](images/30/3003f0cb329a8f8ef9bc315c70df4773ac1f0c54843dea1208c68bd0f6227bd1.png)\n\nFirst, we find the components of $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =q-p \\\\  & =⟨5,1,1⟩-⟨1,5,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4,-4,2⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{PR} & =r-p \\\\  & =⟨2,5,0⟩-⟨1,5,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,0,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the cosine of the angle between $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{PR}}{|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}||\\overset{\\to }{PR}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\cdot 1+(-4)\\cdot 0+2\\cdot 1}{\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+(-4)^{2}+{2}^{2}}\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{36}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}})={45}^{∘}.\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237839 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237756 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237839 1069 "q-101507" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237839 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237839 1071 "Find $∠QPR$ given the points $P(3,3,-5)$, $Q(4,2,-3)$, and $R(7,5,-3).$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237839 1072 #uuid "6125d20d-d091-5df9-9c87-5b9a645bb5dd" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237839 1074 "Note that the position vectors of the points $P$, $Q$, $R$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p=⟨3,3,-5⟩,\\,q=⟨4,2,-3⟩,\\,r=⟨7,5,-3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $△PQR$ is shown (schematically) in the diagram below, where we denote $∠QPR=\\theta .$ \n\n![](images/30/3003f0cb329a8f8ef9bc315c70df4773ac1f0c54843dea1208c68bd0f6227bd1.png)\n\nFirst, we find the components of $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =q-p \\\\  & =⟨4,2,-3⟩-⟨3,3,-5⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,-1,2⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{PR} & =r-p \\\\  & =⟨7,5,-3⟩-⟨3,3,-5⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4,2,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the cosine of the angle between $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{PR}}{|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}||\\overset{\\to }{PR}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot 4+(-1)\\cdot 2+2\\cdot 2}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{2}^{2}}\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{6}\\cdot \\sqrt{24}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})={60}^{∘}.\n$$" 1