the scalar projection of $a$ onto $b.$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237850 1072 #uuid "c9e0b18d-47ac-57ac-a417-f1e364d929df" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237850 1074 "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a & =\\frac{a\\cdot b}{|\\,b\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{a}_{x}{b}_{x}+{a}_{y}{b}_{y}+{a}_{z}{b}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{b}_{x}^{2}+{b}_{y}^{2}+{b}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\cdot 4+(-1)\\cdot 4+(-1)\\cdot 2}{\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{4}^{2}+{2}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{36}} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237851 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237570 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237851 1069 "q-101527" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237851 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237851 1071 "Let $p=⟨3,2,1⟩$ and $q=⟨1,-1,1⟩.$ Find the scalar projection of $p$ onto $q.$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237851 1072 #uuid "4a8ce835-c1ae-59af-9549-b0aa4dd68f54" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237851 1074 "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{q}\\,p & =\\frac{p\\cdot q}{|\\,q\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{p}_{x}{q}_{x}+{p}_{y}{q}_{y}+{p}_{z}{q}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{q}_{x}^{2}+{q}_{y}^{2}+{q}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\cdot 1+2\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot 1}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237852 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237754 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237852 1069 "q-101528" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237852 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237852 1071 "Let $a=⟨2,-1,2⟩$ and $b=⟨6,2,-3⟩.$ Find ${\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a-{\\text{comp}}_{a}\\,b.$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237852 1072 #uuid "144f5038-f97a-55ee-aff4-8a719828b869" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237852 1074 "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a & =\\frac{a\\cdot b}{|\\,b\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{a}_{x}{b}_{x}+{a}_{y}{b}_{y}+{a}_{z}{b}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{b}_{x}^{2}+{b}_{y}^{2}+{b}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot 6+(-1)\\cdot 2+2\\cdot (-3)}{\\sqrt{{6}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-3)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{49}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{a}\\,b & =\\frac{b\\cdot a}{|\\,a\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{a}_{x}{b}_{x}+{a}_{y}{b}_{y}+{a}_{z}{b}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{a}_{x}^{2}+{a}_{y}^{2}+{a}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{2}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{9}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a-{\\text{comp}}_{a}\\,b & =\\frac{4}{7}-\\frac{4}{3} \\\\  & =4(\\frac{1}{7}-\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =4(\\frac{3}{21}-\\frac{7}{21}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{16}{21}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237853 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237532 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237853 1069 "q-101529" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237853 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237853 1071 "Let $p=⟨1,\\sqrt{6},3⟩$ and $q=⟨-7,\\sqrt{6},3⟩.$ Find ${\\text{comp}}_{q}\\,p+{\\text{comp}}_{p}\\,q.$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237853 1072 #uuid "d1b1c513-b8bc-52c7-b4e0-fc1349d07866" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237853 1074 "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{q}\\,p & =\\frac{p\\cdot q}{|\\,q\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{p}_{x}{q}_{x}+{p}_{y}{q}_{y}+{p}_{z}{q}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{q}_{x}^{2}+{q}_{y}^{2}+{q}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot (-7)+\\sqrt{6}\\cdot \\sqrt{6}+3\\cdot 3}{\\sqrt{(-7)^{2}+{\\sqrt{6}}^{2}+{3}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{64}} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{p}\\,q & =\\frac{p\\cdot q}{|\\,p\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{p}_{x}{q}_{x}+{p}_{y}{q}_{y}+{p}_{z}{q}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{p}_{x}^{2}+{p}_{y}^{2}+{p}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{\\sqrt{6}}^{2}+{3}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{16}} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{q}\\,p+{\\text{comp}}_{p}\\,q & =1+2 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237854 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237585 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237854 1069 "q-101541" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237854 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237854 1071 "Let $a=[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 7 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$ Find the vector projection of $a$ onto $b.$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237854 1072 #uuid "9c17c0ad-57b9-5e4e-96cf-a2591d364b0a" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237854 1074 "Using the formula for the vector projection, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{b}\\,a & =\\frac{a\\cdot b}{b\\cdot b}b \\\\  & =\\frac{{a}_{x}{b}_{x}+{a}_{y}{b}_{y}+{a}_{z}{b}_{z}}{{b}_{x}^{2}+{b}_{y}^{2}+{b}_{z}^{2}}b \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\cdot 0+7\\cdot 2+(-4)\\cdot 3}{{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{3}^{2}}b \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{13}⟨0,2,3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\frac{4}{13},\\frac{6}{13}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237855 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237733 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237855 1069 "q-101587" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237855 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237855 1071 "Given that $f(x)=5{e}^{x}+1$, find the slope of the tangent at $x=0.$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237855 1072 #uuid "987874a9-79ce-51e5-b80a-f5a44c3f0800" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237855 1074 "The derivative of ${e}^{x}$ is just ${e}^{x}$ itself. On the other hand, $1$ is a constant, so its derivative is $0.$\n\nFirst, using the addition rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5{e}^{x}+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5{e}^{x})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(1) \\\\  & =5\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x})+0 \\\\  & =5{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent at $x=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=0} & ={(5{e}^{x})\\,|}_{x=0} \\\\  & =5{e}^{0} \\\\  & =5\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237856 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237510 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237856 1069 "q-101590" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237856 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237856 1071 "Given that $f(x)=2{e}^{x}-ln⁡11$, find the slope of the tangent at $x=1.$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237856 1072 #uuid "cf78a5ba-4334-5b57-bc16-3a848e2f0dba" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237856 1074 "The derivative of ${e}^{x}$ is just ${e}^{x}$ itself. On the other hand, $-ln⁡11$ is a constant, so its derivative is $0.$\n\nFirst, using the addition rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{e}^{x}-ln⁡11) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{e}^{x})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-ln⁡11) \\\\  & =2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x})+0 \\\\  & =2{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent at $x=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=1} & ={(2{e}^{x})\\,|}_{x=1} \\\\  & =2{e}^{1} \\\\  & =2e.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237857 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237647 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237857 1069 "q-101600" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237857 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237857 1071 "Given that $y=2eln⁡x$, find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237857 1072 #uuid "319b8cc3-c619-5308-8487-fad516398fb6" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237857 1074 "The derivative of the natural logarithm is the reciprocal function:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(ln⁡x)=\\frac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nWe apply our formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2eln⁡x) \\\\  & =2e\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(ln⁡x) \\\\  & =2e\\cdot \\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2e}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237858 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237587 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237858 1069 "q-101604" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237858 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237858 1071 "Given that $y=-9ln⁡x$, find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237858 1072 #uuid "681375a5-0c42-5d37-8f3f-a0f73ddc205e" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237858 1074 "The derivative of the natural logarithm is the reciprocal function:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(ln⁡x)=\\frac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nWe apply our formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-9ln⁡x) \\\\  & =-9\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(ln⁡x) \\\\  & =-9\\cdot \\frac{1}{x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237859 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237489 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237859 1069 "q-101716" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237859 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237859 1071 "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for $y=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-10x+7{x}^{3}-1.$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237859 1072 #uuid "2ed4b1bb-20cc-53c5-859e-2495715a907d" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237859 1074 "Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-10x+7{x}^{3}-1) \\\\  & =2(\\frac{1}{x})-10+21{x}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{x}-10+21{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237860 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237525 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237860 1069 "q-101726" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237860 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237860 1071 "Given that $y=3x-5+8{log}_{2}⁡x$, find the slope of the tangent at $x=\\frac{1}{ln⁡2}.$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237860 1072 #uuid "09ca9b0e-b19f-5aca-b14f-466e3c4366f1" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237860 1074 "To find the slope of the tangent at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nTaking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3x-5+8{log}_{2}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3x)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-5)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(8{log}_{2}⁡x) \\\\  & =3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x)-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5)+8\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({log}_{2}⁡x) \\\\  & =3-0+8\\cdot \\frac{1}{xln⁡2} \\\\  & =3+\\frac{8}{xln⁡2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent at $x=\\frac{1}{ln⁡2}$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=1/ln⁡2} & ={(3+\\frac{8}{xln⁡2})|}_{x=1/ln⁡2} \\\\  & =3+\\frac{8}{(\\frac{1}{ln⁡2}\\cdot ln⁡2)} \\\\  & =3+8 \\\\  & =11\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237861 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237588 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237861 1069 "q-101735" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237861 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237861 1071 "Which of the following improper integrals are divergent?\n\n- ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-2}\\frac{1}{{u}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}u$\n- ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{(z+3)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z$\n- ${\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237861 1072 #uuid "8e90080f-3ef7-5c8d-a0d7-de79fffa1eb1" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237861 1074 "Let's work out each integral.\n\nFor the first integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-2}\\frac{1}{{u}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}u & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-2}\\frac{1}{{u}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{2{u}^{2}}]}_{a}^{-2} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(-\\frac{1}{2(-2)^{2}}+\\frac{1}{2{a}^{2}}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{1}{2{a}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{8}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral is convergent.\n\nFor the second integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{(z+3)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}\\frac{1}{(z+3)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{z+3}]}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(-\\frac{1}{a+3}+\\frac{1}{0+3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral is convergent.\n\nFor the third integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{2}^{a}\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|]_{2}^{a} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{ln}⁡a-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) \\\\  & =\\infty ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral diverges.\n\nIn conclusion, only integrals III is divergent." 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237862 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237632 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237862 1069 "q-101738" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237862 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237862 1071 "Which of the following improper integrals are divergent?\n\n- ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{1}{(u-1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u$\n- ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}t\\,\\text{d}t$\n- ${\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{x-2}}\\,\\text{d}x$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237862 1072 #uuid "30834ba5-7de3-5c58-8e09-a32166c98581" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237862 1074 "Let's work out each integral.\n\nFor the first integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{1}{(u-1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{1}{(u-1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{u-1}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(1+\\frac{1}{a-1}) \\\\  & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral is convergent.\n\nFor the second integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}t\\,\\text{d}t & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}]}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(0-{a}^{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{a}^{2} \\\\  & =-\\infty ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral diverges.\n\nFor the third integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{x-2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{3}^{a}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{x-2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[4\\sqrt{x-2}]}_{3}^{a} \\\\  & =4{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\sqrt{a-2}-\\sqrt{3-2}) \\\\  & =4{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\sqrt{a-2}-1) \\\\  & =\\infty ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral diverges.\nIn conclusion, only integrals II and III are divergent." 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237863 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237547 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237863 1069 "q-10195" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237863 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237863 1071 "Solve the following system of equations:\n    \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & x+y+z=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & y+z=1 \\\\  & z=2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237863 1072 #uuid "cbce6ae8-46c9-5589-9b0d-0bac9aaa8725" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237863 1074 "The third equation states that  $z=2.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get \n\n$$\ny+2=1\\,⟹\\,y=1-2=-1.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $z=2$ and $y=-1$into the first equation to get\n\n$$\nx+(-1)+(2)=\\frac{1}{2}\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{1}{2}-2+1=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$, $y=-1$, and $z=2.$ In vector form, the solution can be written as $⟨-\\frac{1}{2},-1,2⟩.$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237864 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237472 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237864 1069 "q-10196" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237864 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237864 1071 "Solve the following system of equations:\n    \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & {x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=1 \\\\  & {x}_{2}-{x}_{3}=-1 \\\\  & {x}_{3}=3\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237864 1072 #uuid "4e84c628-87b9-5d74-b46c-f0558ebf65c9" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237864 1074 "The third equation states that ${x}_{3}=3.$ Substitute this into the second equation to get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}-(3)=-1\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=-1+3=2.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute ${x}_{3}=3$ and ${x}_{2}=2$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-(2)+(3)=1\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=1-3+2=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is ${x}_{1}=0$,  ${x}_{2}=2$, and ${x}_{3}=3.$ In vector form, the solution can be written as $⟨0,2,3⟩.$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237865 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237506 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237865 1069 "q-101964" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237865 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237865 1071 "![](images/80/80fca5dc871cd083e638472abaf26b2a8ba91e81cfa5372df7398b4b63346dfa.png)\n\nGiven the plot of the function $y=f(x)$ above, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The function is continuous at $x=0$\n- The function is continuous at $x=5$\n- The function is differentiable at $x=10$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237865 1072 #uuid "016438b5-f9c2-5b0d-a1cf-02248bb802c0" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237865 1074 "- Statement I is true. The graph can be drawn in the neighborhood of $x=0$ without taking a pencil off the paper.\n\n- Statement II is true. The graph can be drawn in the neighborhood of $x=5$ without taking a pencil off the paper.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the function is not continuous at $x=10$, it can't be differentiable at that point.\n\nTherefore, only statements I and II are true." 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237866 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237630 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237866 1069 "q-10197" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237866 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237866 1071 "Solve the following system of equations:\n    \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & x-y-z=4 \\\\  & y+2z=2 \\\\  & z=\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237866 1072 #uuid "498c3ac6-7680-54fc-be56-c8179953d99f" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237866 1074 "he third equation states that  $z=\\frac{1}{2}.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\ny+2(\\frac{1}{2})=2\\,⟹\\,y=2-1=1.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $z=\\frac{1}{2}$ and $y=1$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\nx-(1)-(\\frac{1}{2})=4\\,⟹\\,x=4+1+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{11}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=\\frac{11}{2}$,  $y=1$, and $z=\\frac{1}{2}.$  In vector form, the solution can be written as $⟨\\frac{11}{2},1,\\frac{1}{2}⟩.$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237867 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237688 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237867 1069 "q-10198" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237867 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237867 1071 "Solve the following system of equations:\n    \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & {x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}=9 \\\\  & {x}_{2}-3{x}_{3}=2 \\\\  & {x}_{3}=\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237867 1072 #uuid "bcc22116-bc14-5a39-b8c2-56ca9a77cd3e" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237867 1074 "The third equation states that  ${x}_{3}=\\frac{1}{3}.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}-3(\\frac{1}{3})=2\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=2+1=3.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute ${x}_{3}=\\frac{1}{3}$ and ${x}_{2}=3$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-(3)+3(\\frac{1}{3})=9\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=9+3-1=11.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is ${x}_{1}=11$, ${x}_{2}=3$, and ${x}_{3}=\\frac{1}{3}.$ In vector form, the solution can be written as $⟨11,3,\\frac{1}{3}⟩.$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237868 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237666 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237868 1069 "q-101983" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237868 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237868 1071 "![](images/dc/dc27a54ac20efc84bfbc404a20a91309c60c3fb42118f164129e93eee584437b.png)\n\nGiven the plot of the function $y=f(x)$ above, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The function is continuous at $x=-5$\n- The function is differentiable at $x=-5$\n- The function is differentiable at $x=0$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237868 1072 #uuid "08a8dbc9-b97f-57b3-acad-4c86f2f3f516" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237868 1074 "- Statement I is false. The function has an infinite discontinuity at $x=-5.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=-5$ it can't be differentiable there either.\n\n- Statement III is true. The function is continuous at $x=0$ and doesn't have a corner point, a  cusp, or a vertical tangent.\n\nTherefore, only statement III  is true." 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237869 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237505 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237869 1069 "q-10199" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237869 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237869 1071 "Solve the following system of equations:\n    \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & x-y-z=3 \\\\  & y-z=2 \\\\  & z=1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237869 1072 #uuid "d50aa8dd-507e-51de-8c65-c56ceae864d0" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237869 1074 "The third equation states that $z=1.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\ny-(1)=2\\,⟹\\,y=2+1=3.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $z=1$ and $y=3$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\nx-(3)-(1)=3\\,⟹\\,x=3+4=7.\n$$\n\nThus, the solution is $x=7$,  $y=3$,  and $z=1.$ In vector form, the solution can be written as $⟨7,3,1⟩.$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237870 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237764 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237870 1069 "q-101990" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237870 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237870 1071 "![](images/e7/e73fd08c7745ed049975a3a7c3af2f69e88bf0b374db741f96ce1812dc40f5f5.png)\n\nConsider the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is plotted above. At which points is the function $f(x)$ **not** differentiable?" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237870 1072 #uuid "4ef914db-9e18-5c36-9bab-505a3f627844" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237870 1074 "The function is not differentiable wherever it has a discontinuity, corner, cusp, or vertical tangent.\n\n- At $x=1$ the function has a removable discontinuity, so it's not differentiable there.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $x=1.$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237871 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237683 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237871 1069 "q-101991" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237871 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237871 1071 "![](images/6e/6ec1bb6f164aa4878480f06707722769d1e17ff735a04c29a3fc2b9bd09ca467.png)\n\nConsider the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is plotted above. At which points is the function $f(x)$ **not** differentiable?" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237871 1072 #uuid "b062f6b5-3d01-5027-b39d-eb8b76c864c0" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237871 1074 "The function is not differentiable wherever it has a discontinuity, corner, cusp, or vertical tangent.\n\n- At $x=-10$ the function has a corner, so it's not differentiable there.\n\n- At $x=-5$ the function has an infinite discontinuity, so it's not differentiable there.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $x=-10$ and $x=-5.$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237872 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237599 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237872 1069 "q-101994" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237872 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237872 1071 "![](images/ce/ce2ec4b51b4c55975bf25234fdfbfa98663ae95ef7ee2bd5a774c4e4d4655292.png)\n\nConsider the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is plotted above. At which points is the function $f(x)$ **not** differentiable?" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237872 1072 #uuid "c5681c63-3803-585b-a4d4-bb304a849958" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237872 1074 "The function is not differentiable wherever it has a discontinuity, corner, cusp, or vertical tangent.\n\n- At $x=0$ the function has a cusp, so it's not differentiable there.\n\n- At $x=1$ the function has a jump discontinuity, so it's not differentiable there.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is  $x=0$ and $x=1.$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237873 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237744 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237873 1069 "q-101995" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237873 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237873 1071 "Given that a function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=0$, has a removable discontinuity at $x=1$, and is differentiable at $x=-1$, which of the following statements must be true for **any** $f(x)$ that satisfy these criteria?\n\n- It is differentiable at $x=0$\n- It  is **not** differentiable at $x=1$\n- It is continuous at $x=-1$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237873 1072 #uuid "0efb43bb-72c2-5796-b21b-537b0708d8ce" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237873 1074 "- Statement I is false. Continuity does not imply differentiability. For example, the function $y=|x|$ is continuous but it's not differentiable at $x=0.$\n\n- Statement II is true.  A function that is discontinuous at a point can't be differentiable at this point (because differentiability implies continuity).\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, differentiability implies continuity." 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237874 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237554 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237874 1069 "q-101997" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237874 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237874 1071 "Given that a function $f(x)$ has a removable discontinuity at $x=-5$, is differentiable at $x=-2$, and has a  vertical tangent at $x=0$, which of the following statements must be true for **any** $f(x)$ that satisfy these criteria?\n\n- It is **not** differentiable at $x=-5$\n- It  is continuous at $x=-2$\n- It is differentiable at $x=0$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237874 1072 #uuid "5280401d-3d75-5547-be89-ad4f6f6c9526" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237874 1074 "Differentiability implies continuity. Hence,\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=-5$, it can't be differentiable there either.\n\n- Statement II is true.   Since $f(x)$ is differentiable at $x=-2$ it is also  continuous at $x=-2.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since we have a vertical tangent at $x=0$, the function $f(x)$ is not differentiable at this point." 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237875 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237535 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237875 1069 "q-101998" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237875 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237875 1071 "Which of the following functions is differentiable at the point $x=0?$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237875 1072 #uuid "7ea25961-eeaa-5cf0-a5db-7ca6fd38017f" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237875 1074 "Notice that the function $q(x)$ is differentiable for all $x\\ne 1.$ Therefore, the correct answer is $q(x)={\\begin{aligned}x-1 & x<1 \\\\ x+1, & x\\ge 1\\end{aligned}.$\n\nAll other functions are not differentiable at $x=0.$\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not defined at $x=0$, so it is not differentiable there.\n\n- The function $g(x)$ has a corner point at $x=0$, so it is not differentiable there.\n\n- The function $p(x)$ has a vertical tangent at  $x=0$, so it is not differentiable there.\n\n- The function $h(x)$ is not defined at $x=0$, so it is not differentiable there." 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237876 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237553 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237876 1069 "q-10203" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237876 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237876 1071 "Solve the system of equations represented by the augmented matrix\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & 1 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237876 1072 #uuid "f51fa541-69ba-512e-9b86-f9dbb4dce8b1" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237876 1074 "The matrix represents a system of three linear equations in three variables, say, $x$, $y$, and $z.$ We obtain the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned} & 2x-2y+z=8 \\\\  & y-z=-1 \\\\  & z=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe third equation states that $z=0.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\ny-0=-1\\,⟹\\,y=-1.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $z=0$ and $y=-1$ into the first equation to get\n\n$$\n2x-2(-1)+(0)=8\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{8-2}{2}=3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=3$, $y=-1$, and $z=0.$ In vector form, the solution can be written as $⟨3,-1,0⟩.$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237877 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237706 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237877 1069 "q-102115" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237877 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237877 1071 "![](images/d1/d1d91b2d791d69c512a6bd2e7d523563de076a7f3afda53513234e799152ef8d.png)\n\nThe curve $y=f(x)$ is shown above. At which of the following points is $f(x)$ continuous?\n\n- $x=-1$\n- $x=1$\n- $x=3$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237877 1072 #uuid "a3b92036-278a-5fb6-a1d2-39caf6b31601" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237877 1074 "Let's consider each point separately.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=-1.$ Since there is a jump at $x=-1,$ it is impossible to draw the function in the neighborhood of $x=-1$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=1.$ Since there is a break at $x=1,$ it is impossible to draw the function in the neighborhood of $x=1$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=3.$ The graph can be drawn in the neighborhood of $x=3$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\nHence, the correct answer is  \"III only\".\n\n \n\n![](images/57/57415045f7a1cc1c896756ff19cb17f90074151a25d12c56068fb6f86a064753.png)" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237878 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237765 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237878 1069 "q-102159" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237878 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237878 1071 "Which of the following functions is differentiable at the point $x=-1?$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237878 1072 #uuid "4fad11de-5b75-50e6-bc02-1a3c1209884c" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237878 1074 "Notice that the function $h(x)$ is differentiable for all $x\\ne 1.$ Therefore, the correct answer is $h(x)=\\frac{1}{x-1}.$\n\nThe other functions are not differentiable at $x=-1.$\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not defined at $x=-1$ and hence it is not differentiable there.\n\n- The function $p(x)$ has a vertical tangent at $x=-1$ and hence it is not differentiable there.\n\n- The function $q(x)$ has a corner point at $x=-1$ and hence it is not differentiable there.\n\n- The function $g(x)$ is not defined at $x=-1$ and hence it is not differentiable there." 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237879 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237682 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237879 1069 "q-102163" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237879 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237879 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function given below?\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}2x-2, & x<0 \\\\ -2, & x\\ge 0\\end{aligned}\\,\n$$\n\n- It is continuous at $x=0$\n- It is differentiable at $x=0$\n- It is differentiable on the interval $(0,2)$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237879 1072 #uuid "a6fe09a9-5d63-559e-a71c-67bb17b36b63" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237879 1074 "- Statement I is true  Indeed, let's investigate the continuity of $f(x).$ It is continuous everywhere except, possibly, at  $x=0.$ At this point, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}(2x-2)=2(0)-2=-2 \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}-2=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)=f(0)=-2,\n$$\n\nand so $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=0$ .\n\n- Statement II is false, $f(x)$ is not differentiable at $x=0$ because it has a corner point there: \n\n$\\,$ ${f}^{′}(x)=2\\,$  if  $\\,x<0$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0\\,$ if $\\,x>0$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, $f(x)$ is differentiable everywhere except at $x=0.$   Since $x=0$  doesn't belong to $(0,2)$, the given function is differentiable on this interval.\n\nTherefore, only statements I and III are true." 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237880 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237775 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237880 1069 "q-102192" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237880 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237880 1071 "If $y=\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}},$ find $\\frac{dy}{dx}.$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237880 1072 #uuid "ec04a0d1-192f-5dcf-aa4b-20bf3669c6e0" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237880 1074 "We first rewrite the function as\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}}=\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{-3/2}.\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the constant multiple and power rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{-3/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{-3/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (-\\frac{3}{2}){x}^{(-\\frac{3}{2}-1)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-5/2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{5}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237881 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237769 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237881 1069 "q-102224" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237881 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237881 1071 "What is the slope of the tangent to the curve $y=x-\\sqrt[3]{x}$ at the point $(8,6)?$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237881 1072 #uuid "f43899a9-0d39-51e2-a17a-ed84d3a6182c" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237881 1074 "This time, we'll use $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ notation. First, we compute the derivative, which gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x-{x}^{1/3}) \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {x}^{-2/3} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=8$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=8} & =1-\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt[3]{{8}^{2}}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent to the curve is $\\frac{11}{12}.$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237882 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237582 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237882 1069 "q-102236" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237882 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237882 1071 "Find the $x$-coordinate of the point on the curve $y={x}^{2}-3x+10$ whose tangent has a slope of $-1.$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237882 1072 #uuid "3b74e076-1211-5eb5-9bb6-77fa9cea478c" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237882 1074 "Let $f(x)={x}^{2}-3x+10.$ The slope of the tangent at the point $x$ is equal to ${f}^{′}(x).$ Computing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}-3x+10) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2})-3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(10) \\\\  & =2x-3\\cdot 1+0 \\\\  & =2x-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now set ${f}^{′}(x)=-1$ and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-3 & =-1 \\\\ 2x & =-1+3 \\\\ 2x & =2 \\\\ x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent is equal to $-1$ at the point where $x=1.$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237883 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237768 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237883 1069 "q-102243" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237883 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237883 1071 "![](images/d7/d71510f1dc050f06f47d2c90970eca7734ae9bad82f69c67b37d84eae334c87d.png)\n\nLet $U={♢,△,♣,◻}$ be the universal set and let $C={△,♢}.$ What is $\\overset{―}{C}?$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237883 1072 #uuid "fcec1fe3-59d9-58d4-a901-4484232ce96a" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237883 1074 "The complement of $C,$ denoted $\\overset{―}{C},$ consists of all the elements in $U$ that are not in $C.$ To represent $\\overset{―}{C},$ we will shade in all the area of $U$ that is *outside* of $C,$ as follows:\n\n![](images/9f/9ff9378b08cdd6175901e40a70c3a6ed38cfa21cd15e948f766f1a668345834a.png)\n\nThe elements in the shaded area are $◻,$ and $♣.$ These are the elements of $U$ that are not in $C.$ Therefore, the complement of $C$ is\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{C}={◻,♣}.\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237884 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237676 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237884 1069 "q-102246" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237884 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237884 1071 "![](images/95/95f7caf5956ab22fe5b47d14098730e23332c41a26d58fc8dc0f0e4ca28546ab.png)\n\nLet $U={l,m,n,o}$ be the universal set and let $K={l,m,o}.$ What is $\\overset{―}{K}?$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237884 1072 #uuid "5a339fd3-f450-5a9a-b0dd-ff8dba5b4293" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237884 1074 "The complement of $K,$ denoted $\\overset{―}{K},$ consists of all the elements in $U$ that are not in $K.$ To represent $\\overset{―}{K},$ we will shade in all the area of $U$ that is *outside* of $K,$ as follows:\n\n![](images/74/74345aeb2a1028e07d155dd357a9bb412860309c99cee37c62380907b3e1bd37.png)\n\nThe element in the shaded area is $n.$ This is the only element of $U$ that is not in $K.$ Therefore, the complement of $K$ is\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{K}={n}.\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237885 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237609 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237885 1069 "q-102248" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237885 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237885 1071 "![](images/27/277debf60ec84e4693e5a699a9c2eda6af6248e8f1a53e005dae79f70b536722.png)\n\nLet $U={1,10,11,101}$ be the universal set and let $Y={1,11}.$ What is $\\overset{―}{Y}?$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237885 1072 #uuid "c1485238-3246-51bb-ad92-9828f6f675bd" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237885 1074 "The complement of $Y,$ denoted $\\overset{―}{Y},$ consists of all the elements in $U$ that are not in $Y.$ To represent $\\overset{―}{Y},$ we will shade in all the area of $U$ that is *outside* of $Y,$ as follows:\n\n![](images/25/25b1388466c800664b7d438eabf58cafd365174ca877abff8cd0465e2b23e7cb.png)\n\nThe elements in the shaded area are $10,$ and $101.$ These are the elements of $U$ that are not in $Y.$ Therefore, the complement of $Y$ is\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{Y}={10,101}.\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237886 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237465 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237886 1069 "q-102304" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237886 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237886 1071 "Let $y={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}$ be a series solution to the equation $4y+12{y}^{′}=0.$ \n\nWhat is the recurrence relation for the coefficients ${a}_{n},$ where $n=0,1,2,…?$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237886 1072 #uuid "348dc22b-3c75-5d93-b5cd-b56e52829b14" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237886 1074 "First, we find ${y}^{′}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & ={({\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n})}^{′} \\\\  & ={({a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n})}^{′} \\\\  & =0+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we substitute expressions for $y(x)$ and ${y}^{′}(x)$ into the given differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}+12{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to make the powers of $x$ the same. Therefore, in the second sum, we change the index of the summation by writing $m=n-1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}+12{\\sum }_{m=0}^{\\infty }(m+1){a}_{m+1}{x}^{m} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReplacing $m$ with $n$ in the second summation (since it does not matter which letter we use) gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}+12{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(n+1){a}_{n+1}{x}^{n} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince both sums now start with $n=0,$ we can combine them as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }[4{a}_{n}+12(n+1){a}_{n+1}]{x}^{n} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting each coefficient of $x$ equal to zero, we obtain\n\n$$\n4{a}_{n}+12(n+1){a}_{n+1}=0.\n$$\n\nFinally, the recursion formula is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}=-\\frac{{a}_{n}}{3(n+1)},\\,n=0,1,2,….\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237887 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237705 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237887 1069 "q-102307" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237887 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237887 1071 "Let $y={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}$ be a series solution to the equation $y+{y}^{″}=0.$  Find $f(n),$ given that the recurrence relation for ${a}_{n}$ can be written as\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+2}=\\frac{{a}_{n}}{f(n)},\\,n=0,1,2,…\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237887 1072 #uuid "895dd62a-6209-5592-a28c-2c173d2dadf2" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237887 1074 "First, we find ${y}^{′}(x):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & ={({\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n})}^{′} \\\\  & ={({a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n})}^{′} \\\\  & =0+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we find ${y}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″}(x) & ={({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1})}^{′} \\\\  & ={({a}_{1}+{\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }n{a}_{n}{x}^{n-1})}^{′} \\\\  & =0+{\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }n(n-1){a}_{n}{x}^{n-2} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }n(n-1){a}_{n}{x}^{n-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we substitute the expressions for $y(x)$ and ${y}^{″}(x)$ into the given differential equation:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}+{\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }n(n-1){a}_{n}{x}^{n-2}=0\n$$\n\nWe want to make the powers of $x$ the same. Therefore, in the second sum, we change the index of the summation by writing $m=n-2.$ This gives\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}+{\\sum }_{m=0}^{\\infty }(m+2)(m+1){a}_{m+2}{x}^{m}=0.\n$$\n\nReplacing $m$ with $n$ in the second summation (since it does not matter which letter we use) gives\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(n+2)(n+1){a}_{n+2}{x}^{n}=0.\n$$\n\nSince both sums now start with $n=0,$ we can combine them as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }[{a}_{n}+(n+2)(n+1){a}_{n+2}]{x}^{n}=0\n$$\n\nSetting each coefficient of $x$ equal to zero, we obtain\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}+(n+2)(n+1){a}_{n+2}=0.\n$$\n\nFinally, the recursion formula is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+2}=-\\frac{{a}_{n}}{(n+1)(n+2)},\\,n=0,1,2,…\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(n)=-(n+1)(n+2).$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237888 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237497 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237888 1069 "q-102309" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237888 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237888 1071 "Let $y={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}$ be a series solution to the equation $6y+9{y}^{″}=0.$  Find $f(n),$ given that the recurrence relation for ${a}_{n}$ can be written as\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+2}=\\frac{{a}_{n