ligned}2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237892 1072 #uuid "cfeb000c-7c59-5f74-9ec1-f5486996f27d" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237892 1074 "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ as ${q}_{1},\\,{q}_{2}.$ \n\nSince $B$ is an orthonormal basis, we have that $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$, where ${x}_{i}=x\\cdot {q}_{i}.$ Computing the components of $x,$ we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]=\\sqrt{3}-1 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{aligned}]=1+\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3}-1 \\\\ 1+\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237893 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237637 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237893 1069 "q-102328" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237893 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237893 1071 "Determine the stationary points of $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-6x-2y+12.$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237893 1072 #uuid "b5a6d7e9-e6c6-56f2-868d-1ad027ad7493" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237893 1074 "The stationary points of a multivariable function $f$ are the points $a$ in the domain of $f$ such that ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T},$ where\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]|}_{x=a}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the domain of $f$ is ${R}^{2},$ and the first partial derivatives of $f$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =2x-6 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =2y-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the first derivative of $f$ is\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}2x-6 & 2y-2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the stationary points, we need to find the points where ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T}.$\nSetting ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we have the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x-6=0 \\\\ 2y-2=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for $x$ in the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n2x-6=0\\,⟹\\,x=3.\n$$\n\nNow, solving for $y$ in the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n2y-2=0\\,⟹\\,y=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our system has one solution, $(3,1).$\n\nIn conclusion, $f$ has one stationary point, $(3,1).$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237894 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237529 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237894 1069 "q-102333" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237894 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237894 1071 "Given the **orthonormal** basis $B$ of ${R}^{3}$ and the vector $x$ below, find the value of $5{x}_{2}$, where ${x}_{2}$ is the second component of $[x]_{B}.$\n\n$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]},\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237894 1072 #uuid "308c2ab2-c6c5-594c-a9c9-8ab5eac9da93" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237894 1074 "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ as ${q}_{1},\\,{q}_{2},\\,{q}_{3}.$ \n\nSince $B$ is an orthonormal basis, we have that $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]$, where ${x}_{i}=x\\cdot {q}_{i}.$ Computing ${x}_{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =x\\cdot {q}_{2} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =4+0+3 \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n5{x}_{2}=5\\cdot 7=35.\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237895 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237581 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237895 1069 "q-102337" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237895 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237895 1071 "Given the orthogonal basis $B$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the vector $x$ below, find the value of $5{x}_{1},$ where ${x}_{1}$ is the first component of $[x]_{B}.$\n\n$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]},\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237895 1072 #uuid "1b161241-4160-51dc-b8ef-c729dd1d8482" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237895 1074 "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ as ${q}_{1},\\,{q}_{2}.$ \n\nSince $B$ is an orthogonal basis, we have that $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$, where ${x}_{i}=\\frac{x\\cdot {q}_{i}}{{q}_{i}\\cdot {q}_{i}}.$ Computing ${x}_{1},$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{x\\cdot {q}_{1}}{{q}_{1}\\cdot {q}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\cdot (-4)+(-4)\\cdot 2}{{(-4)}^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-20-8}{20} \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n5{x}_{1}=5\\cdot (-\\frac{7}{5})=-7.\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237896 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237457 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237896 1069 "q-102339" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237896 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237896 1071 "Given the orthogonal basis $B$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the vector $x$ below, find the value of $6{x}_{1},$ where ${x}_{1}$ is the first component of $[x]_{B}.$\n\n$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}]},\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237896 1072 #uuid "3e19c705-cd14-5edd-9733-28f10e7bbc9e" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237896 1074 "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ as ${q}_{1},\\,{q}_{2}.$ \n\nSince $B$ is an orthogonal basis, we have that $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$, where ${x}_{i}=\\frac{x\\cdot {q}_{i}}{{q}_{i}\\cdot {q}_{i}}.$ Computing ${x}_{1},$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{x\\cdot {q}_{1}}{{q}_{1}\\cdot {q}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-9)\\cdot 4+8\\cdot 2}{{4}^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-36+16}{16+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{-20}{20} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n6{x}_{1}=6\\cdot (-1)=-6.\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237897 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237650 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237897 1069 "q-102341" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237897 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237897 1071 "Given the orthogonal basis $B$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the vector $x$ below, find the value of $4{x}_{2},$ where ${x}_{2}$ is the second component of $[x]_{B}.$\n\n$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]},\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}15 \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237897 1072 #uuid "a3f9b9a5-73fe-534f-bd10-3b8c70afae2e" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237897 1074 "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ as ${q}_{1},\\,{q}_{2}.$ \n\nSince $B$ is an orthogonal basis, we have that $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$, where ${x}_{i}=\\frac{x\\cdot {q}_{i}}{{q}_{i}\\cdot {q}_{i}}.$ Computing ${x}_{1},$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\frac{x\\cdot {q}_{2}}{{q}_{2}\\cdot {q}_{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{15\\cdot (-5)+(-12)\\cdot 4}{(-5)^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-75-48}{25+16} \\\\  & =-\\frac{123}{41} \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n4{x}_{1}=4\\cdot (-3)=-12.\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237898 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237615 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237898 1069 "q-102348" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237898 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237898 1071 "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}-11x{y}^{′}+36y=0,\\,x>0.\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237898 1072 #uuid "9372000a-a262-5823-8d55-11add35abca4" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237898 1074 "The given equation is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation. So, we assume that the solutions take the form $y={x}^{\\lambda }.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {x}^{\\lambda -1},\\,{y}^{″}=\\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda -2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda -2}-11x\\cdot \\lambda {x}^{\\lambda -1}+36{x}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda }-11\\lambda {x}^{\\lambda }+36{x}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ {x}^{\\lambda }[\\lambda (\\lambda -1)-11\\lambda +36] & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda (\\lambda -1)-11\\lambda +36 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-12\\lambda +36 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -6)^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we have a double root $\\lambda =6.$\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)={c}_{1}{x}^{6}+{c}_{2}{x}^{6}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237899 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237502 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237899 1069 "q-102352" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237899 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237899 1071 "Given the orthogonal basis $B$ of ${R}^{3}$ and the vector $x$ below, find the value of $100{x}_{2}$, where ${x}_{2}$ is the second component of $[x]_{B}.$\n\n$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}25 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]},\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237899 1072 #uuid "5a087a5d-0e2d-5fa0-8add-773aec6e0a90" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237899 1074 "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ as ${q}_{1},\\,{q}_{2},\\,{q}_{3}.$ \n\nSince $B$ is an orthogonal basis, we have that $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]$, where ${x}_{i}=\\frac{x\\cdot {q}_{i}}{{q}_{i}\\cdot {q}_{i}}.$ Computing ${x}_{2},$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\frac{x\\cdot {q}_{2}}{{q}_{2}\\cdot {q}_{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot 25+0\\cdot (-3)+3\\cdot (-4)}{{25}^{2}+(-3)^{2}+(-4)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{25-12}{625+9+16} \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{650} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{50}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n100{x}_{2}=100\\cdot \\frac{1}{50}=2.\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237900 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237729 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237900 1069 "q-102364" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237900 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237900 1071 "Given that the differential equation\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+13x{y}^{′}+36y=0\n$$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$$\ny={c}_{1}{x}^{-6}+{c}_{2}{x}^{-6}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,\n$$\n\nsolve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+13x{y}^{′}+36y=0,\\,y(1)=4,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=-10,\\,x>0.\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237900 1072 #uuid "a8ac38ea-1f77-5f1f-95a7-c89e02bd2ca3" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237900 1074 "We find the constants ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- First, we apply the condition $y(1)=4.$ Substituting $y(1)=4$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n4={c}_{1}.\n$$\n\n- Then, we apply the condition ${y}^{′}(1)=-10.$ Differentiating $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =-6{c}_{1}{x}^{-7}+{c}_{2}{x}^{-7}-6{c}_{2}{x}^{-7}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(1)=-10$ and ${c}_{1}=4$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-10 & =-6(4)+{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{2} & =14.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & =4{x}^{-6}+14{x}^{-6}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x \\\\  & =\\frac{4+14\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{x}^{6}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237901 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237467 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237901 1069 "q-102398" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237901 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237901 1071 "Find the Laplace transform of $y(t),$ if \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-4y={e}^{-t},\\,y(0)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=1.\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237901 1072 #uuid "e3d7b81b-ba5d-571d-ad8b-33833f7cf533" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237901 1074 "First, we take the Laplace transform of both sides and use the addition and scalar multiplication properties of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{y}^{″}-4y} & =L{{e}^{-t}} \\\\ L{{y}^{″}}-4L{y} & =L{{e}^{-t}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's introduce $L{y}=Y(s).$ \n\nTo calculate $L{{y}^{″}},$ we use the formula for the Laplace transform of a second derivative:\n\n$$\nL{{y}^{″}}={s}^{2}L{y}-sy(0)-{y}^{′}(0)={s}^{2}Y(s)-sy(0)-{y}^{′}(0)\n$$\n\nTaking into account the given initial conditions, we get\n\n$$\nL{{y}^{′}}={s}^{2}Y(s)-1.\n$$\n\nFrom the table of Laplace transforms, we have the following result:\n\n$$\nL{{e}^{-t}}=\\frac{1}{s+1}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can solve for $Y(s):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{y}^{″}}-4L{y} & =L{{e}^{-t}} \\\\ ({s}^{2}Y(s)-1)-4Y(s) & =\\frac{1}{s+1} \\\\ ({s}^{2}-4)Y(s) & =\\frac{1}{s+1}+1 \\\\ ({s}^{2}-4)Y(s) & =\\frac{s+2}{s+1} \\\\ Y(s) & =\\frac{s+2}{(s+1)({s}^{2}-4)} \\\\ Y(s) & =\\frac{s+2}{(s+1)(s-2)(s+2)} \\\\ Y(s) & =\\frac{1}{(s+1)(s-2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237902 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237491 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237902 1069 "q-102416" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237902 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237902 1071 "Find the root of $f(x)=\\sqrt[7]{3x+13}-1.$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237902 1072 #uuid "1ebf9ff3-e2bb-528f-a554-c8a05b21ceab" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237902 1074 "To find the root of $f(x),$  we must solve $f(x)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[7]{3x+13}-1=0\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation by making $x$ the subject, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[7]{3x+13}-1 & =0 \\\\ \\sqrt[7]{3x+13} & =1 \\\\ (\\sqrt[7]{3x+13})^{7} & ={1}^{7} \\\\ 3x+13 & =1 \\\\ 3x & =-12 \\\\ x & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the root of $f(x)$ is $x=-4.$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237903 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237524 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237903 1069 "q-10244" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237903 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237903 1071 "Consider the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 5 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat matrix results from carrying out the row operation ${R}_{1}↔{R}_{2}$ on $A?$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237903 1072 #uuid "89449fa7-e2ad-5aa8-8f8e-c30702d39ac6" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237903 1074 "The operation ${R}_{1}↔{R}_{2}$ means that we need to swap the first and second rows.\n\nApplying this row operation, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 5 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 & -3 \\\\ 7 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237904 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237455 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237904 1069 "q-102531" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237904 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237904 1071 "Express $\\frac{37-19x}{(3x+1)(x-4)(3-x)}$ as a sum of partial fractions." 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237904 1072 #uuid "48525aef-1401-5350-b706-3dd8e85f6bef" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237904 1074 "Our task is to find $A,$ $B$ and $C$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{37-19x}{(3x+1)(x-4)(3-x)} & =\\frac{A}{(3x+1)}+\\frac{B}{(x-4)}+\\frac{C}{(3-x)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides by $(3x+1)(x-4)(3-x)$ gives\n\n$$\n37-19x=A(x-4)(3-x)+B(3x+1)(3-x)+C(3x+1)(x-4).\n$$\n\nFor $x=-\\frac{1}{3}$, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}37-19(-\\frac{1}{3}) & =A(-\\frac{1}{3}-4)(3-(-\\frac{1}{3})) \\\\  & +B(3(-\\frac{1}{3})+1)(3-(-\\frac{1}{3})) \\\\  & +C(3(-\\frac{1}{3})+1)(-\\frac{1}{3}-4) \\\\ 37+\\frac{19}{3} & =A(-\\frac{130}{9}) \\\\ \\frac{130}{3} & =A(-\\frac{130}{9}) \\\\ A & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x=4$, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}37-19(4) & =A(4-4)(3-4) \\\\  & \\,+B(3(4)+1)(3-4) \\\\  & +C(3(4)+1)(x-4) \\\\ 37-76 & =B(-13) \\\\ B & =\\frac{-39}{-13} \\\\ B & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x=3$, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}37-19(3) & =A(3-4)(3-3) \\\\  & \\,+B(3(3)+1)(3-3) \\\\  & +C(3(3)+1)(3-4) \\\\ 37-57 & =C(-10) \\\\ C & =\\frac{-20}{-10} \\\\ C & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\frac{37-19x}{(3x+1)(x-4)(3-x)}=-\\frac{3}{(3x+1)}+\\frac{3}{(x-4)}+\\frac{2}{(3-x)}.\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237905 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237496 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237905 1069 "q-102540" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237905 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237905 1071 "Given that\n\n$$\n\\frac{2{x}^{2}+7x-24}{(x+2)(x-1)(x+4)}=\\frac{A}{(x+2)}+\\frac{B}{(x-1)}+\\frac{C}{(x+4)},\n$$\n\nwhere $A,$ $B$ and $C$ are real constants, calculate $A+B+C.$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237905 1072 #uuid "0e2dbe92-8a3f-5a78-aac5-ca329b9e200e" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237905 1074 "Our task is to find $A,$ $B$ and $C$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+7x-24}{(x+2)(x-1)(x+4)}=\\frac{A}{(x+2)}+\\frac{B}{(x-1)}+\\frac{C}{(x+4)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides by $(x+2)(x-1)(x+4)$ gives\n\n$$\n2{x}^{2}+7x-24=A(x-1)(x+4)+B(x+2)(x+4)+C(x+2)(x-1).\n$$\n\nFor $x=-2,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(-2)^{2}+7(-2)-24 & =A(-2-1)(-2+4) \\\\  & \\,+B(-2+2)(-2+4) \\\\  & \\,+C(-2+2)(-2-1) \\\\ -30 & =A(-6) \\\\ A & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x=1,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{(1)}^{2}+7(1)-24 & =A(1-1)(1+4) \\\\  & \\,+B(1+2)(1+4) \\\\  & \\,+C(1+2)(1-1) \\\\ 2+7-24 & =B(15) \\\\ -15 & =15B \\\\ B & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x=-4,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(-4)^{2}+7(-4)-24 & =A(-4-1)(-4+4) \\\\  & \\,+B(-4+2)(-4+4) \\\\  & \\,+C(-4+2)(-4-1) \\\\ 32-28-24 & =C(10) \\\\ -20 & =C(10) \\\\ C & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+B+C & =5+(-1)+(-2)=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237906 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237644 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237906 1069 "q-102545" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237906 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237906 1071 "Find the root of $f(x)=\\sqrt[6]{11-5x}-1.$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237906 1072 #uuid "a455185a-d45e-53e1-ad70-9c8898740251" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237906 1074 "To find the roots of $f(x),$  we must solve $f(x)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[6]{11-5x}-1=0\n$$\n\nWe solve this equation by making $x$ the subject, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[6]{11-5x}-1 & =0 \\\\ \\sqrt[6]{11-5x} & =1 \\\\ (\\sqrt[6]{11-5x})^{6} & ={1}^{6} \\\\ 11-5x & =1 \\\\ -5x & =-10 \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we must check the answer by substituting it back into the initial equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[6]{11-5\\cdot 2}-1 & =0 \\\\ \\sqrt[6]{1}-1 & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the root of $f(x)$ is $x=2.$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237907 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237548 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237907 1069 "q-102552" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237907 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237907 1071 "Given that\n\n$$\n\\frac{7{x}^{2}+20x+3}{(3x+2)(x-1)(x+5)}=\\frac{A}{(3x+2)}+\\frac{B}{(x-1)}+\\frac{C}{(x+5)},\n$$\n\nwhere $A,$ $B$ and $C$ are real constants, calculate $A+B+C.$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237907 1072 #uuid "9e760206-a007-5062-8f2d-fefff458b4ed" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237907 1074 "Our task is to find $A,$ $B$ and $C$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7{x}^{2}+20x+3}{(3x+2)(x-1)(x+5)}=\\frac{A}{(3x+2)}+\\frac{B}{(x-1)}+\\frac{C}{(x+5)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides by $(3x+2)(x-1)(x+5)$ gives\n\n$$\n7{x}^{2}+20x+3=A(x-1)(x+5)+B(3x+2)(x+5)+C(3x+2)(x-1).\n$$\n\nFor $x=-\\frac{2}{3}$, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7{(-\\frac{2}{3})}^{2}+20(-\\frac{2}{3})+3 & =A(-\\frac{2}{3}-1)(-\\frac{2}{3}+5) \\\\  & \\,+B(3(-\\frac{2}{3})+2)(-\\frac{2}{3}+5) \\\\  & \\,+C(3(-\\frac{2}{3})+2)(-\\frac{2}{3}-1) \\\\ \\frac{28}{9}-\\frac{40}{3}+3 & =A(-\\frac{65}{9}) \\\\ -\\frac{65}{9} & =A(-\\frac{65}{9}) \\\\ A & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x=1$, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7(1)^{2}+20(1)+3 & =A(1-1)(1+5) \\\\  & \\,+B(3(1)+2)(1+5) \\\\  & \\,+C(3(1)+2)(1-1) \\\\ 7+20+3 & =B(30) \\\\ B & =\\frac{30}{30} \\\\ B & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x=-5$, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7(-5)^{2}+20(-5)+3 & =A(-5-1)(-5+5) \\\\  & \\,+B(3(-5)+2)(-5+5) \\\\  & \\,+C(3(-5)+2)(-5-1) \\\\ 175-100+3 & =C(78) \\\\ C & =\\frac{78}{78} \\\\ C & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+B+C=1+1+1=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237908 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237451 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237908 1069 "q-102558" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237908 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237908 1071 "Given that \n\n$$\n\\frac{5{x}^{2}-5x+17}{({x}^{2}+5)(x-2)}=\\frac{Ax-1}{{x}^{2}+5}+\\frac{3}{x-2},\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $A?$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237908 1072 #uuid "35959a3c-8f39-5e4d-b242-d30aaa9a5b7a" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237908 1074 "Multiplying both sides of the given equality by $({x}^{2}+5)(x-2)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{x}^{2}-5x+17 & =(Ax-1)(x-2)+3({x}^{2}+5).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLetting $x=1$  gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(1)^{2}-5(1)+17 & =(A(1)-1)(1-2)+3({1}^{2}+5) \\\\ 17 & =(A-1)(-1)+3(6) \\\\ 17 & =-A+1+18 \\\\ -2 & =-A \\\\ A & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237909 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237625 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237909 1069 "q-10256" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237909 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237909 1071 "![](images/da/da529910e268ea4c124d7c27b085ca1799e255604459c539cc8d5809dda8f95a.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx={sin}^{3}⁡t,\\,y={cos}^{3}⁡t,\\,t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237909 1072 #uuid "eeda4143-b348-582e-8960-ff5a4ad7dde2" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237909 1074 "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({sin}^{3}⁡t)=3{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({cos}^{3}⁡t)=-3{cos}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{(3{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}+{(-3{cos}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{9{sin}^{4}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t+9{cos}^{4}⁡t{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{9{sin}^{2}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t){|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi -\\operatorname{cos}⁡0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4}(-1-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237910 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237712 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237910 1069 "q-102560" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237910 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237910 1071 "Given that \n\n$$\n\\frac{5{x}^{2}-5x+12}{(2{x}^{2}+3)(x-3)}=\\frac{x+B}{2{x}^{2}+3}+\\frac{2}{x-3},\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $B?$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237910 1072 #uuid "4ac20bfc-7d04-5fe8-b515-a6b04f0874d8" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237910 1074 "Multiplying both sides of the given equality by $(2{x}^{2}+3)(x-3)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{x}^{2}-5x+12 & =(x+B)(x-3)+2(2{x}^{2}+3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLetting $x=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(0)^{2}-5(0)+12 & =(0+B)(0-3)+2(2(0)^{2}+3) \\\\ 12 & =-3B+6 \\\\ 6 & =-3B \\\\ B & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237911 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237759 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237911 1069 "q-102561" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237911 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237911 1071 "Express $\\frac{3{x}^{2}+2x-3}{({x}^{2}+6)(x+5)}$ as a sum of partial fractions." 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237911 1072 #uuid "b55a1d00-b593-52f8-a903-8f54813f000f" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237911 1074 "Our task is to find $A,$ $B,$ and $C$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3{x}^{2}+2x-3}{({x}^{2}+6)(x+5)} & =\\frac{Ax+B}{({x}^{2}+6)}+\\frac{C}{(x+5)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that since ${x}^{2}+6$ is an irreducible quadratic factor, we must use a linear term $Ax+B$ in the numerator.\n\nWe multiply both sides by $({x}^{2}+6)(x+5)$ and get\n\n$$\n3{x}^{2}+2x-3=(Ax+B)(x+5)+C({x}^{2}+6).\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-5$ eliminates $A$ and $B$ and allows us to solve for $C.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(-5)^{2}+2(-5)-3 & =(A(-5)+B)(-5+5)+C((-5)^{2}+6) \\\\ 75-10-3 & =31C \\\\ C & =\\frac{62}{31} \\\\ C & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=0$ eliminates the $A$ term, and using our result for $C$ allows us to solve for $B.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(0)^{2}+2(0)-3 & =(A(0)+B)(0+5)+C((0)^{2}+6) \\\\ -3 & =5B+6C \\\\ -3 & =5B+6(2) \\\\ 5B & =-3-12 \\\\ B & =\\frac{-15}{5} \\\\ B & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to solve for $A$ we choose $x=1$, and use our results for $B$ and $C.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(1)^{2}+2(1)-3 & =(A(1)+B)(1+5)+C((1)^{2}+6) \\\\ 2 & =6A+6B+7C \\\\ 2 & =6A+6(-3)+7(2) \\\\ 6A & =2+18-14 \\\\ A & =\\frac{6}{6} \\\\ A & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have $A=1,B=-3,$ and $C=2.$ Therefore, our final result is\n\n$$\n\\frac{3{x}^{2}+2x-3}{({x}^{2}+6)(x+5)}=\\frac{x-3}{({x}^{2}+6)}+\\frac{2}{(x+5)}.\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237912 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237490 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237912 1069 "q-102568" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237912 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237912 1071 "Express $\\frac{4{x}^{2}+10x+6}{({x}^{2}+3x+4)(x+3)}$ as a sum of partial fractions." 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237912 1072 #uuid "2f8cdd07-40b5-54a5-b9f3-e723ba34c913" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237912 1074 "Our task is to find $A,$ $B,$ and $C$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4{x}^{2}+10x+6}{({x}^{2}+3x+4)(x+3)} & =\\frac{Ax+B}{({x}^{2}+3x+4)}+\\frac{C}{(x+3)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that since ${x}^{2}+3x+4$ is an irreducible quadratic factor, we must use a linear term $Ax+B$ in the numerator.\n\nWe multiply both sides by $\\frac{4{x}^{2}+10x+6}{({x}^{2}+3x+4)(x+3)}$ and get\n\n$$\n4{x}^{2}+10x+6=(Ax+B)(x+3)+C({x}^{2}+3x+4).\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-3$ eliminates the $A$ and $B$ terms and allows us to solve for $C.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(-3)^{2}+10(-3)+6 & =(A(-3)+B)(-3+3)+C((-3)^{2}+3(-3)+4) \\\\ 36-30+6 & =4C \\\\ 12 & =4C \\\\ C & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=0$ eliminates the $A$ term, and using our result for $C$ allows us to solve for $B.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(0)^{2}+10(0)+6 & =(A(0)+B)(0+3)+C({0}^{2}+3(0)+4) \\\\ 6 & =3B+4C \\\\ 6 & =3B+4(3) \\\\ 3B & =6-12 \\\\ B & =\\frac{-6}{3} \\\\ B & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to solve for $A,$ we choose $x=1$ and use our results for $B$ and $C.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(1)^{2}+10(1)+6 & =(A(1)+B)(1+3)+C({1}^{2}+3(1)+4) \\\\ 20 & =4A+4B+8C \\\\ 20 & =4A+4(-2)+8(3) \\\\ 4A & =20+8-24 \\\\ A & =\\frac{4}{4} \\\\ A & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have $A=1,B=-2,$ and $C=3.$ Therefore, our final result is\n\n$$\n\\frac{4{x}^{2}+10x+6}{({x}^{2}+3x+4)(x+3)}=\\frac{x-2}{({x}^{2}+3x+4)}+\\frac{3}{(x+3)}.\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237913 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237727 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237913 1069 "q-10257" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237913 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237913 1071 "Find the arc length of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=2{cos}^{3}⁡t,\\,y=2{sin}^{3}⁡t,\\,t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237913 1072 #uuid "01f0c95b-ef07-5cce-9a1b-7f84a8200390" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237913 1074 "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{cos}^{3}⁡t)=-6{cos}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{sin}^{3}⁡t)=6{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{(-6{cos}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}+{(6{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{36{cos}^{4}⁡t{sin}^{2}⁡t+36{sin}^{4}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{36{sin}^{2}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t){|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi -\\operatorname{cos}⁡0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}(-1-1) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237914 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237501 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237914 1069 "q-102571" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237914 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237914 1071 "Express $\\frac{2{x}^{2}+7x-1}{({x}^{2}-2x+3)(x+1)}$ as a sum of partial fractions." 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237914 1072 #uuid "009170b7-1c66-55a4-bf89-ee38c9921e16" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237914 1074 "Our task is to find $A,$ $B,$ and $C$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+7x-1}{({x}^{2}-2x+3)(x+1)} & =\\frac{Ax+B}{({x}^{2}-2x+3)}+\\frac{C}{(x+1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that since ${x}^{2}-2x+3$ is an irreducible quadratic factor, we must use a linear term $Ax+B$ in the numerator.\n\nWe multiply both sides by $({x}^{2}-2x+3)(x+1)$ and get\n\n$$\n2{x}^{2}+7x-1=(Ax+B)(x+1)+C({x}^{2}-2x+3).\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=-1$ eliminates the $A$ and $B$ terms and allows us to solve for $C.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(-1)^{2}+7(-1)-1 & =(A(-1)+B)(-1+1)+C((-1)^{2}-2(-1)+3) \\\\ -6 & =6C \\\\ C & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=0$ eliminates the $A$ term, and using our result for $C$ allows us to solve for $B.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(0)^{2}+7(0)-1 & =(A(0)+B)(0+1)+C({0}^{2}-2(0)+3) \\\\ -1 & =B+3C \\\\ -1 & =B+3(-1) \\\\ B & =3-1 \\\\ B & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to solve for $A,$ we choose $x=1$ and use our results for $B$ and $C.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(1)^{2}+7(1)-1 & =(A(1)+B)(1+1)+C((1)^{2}-2(1)+3) \\\\ 8 & =2A+2B+2C \\\\ 8 & =2A+2(2)+2(-1) \\\\ 2A & =8-4+2 \\\\ A & =\\frac{6}{2} \\\\ A & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have $A=3,B=2,$ and $C=-1.$ Therefore, our final result is\n\n$$\n\\frac{2{x}^{2}+7x-1}{({x}^{2}-2x+3)(x+1)}=\\frac{3x+2}{({x}^{2}-2x+3)}-\\frac{1}{(x+1)}.\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237915 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237730 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237915 1069 "q-102574" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237915 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237915 1071 "Express $\\frac{7{x}^{2}-29x+34}{({x}^{2}-4x+5)(x-3)}$ as a sum of partial fractions." 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237915 1072 #uuid "55fac750-a595-54b4-a3c5-8bdf5003bec3" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237915 1074 "Our task is to find $A,$ $B,$ and $C$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7{x}^{2}-29x+34}{({x}^{2}-4x+5)(x-3)} & =\\frac{Ax+B}{({x}^{2}-4x+5)}+\\frac{C}{(x-3)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that since ${x}^{2}-4x+5$ is an irreducible quadratic factor, we must use a linear term $Ax+B$ in the numerator.\n\nWe multiply both sides by $({x}^{2}-4x+5)(x-3)$ and get\n\n$$\n7{x}^{2}-29x+34=(Ax+B)(x-3)+C({x}^{2}-4x+5).\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=3$ eliminates the $A$ and $B$ terms and allows us to solve for $C.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7(3)^{2}-29(3)+34 & =(A(3)+B)(3-3)+C({3}^{2}-4(3)+5) \\\\ 63-87+34 & =2C \\\\ C & =\\frac{10}{2} \\\\ C & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=0$ eliminates the $A$ term, and using our result for $C$ allows us to solve for $B.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7(0)^{2}-29(0)+34 & =(A(0)+B)(0-3)+C({0}^{2}-4(0)+5) \\\\ 34 & =-3B+5C \\\\ 34 & =-3B+5(5) \\\\ 3B & =25-34 \\\\ B & =\\frac{-9}{3} \\\\ B & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to solve for $A,$ we choose $x=1$ and use our results for $B$ and $C.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7(1)^{2}-29(1)+34 & =(A(1)+B)(1-3)+C({1}^{2}-4(1)+5) \\\\ 12 & =-2A-2B+2C \\\\ 12 & =-2A-2(-3)+2(5) \\\\ 2A & =-12+6+10 \\\\ A & =\\frac{4}{2} \\\\ A & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have $A=2,B=-3,$ and $C=5.$ Therefore, our final result is\n\n$$\n\\frac{7{x}^{2}-29x+34}{({x}^{2}-4x+5)(x-3)}=\\frac{2x-3}{({x}^{2}-4x+5)}+\\frac{5}{(x-3)}.\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237916 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237753 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237916 1069 "q-102575" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237916 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237916 1071 "Express $\\frac{6{x}^{2}-8x+5}{({x}^{2}+x+3)(x-4)}$ as a sum of partial fractions." 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237916 1072 #uuid "5b0bcafe-5ef3-59fa-b3d6-65bcce1fbf7f" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237916 1074 "Our task is to find $A,$ $B,$ and $C$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{6{x}^{2}-8x+5}{({x}^{2}+x+3)(x-4)} & =\\frac{Ax+B}{({x}^{2}+x+3)}+\\frac{C}{(x-4)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that since ${x}^{2}+2x+3$ is an irreducible quadratic factor, we must use a linear term $Ax+B$ in the numerator.\n\nWe multiply both sides by $({x}^{2}+x+3)(x-4)$ and get\n\n$$\n6{x}^{2}-8x+5=(Ax+B)(x-4)+C({x}^{2}+x+3).\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=4$ eliminates the $A$ and $B$ terms and allows us to solve for $C.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6(4)^{2}-8(4)+5 & =(A(4)+B)(4-4)+C({4}^{2}+4+3) \\\\ 69 & =23C \\\\ C & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging in $x=0$ eliminates the $A$ term, and using our result for $C$ allows us to solve for $B.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6(0)^{2}-8(0)+5 & =(A(0)+B)(0-4)+C({0}^{2}+0+3) \\\\ 5 & =-4B+3C \\\\ 5 & =-4B+3(3) \\\\ 4B & =9-5 \\\\ B & =\\frac{4}{4} \\\\ B & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to solve for $A,$ we choose $x=1$ and use our results for $B$ and $C.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6(1)^{2}-8(1)+5 & =(A(1)+B)(1-4)+C({1}^{2}+1+3) \\\\ 3 & =-3A-3B+5C \\\\ 3 & =-3A-3(1)+5(3) \\\\ 3 & =-3A-3+15 \\\\ 3A & =-3-3+15 \\\\ A & =\\frac{9}{3} \\\\ A & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have $A=3,B=1,$ and $C=3.$ Therefore, our final result is\n\n$$\n\\frac{6{x}^{2}-8x+5}{({x}^{2}+x+3)(x-4)}=\\frac{3x+1}{({x}^{2}+x+3)}+\\frac{3}{(x-4)}.\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237917 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237563 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237917 1069 "q-10258" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237917 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237917 1071 "Find the arc length of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,y=2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,t\\in [0,\\pi ].\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237917 1072 #uuid "27c17b75-37ea-5768-beae-14242dea6c22" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237917 1074 "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)) \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t) \\\\  & =2t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{(2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}+{(2t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\cdot {\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =2(\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{2}-0) \\\\  & ={\\pi }^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237918 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237542 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237918 1069 "q-102605" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237918 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237918 1071 "![](images/dc/dcac814ed1c1caf5a8796a25ccee57cc5fd988d10f24503cbf0755aeaedb5e2c.png)\n\nFind the values of $t$ at which the curve $x=8t,y=-{t}^{2}$ intersects the line $\\frac{1}{2}x-y=0,$ as shown above." 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237918 1072 #uuid "1d0404a4-f0d4-5c3c-afa8-88439fb00c01" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237918 1074 "Notice that the line and curve intersect twice, so we are expecting two solutions.\n\nTo find the coordinates of the intersection points, we take the equation of the line, replace $x$ and $y$ with the parametric formulas, and then solve for $t.$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}x-y & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(8t)-(-{t}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ 4t+{t}^{2} & =0 \\\\ t(4+t) & =0 \\\\ t & =-4,0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237919 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237670 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237919 1069 "q-10261" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237919 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237919 1071 "![](images/82/8288453c2625dac592a51054df332abf8ee49dbf8cbcabaf327c3ffe63b55f71.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),\\,t\\in [0,\\pi ].\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237919 1072 #uuid "31e80fe7-2790-57b6-8a22-f24ca436c4a9" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237919 1074 "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))}^{2}+{(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{4{sin}^{2}⁡t-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+4{sin}^{2}⁡(2t)+4{cos}^{2}⁡t-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+4{cos}^{2}⁡(2t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{4+4-8(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{8-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{8-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the double-angle formula for cosine, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )=1-2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .$ Substituting $\\theta =\\frac{t}{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{t}{2}) & =1-2{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{t}{2}) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t & =1-2{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{t}{2}) \\\\ 1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & =2{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{t}{2}) \\\\ 8-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & =16{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{t}{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{8-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{16{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{t}{2})}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{t}{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-8{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{t}{2})|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =-8(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡0) \\\\  & =-8(0-1) \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237920 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237746 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237920 1069 "q-102616" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237920 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237920 1071 "The velocity $v$ (in $\\text{m/s}$) of a particle at time $t$ (in seconds) is given by $v(t)=(t\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+4t).$ Calculate the velocity of the particle when the acceleration $a$ is equal to $6\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237920 1072 #uuid "1beea8ac-55df-5e67-b8e6-69ae2990df1e" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237920 1074 "We differentiate $v(t)$ to calculate $a(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+4t) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+1+4 \\\\  & =5+\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the time when the acceleration is $a=6,$ we substitute this into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6 & =5+\\operatorname{ln}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡t & =1 \\\\ {e}^{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)} & ={e}^{1} \\\\ t & =e\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, to calculate the velocity at time $t=e,$ we substitute this into the equation for the velocity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(e) & =(e\\operatorname{ln}⁡e+4e) \\\\  & =e(1)+4e \\\\  & =5e\\text{ m/s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity when $a=6\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}$ is $5e\\text{ m/s}.$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237921 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237462 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237921 1069 "q-102633" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237921 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237921 1071 "Find the values of $\\theta $ at which the curve $x=5\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi $ intersects the line $x-5y=0.$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237921 1072 #uuid "ab8e7224-2925-52f2-b6fc-d5c7f1d40ef8" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237921 1074 "To find the coordinates of the points of intersection, we take the equation of the line, replace $x$ and $y$ with the parametric formulas, and then solve for $\\theta .$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-5y & =0 \\\\ 5\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ 5\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta } \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe principal value of $\\theta $ is\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3})=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nThis value lies inside the domain $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ so our first solution is ${\\theta }_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\nTo get a second solution, we add $\\pi $ to ${\\theta }_{1}:$\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}+\\pi =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our solutions are $\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\,\\frac{4\\pi }{3}.$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237922 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237633 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237922 1069 "q-102664" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237922 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237922 1071 "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically by\n\n$$\nx=1-2t,\\,\\,\\,y=(t-2)^{2}(t+1),\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237922 1072 #uuid "440dd157-2982-5c1f-8661-23a8368ce029" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237922 1074 "To find the Cartesian equation, we eliminate $t.$ First, we use the $x$ equation to solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=1-2t\\,⇒\\,t=\\frac{1-x}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $t=\\frac{1-x}{2}$ into our expression for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={(\\frac{1-x}{2}-2)}^{2}(\\frac{1-x}{2}+1) \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1-x-4}{2})}^{2}(\\frac{1-x+2}{2}) \\\\  & ={(\\frac{-x-3}{2})}^{2}(\\frac{3-x}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}(x+3)^{2}(x-3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=-\\frac{1}{8}(x+3)^{2}(x-3).$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237923 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237663 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237923 1069 "q-102666" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237923 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237923 1071 "Consider the matrices\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3} & -\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $AB=BA={I}_{2}$\n- $AC=CA={I}_{2}$\n- $C={A}^{-1}$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237923 1072 #uuid "efd9ac2b-7a32-57f6-9899-3da1aeffaa13" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237923 1074 "- First, we check whether $AB=BA={I}_{2}.$ Computing $AB$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3} & -\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={I}_{2}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLikewise, computing $BA$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BA & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3} & -\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={I}_{2}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, statement I is true.\n\n- Now, we check whether $AC=CA={I}_{2}.$ Computing $AC$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AC & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & ∗ \\\\ ∗ & ∗\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne {I}_{2}.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, statement II is false. As a result, $C\\ne {A}^{-1}$ and statement III is false too.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237924 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237674 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237924 1069 "q-102668" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237924 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237924 1071 "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically by\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{t-5}{2},\\,\\,\\,y=({t}^{2}-25)(t-4),\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237924 1072 #uuid "44f36d19-593e-54ad-8307-e8844e507156" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237924 1074 "To find the Cartesian equation, we eliminate $t.$ First, we use the $x$ equation to solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{t-5}{2}\\,⇒\\,t=2x+5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $t=2x+5$ into our expression for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =((2x+5)^{2}-25)(2x+5-4) \\\\  & =(4{x}^{2}+20x+25-25)(2x+1) \\\\  & =(4{x}^{2}+20x)(2x+1) \\\\  & =4x(x+5)(2x+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is $y=4x(x+5)(2x+1).$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237925 1068 #edb/ref 17592186237684 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237925 1069 "q-102669" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237925 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237925 1071 "Consider the matrices\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $AB=BA={I}_{2}$\n- $AC=CA={I}_{2}$\n- $B={A}^{-1}$" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237925 1072 #uuid "c35a4d59-f293-5742-bd9e-003fa5d50c23" 13194139533391 17592186045585 true] [17592186237925 1074 "- First, we check whether $AB=BA={I}_{2}.$ Computing $AB$, we get\n\n$$\n\